{"hadm_id": "12002987-DS-14", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> CARDIOTHORACIC <ALLERGIES> No Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> chest pain <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> ___: PROCEDURE: Coronary artery bypass grafting x3 with the left internal mammary artery to the left anterior descending artery, and reverse saphenous vein graft to the obtuse marginal artery and the distal circumflex artery. <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> : ___ year old male with hx of HTN, DMII, former smoker presents to ___ with chest pain. Cardiac cath shows multivessel coronary disease. He was transferred to ___ for evaluation of coronary artery revascularization. He received Plavix load. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> Cornary Artery Disease Hypertension Diabetes Mellitus type II Hypothyroidism <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Family History(+), Brother (+)MI,s/p CABG ___ Race: Hispanic <PHYSICAL EXAM> Admission Physical Exam Pulse: 71 B/P ___ Resp: 20 O2 sat: 99%RA Height: 5'6\" Weight: 208 LB General: A&O, NAD Skin: Dry [x] intact [x] HEENT: PERRLA [] EOMI [x] Neck: Supple [x] Full ROM [] Chest: Lungs clear bilaterally [x] Heart: RRR [x] Irregular [] Murmur [] grade ______ Abdomen: Soft [x] non-distended [x] non-tender [x] +BS [x] Extremities: Warm [x], well-perfused [x] Edema []none Varicosities: None [x] Neuro: Grossly intact [x] ___ 1+(B) Femoral Right: Left: DP Right: Left: ___ Radial Right: Left: Carotid Bruit -none Discharge Exam: VS: T 98.8 HR: 88 SR BP: 139/86 RR: 20 Sats: 95 RA Wt: 98 Kg, preopp 95 Kg General; ___ year-old male in no apparent distress HEENT: normocephalic, mucus membranes moist Neck: supple no lymphadenopathy Card: RRR normal S1,S2 no murmur Resp: clear breath sounds throughout GI: obese Extr: warm 1+ left lower extremity edema Wound: sternal and left lower extremity sites clean dry intact, no erythema no sternal click Neuro: awake, alert oriented VS: T HR BP RR O2sat WT Preop Wt 94.6kg <PERTINENT RESULTS> Admission Labs: ___ 01: 32AM BLOOD WBC-10.7 RBC-4.88 Hgb-14.0 Hct-39.7* MCV-81* MCH-28.7 MCHC-35.2* RDW-13.8 Plt ___ ___ 01: 32AM BLOOD ___ PTT-51.2* ___ ___ 01: 32AM BLOOD Glucose-189* UreaN-15 Creat-1.1 Na-136 K-4.3 Cl-101 HCO3-25 AnGap-14 ___ 01: 32AM BLOOD ALT-26 AST-17 LD(LDH)-136 AlkPhos-79 Amylase-31 TotBili-0.4 ___ 01: 32AM BLOOD Lipase-22 ___ 01: 32AM BLOOD Albumin-4.2 Mg-2.3 ___ 01: 32AM BLOOD % HbA1c-10.1* eAG-243* Discharge Labs: ___ WBC-11.7* RBC-3.22*# Hgb-9.3*# Hct-27.8* MCV-86 MCH-28.9 MCHC-33.5 RDW-14.9 RDWSD-47.3* Plt ___ ___ UreaN-14 Creat-0.9 Na-138 K-3.8 Cl-105 HCO3-25 ___ Mg-2.2 ___ ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY REPORT Echocardiographic Measurements Results Measurements Normal Range Left Atrium - Long Axis Dimension: 3.4 cm <= 4.0 cm Left Atrium - Four Chamber Length: 4.2 cm <= 5.2 cm Right Atrium - Four Chamber Length: 4.7 cm <= 5.0 cm Left Ventricle - Septal Wall Thickness: *1.3 cm 0.6 - 1.1 cm Left Ventricle - Inferolateral Thickness: *1.3 cm 0.6 - 1.1 cm Left Ventricle - Diastolic Dimension: 4.7 cm <= 5.6 cm Left Ventricle - Systolic Dimension: 3.0 cm Left Ventricle - Fractional Shortening: 0.36 >= 0.29 Left Ventricle - Ejection Fraction: >= 55% >= 55% Left Ventricle - Lateral Peak E': *0.07 m/s > 0.08 m/s Left Ventricle - Septal Peak E': *0.06 m/s > 0.08 m/s Left Ventricle - Ratio E/E': 12 < 13 Right Ventricle - Diastolic Diameter: *4.4 cm <= 4.2 cm Aorta - Sinus Level: 3.2 cm <= 3.6 cm Aorta - Ascending: 3.0 cm <= 3.4 cm Aorta - Arch: 2.7 cm <= 3.0 cm Aortic Valve - Peak Velocity: 1.2 m/sec <= 2.0 m/sec Aortic Valve - LVOT diam: 1.9 cm Mitral Valve - E Wave: 0.8 m/sec Mitral Valve - A Wave: 0.9 m/sec Mitral Valve - E/A ratio: 0.89 Mitral Valve - E Wave deceleration time: 223 ms 140-250 ms TR Gradient (+ RA = PASP): 25 mm Hg <= 25 mm Hg Findings LEFT ATRIUM: Normal LA and RA cavity sizes. RIGHT ATRIUM/INTERATRIAL SEPTUM: No ASD by 2D or color Doppler. LEFT VENTRICLE: Mild symmetric LVH with normal cavity size and regional/global systolic function (LVEF>55%). No resting LVOT gradient. RIGHT VENTRICLE: Mildly dilated RV cavity. Normal RV systolic function. AORTA: Normal diameter of aorta at the sinus, ascending and arch levels. Focal calcifications in aortic root. No 2D or Doppler evidence of distal arch coarctation. AORTIC VALVE: Normal aortic valve leaflets (3). No AS. No AR. MITRAL VALVE: Mildly thickened mitral valve leaflets. No MVP. Mild thickening of mitral valve chordae. Mild (1+) MR. ___ VALVE: Normal tricuspid valve leaflets. Mild [1+] TR. Normal PA systolic pressure. PULMONIC VALVE/PULMONARY ARTERY: Normal pulmonic valve leaflet. No PS. Physiologic PR. PERICARDIUM: No pericardial effusion. Conclusions The left atrium and right atrium are normal in cavity size. No atrial septal defect is seen by 2D or color Doppler. There is mild symmetric left ventricular hypertrophy with normal cavity size and regional/global systolic function (LVEF>55%). The right ventricular cavity is mildly dilated with normal free wall contractility. The diameters of aorta at the sinus, ascending and arch levels are normal. The aortic valve leaflets (3) appear structurally normal with good leaflet excursion and no aortic stenosis or aortic regurgitation. The mitral valve leaflets are mildly thickened. There is no mitral valve prolapse. Mild (1+) mitral regurgitation is seen. The estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure is normal. There is no pericardial effusion. IMPRESSION: Mild symmetric left ventricular hypertrophy with normal cavity size and systolic function. Minimally dilated right ventricle with normal systolic function. Mild mitral regurgitation. Chest Film: ___ In comparison with the study ___, the monitoring and support devices have been removed except for the right IJ catheter. With the left chest tube removed, there appears to be an area of the upper left zone without pulmonary markings. This could represent a small to moderate apical pneumothorax. What appears to be a pleural line at the lower portion of this area could merely reflect the cartilaginous calcification in the superior aspect of the first rib on the left. Opacification at the left base is consistent with atelectasis and small effusion. <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> Lipitor 10 mg daily Levothyroxine 150mcg daily Metformin 500mg BID Lisinopril 10mg daily Metoprolol 50mg BID Ntg SL 0.3 prn ASA 81mg daily omeprazole 20mg daily <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Aspirin EC 81 mg PO DAILY 2. Atorvastatin 80 mg PO QPM 3. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID 4. Furosemide 40 mg PO DAILY Duration: 7 Days RX *furosemide 40 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth once a day Disp #*7 Tablet Refills: *0 5. HYDROmorphone (Dilaudid) 2 mg PO Q4H: PRN pain RX *hydromorphone 2 mg ___ tablet(s) by mouth every four (4) hours Disp #*60 Tablet Refills: *0 6. Levothyroxine Sodium 150 mcg PO DAILY 7. Omeprazole 20 mg PO DAILY 8. MetFORMIN (Glucophage) 500 mg PO BID 9. Metoprolol Tartrate 25 mg PO BID 10. Potassium Chloride 10 mEq PO DAILY Duration: 7 Days take with furosemide RX *potassium chloride [Klor-Con 10] 10 mEq 1 tablet(s) by mouth once a day Disp #*7 Tablet Refills: *0 <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home With Service Facility: ___ <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Primary: Coronary Artery Disease s/p Coranaary Artery Bypass Grafting x3(LIMA-LAD,SVG-LCx,SVG-OM) Secondary: Hypertension Diabetes mellitus type II hypothyroidism <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Alert and oriented x3 nonfocal Ambulating with steady gait Incisional pain managed with Incisions: Sternal - healing well, no erythema or drainage Leg Right/Left - healing well, no erythema or drainage. Edema <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Please shower daily including washing incisions gently with mild soap, no baths or swimming until cleared by surgeon. Look at your incisions daily for redness or drainage Please NO lotions, cream, powder, or ointments to incisions Each morning you should weigh yourself and then in the evening take your temperature, these should be written down on the chart No driving for one month or while taking narcotics. Driving will be discussed at follow up appointment with surgeon-when you will be able to drive No lifting more than 10 pounds for 10 weeks **Please call cardiac surgery office with any questions or concerns ___. Answering service will contact on call person during off hours** ", "bhc": "The patient was transferred from outside hospital for revascularization after cardiac catheterization revealed multivessel disease. He had been loaded with Plavix for the catheterization and was started on a heparin infusion for unstable angina during his plavix washout. On ___ he was brought to the operating room by Dr ___ bypass grafting. Please see the operative report for details, in summary he had: Coronary artery bypass grafting x3 with the left internal mammary artery to the left anterior descending artery, and reverse saphenous vein graft to the obtuse marginal artery and the distal circumflex artery. His bypass time was 80 minutes with a crossclamp time of 66 minutes. He tolerated the operation well and after surgery was transferred to the cardiac surgery ICU in stable condition. He had a fair amout of bloody drainage on arrival to the ICU and given his pPlavix dosing and normal coagulation studies received platelets with resolution of the bleeding. Shortly after the bleeding was stopped he woke neurologically intact and was extubated. On day one he was hemodynamically stable and breathing comfortably. He was started on BBlockers and diuresed toward his pre operative weight. His chest tubes remained in until POD 3, at that time his epicardial wires were removed also. He remained in the ICU until POD 3 because there were no stepdown beds available. Once on the stepdown floor he worked with nursing and physical therapy to increase his strength and endurance. The remainder of his post-op course was uneventful. He was discharged home with ___ on POD4. He will follow-up as an outpatient. "}
{"hadm_id": "14331310-DS-8", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> Betadine / bacitracin <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Scheduled admission for chemotherapy <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> None <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> Mr ___ is a ___ yr old male with hx of poorly differentiated high grade rectal sarcoma causing bowel obstruction and prostatic obstruction s/p diverting loop ileostomy, bilateral ureteral stents and chronic foley placement. He completed C1 infusional doxorubicin and adjuvant radiation last week and presents for cycle 2. He hadnt eaten much since prior discharge and had fall in parking lot prior to arriving on floor. Was orthostatic on arrival but afebrile. Denied any syncope or lightheadedness. Did not hit head or neck,only sequelae was some dirt on his L leg. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> Past Medical History (recently diagnosed, has not seen a physician ___ ___ years): obstructive uropathy, bilateral hydronephrosis, ruptured calyx, enlarged prostate, rectal sarcoma Past Surgical History: diverting loop colostomy ___ <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> brother is diabetic and has CHD, son has testicular cancer <PHYSICAL EXAM> Gen: alert, NAD HEENT: MMM, no OP lesions Neck: supple, no JVD Lymph: no cervical, supraclavicular, axillary or inguinal adenopathy CV: RR, NL S1S2 no S3S4 or MRG PULM: CTAB ABD: BS+, soft, NTND, no masses or hepatosplenomegaly, ostomy with formed brown stool EXT: warm well perfused, no edema SKIN: No rashes or skin breakdown GU: foley with clear yellow urine NEURO: alert and oriented x 4, ___, EOMI, no nystagmus, face symmetric, no tongue deviation, full hand grip, shoulder shrug and bicep flexion, full toe dorsiflexion and hip flexion against resistance bilateral, sensation intact to light touch, no clonus gait normal <PERTINENT RESULTS> ___ 10: 30PM URINE BLOOD-MOD NITRITE-POS PROTEIN-30 GLUCOSE-TR KETONE-NEG BILIRUBIN-NEG UROBILNGN-NEG PH-6.5 LEUK-LG ___: wbc 14, hgb 9, plt 572 lytes and cr wnl, BUN 26 LFTs wnl URINE CULTURE (Final ___: STAPH AUREUS COAG +. >100,000 ORGANISMS/ML.. Oxacillin RESISTANT Staphylococci MUST be reported as also RESISTANT to other penicillins, cephalosporins, carbacephems, carbapenems, and beta-lactamase inhibitor combinations. <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Fentanyl Patch 50 mcg/h TD Q72H 2. Finasteride 5 mg PO DAILY 3. OxycoDONE (Immediate Release) ___ mg PO Q4H: PRN pain 4. Tamsulosin 0.4 mg PO DAILY 5. TraZODone 100 mg PO HS: PRN sleep 6. Acetaminophen 325-650 mg PO Q6H: PRN pain <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Acetaminophen 325-650 mg PO Q6H: PRN pain RX *acetaminophen 325 mg ___ tablet(s) by mouth every 6 hours as needed Disp #*30 Tablet Refills: *2 2. Fentanyl Patch 50 mcg/h TD Q72H RX *fentanyl 50 mcg/hour apply to skin replace patch every 3 days Disp #*1 Patch Refills: *0 3. Finasteride 5 mg PO DAILY 4. OxycoDONE (Immediate Release) ___ mg PO Q4H: PRN pain 5. Tamsulosin 0.4 mg PO DAILY 6. TraZODone 100 mg PO HS: PRN sleep 7. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO DAILY you may not need this once chemotherpy is completed. Stop if output is loose/watery RX *docusate sodium 100 mg 1 capsule(s) by mouth daily Disp #*30 Capsule Refills: *2 8. Senna 8.6 mg PO DAILY you may not need this once chemotherapy is completed RX *sennosides [senna] 8.6 mg 1 capsule by mouth daily Disp #*30 Capsule Refills: *2 9. Sulfameth/Trimethoprim DS 1 TAB PO BID Duration: 7 Days RX *sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim 800 mg-160 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth twice a day Disp #*2 Tablet Refills: *0 <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home With Service Facility: ___ <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Rectal sarcoma Urinary tract infection Orthostasis <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Stable Mental status - Alert and coherent Ambulatory status - Independent <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Mr ___ it was a pleasure caring for you during your stay here at ___. You were admitted for chemotherapy and radiation which you tolerated well. You were also found to have a urinary tract infection. You will continue on antibiotics for a total of 10 days and your foley catheter was changed. ", "bhc": "# Poorly diffentiated high-grade sarcoma - Completed C2 of infusional doxorubicin (4 doses over 24 hrs each with daily dexamethasone and zofran) and ongoing radiation, ___ fractions thus far). Tolerated well # Chronic pain - continued fentanyl 50mcg/hr patch with oxycodone ___ q4h for breakthrough. pain well controlled. # Orthostasis - likely ___ decreased PO intake, BP improved following NS bolus. No signs sepsis but was found to have UTI. Pt also deconditioned as he avoids sitting due to pain. Will encourage OOB and request ___ consult # Leukocytosis/Thrombocytosis - somewhat impoved, likely inflammatory response to tumor and steroid response. surveillance blood cx NGTD. # Hydronephrosis: s/p ureteral stents. creatinine stable #Obstructive uropathy - ___ tumor mass, has chronic foley in place. Discussed with urology, was changed by nursing day of discharge without difficulty. Will need voiding trial once chemotherapy complete, pt will need to f/u to BI Urology. # Constipation - started on prn miralax with good results, continue stool softeners. # MRSA UTI, Complicated - >100K MRSA from foley culture. Initially treated with ceftriaxone but swithced to bactrim based on susceptibilities. Foley changed as above. Will complete course next week "}
{"hadm_id": "10666345-DS-15", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> tramadol <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Pneumothorax <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> Right lower lobe core needle biopsy and fiduciary placement <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> Ms ___ is a ___ woman with history of hypertension and bilateral non small cell lung cancer s/p multiple RFA sessions and systemic chemo/radiation who presents from the radiology unit for overnight monitoring after a high-risk lung biopsy complicated by a small pneumothorax. For a full detailed history of the patient's lung cancer, please see ___ note from ___. In brief, most recently in ___, the patient had a CT chest which showed a 1.3 x 1.0 cm right lower lobe lung nodule, increased from 1.0 x 0.4 cm, thought to be most likely bronchogenic carcinoma. On ___ the patient had a CT guided RFA of this lesion. The procedure was complicated by a small amount of hemorrhage, and a small pneumothorax, so a biopsy of the lesion was not obtained. Ms. ___ was admitted to ___ from ___ to ___ with respiratory failure after the RFA. She was treated with Levaquin for pneumonia. Her oxygenation improved and she was discharged to ___ in ___, where she stayed for two weeks. A follow up CT with contrast on ___ showed a cavitary nodule in the medial RLL significantly increased to 3.4 x 3.7 cm compared with 2.3 cm with central low density suggesting necrosis. The nodule abutted the esophagus and posterior pericardium without definite evidence of invasion. Given the location of the patient's lesion, it was not felt to be amenable to a wedge resection, but would rather require a basal segmentectomy. However, it was also thought to be amenable to CyberKnife SBRT which would require biopsy and fiducial marker placement. On ___, she underwent outpatient biopsy & fiducial placement of RLL lung mass. 4 core biopsy samples were sent for pathology, and 10cc of purulent fluid were sent for microbiology. A CT showed a small paramediastinal pneumothorax post-procedure, with follow up CXRs at 2 and 4 hours post procedure showing resolution of the PTX. Clinically, she has been stable on room air. Upon arrival to the floor, the patient reports that her breathing is at baseline with no shortness of breath. She states that the procedure was quite painful, but that she no longer has any pain. She has no chest pain, shortness of breath, palpitations or other complaints, and is hopeful that she will be able to go home early tomorrow. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> PAST MEDICAL HISTORY: - Age-related macular degeneration - GERD - Hyperlipidemia - Prior removal of a vocal cord polyp - Hypertension - Lung cancer - Squamous cell cancer on the face - Left bundle branch block PAST SURGICAL HISTORY: - s/p squamous cell cancer removal from face - s/p vocal cord polyp removal <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> son died of met CA, daughter healthy Physical ___: ADMISSION EXAM: -== VITALS: 97.3PO148 / 79 HR: 70RR: 20O2: 96RA GENERAL: Alert and interactive. In no acute distress. Lying comfortably in bed, no eye contact (legally blind) HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic. NECK: No cervical lymphadenopathy. No JVD. CARDIAC: Regular rhythm, normal rate. ___ systolic late peaking crescendo-decrescendo murmur radiating to carotids. Carotid upstroke was brisk and not delayed. LUNGS: Clear to auscultation bilaterally w/appropriate breath sounds appreciated in all fields. No wheezes, rhonchi or rales. No increased work of breathing. BACK: Dressing in mid right back over site of biopsy was clean dry and intact. ABDOMEN: Normal bowels sounds, non distended, non-tender to deep palpation in all four quadrants. No organomegaly. EXTREMITIES: No clubbing, cyanosis, or edema. Pulses DP/Radial 2+ bilaterally. Tenderness to palpation of bilateral lower extremities which the patient states is normal and at her baseline. NEUROLOGIC: CN2-12 intact. ___ strength throughout. Normal sensation. AOx3. Discharge Exam: VITALS: 97.___ HR: 70RR: 20O2: 96RA GENERAL: Alert and interactive. In no acute distress. Lying comfortably in bed, no eye contact (legally blind) HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic. NECK: No cervical lymphadenopathy. No JVD. CARDIAC: Regular rhythm, normal rate. ___ systolic late peaking crescendo-decrescendo murmur radiating to carotids. Carotid upstroke was brisk and not delayed. LUNGS: Clear to auscultation bilaterally w/appropriate breath sounds appreciated in all fields. No wheezes, rhonchi or rales. No increased work of breathing. BACK: Dressing in mid right back over site of biopsy was clean dry and intact. ABDOMEN: Normal bowels sounds, non distended, non-tender to deep palpation in all four quadrants. No organomegaly. EXTREMITIES: No clubbing, cyanosis, or edema. Pulses DP/Radial 2+ bilaterally. Tenderness to palpation of bilateral lower extremities which the patient states is normal and at her baseline. NEUROLOGIC: CN2-12 intact. ___ strength throughout. Normal sensation. AOx3. Discharge Exam: VITALS: Per OMR GENERAL: Alert and interactive. In no acute distress. Lying comfortably in bed, no eye contact (legally blind) HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic. NECK: No cervical lymphadenopathy. No JVD. CARDIAC: Regular rhythm, normal rate. ___ systolic late peaking crescendo-decrescendo murmur radiating to carotids. Carotid upstroke was brisk and not delayed. LUNGS: Clear to auscultation bilaterally w/appropriate breath sounds appreciated in all fields. No wheezes, rhonchi or rales. No increased work of breathing. BACK: Dressing in mid right back over site of biopsy was clean dry and intact. ABDOMEN: Normal bowels sounds, non distended, non-tender to deep palpation in all four quadrants. No organomegaly. EXTREMITIES: No clubbing, cyanosis, or edema. Pulses DP/Radial 2+ bilaterally. Tenderness to palpation of bilateral lower extremities which the patient states is normal and at her baseline. NEUROLOGIC: CN2-12 intact. ___ strength throughout. Normal sensation. AOx3. <PERTINENT RESULTS> ADMISSION LABS: -== ___ 09: 35PM BLOOD WBC-8.9 RBC-3.12* Hgb-9.3* Hct-27.8* MCV-89 MCH-29.8 MCHC-33.5 RDW-13.8 RDWSD-44.9 Plt ___ ___ 09: 35PM BLOOD Glucose-92 UreaN-23* Creat-1.1 Na-141 K-4.0 Cl-104 HCO3-25 AnGap-12 PERTINENT STUDIES: -= CXR ___: No pneumothorax appreciated. No significant change compared to the prior study. DISCHARGE LABS: -== ___ 05: 05AM BLOOD WBC-8.3 RBC-3.25* Hgb-9.5* Hct-29.0* MCV-89 MCH-29.2 MCHC-32.8 RDW-13.4 RDWSD-43.7 Plt ___ ___ 05: 05AM BLOOD Glucose-97 UreaN-20 Creat-1.0 Na-142 K-4.3 Cl-106 HCO3-24 AnGap-12 <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Dear Ms. ___, WHY WERE YOU ADMITTED TO THE HOSPITAL? - You had a biopsy of your right lower lung - After the biopsy you had a small collapse of a part of your lung WHAT HAPPENED WHILE I WAS HERE? - You had repeat imaging of your chest - This showed that the small area of collapse had improved - Your breathing remained stable while you were here and you were discharged home WHAT SHOULD I DO WHEN I LEAVE? - Please take all of your medications as prescribed - Please go to all of your follow up appointments as arranged for you It was a pleasure to care for you during your hospital stay. Your ___ Medicine Team ", "bhc": "Ms ___ is a ___ woman with history of hypertension and bilateral non small cell lung cancer s/p multiple RFA sessions and systemic chemo/radiation who presents from the radiology unit for overnight monitoring after a high-risk lung biopsy complicated by a small pneumothorax which resolved 2 hours post procedurally. #Pneumothorax: The patient had a RLL lung biopsy and fiduciary placement with CT showing evidence of a small trace paramediastinal pneumothorax post procedrually. Follow-up chest radiographs at 2 and 4 hours post-procedure demonstrated no residual pneumonthorax. The patient was hemodynamically stable on admission, and satting well on room air. She was monitored overnight in the hospital and remained at her baseline with no shortness of breath or desaturations. She had one follow up chest X-ray on the morning of discharge which showed no evidence of pneumothorax. The patient was able to ambulate well prior to discharge. #Non-small cell lung CA: The patient is followed by ___, ___ radiation oncology, and is s/p multiple RFAs, most recently with RLL lesion increasing in size with some signs of central necrosis though to be amenable to CyberKnife SBRT following biopsy and fiducial marker placement. The patient was discharged with a follow up appointments with her outpatient radiation oncologist. STABLE/CHRONIC: #HLD: The patient was continued on home atorvastatin #HTN: Continued on losartan #GERD: Continued on home pantoprazole, ranitidine and TUMS TRANSITIONAL ISSUES: ==================== [] Pending studies: Please follow up core needle biopsies and fluid cultures # CODE STATUS: Full (confirmed) # CONTACT: ___ (Friend) HCP ___ ___ on Admission: The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Atorvastatin 20 mg PO QPM 2. calcium carbonate 400 mg oral QID:PRN 3. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID 4. Losartan Potassium 50 mg PO DAILY 5. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 6. Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q24H 7. Senna 8.6 mg PO DAILY 8. Vitamin D 1000 UNIT PO DAILY 9. aspirin-caffeine 400-32 mg oral DAILY:PRN 10. Bifidobacterium infantis 4 mg oral DAILY 11. DiphenhydrAMINE 25 mg PO DAILY:PRN itching 12. Ibuprofen 100 mg PO Q8H:PRN Pain - Moderate 13. raloxifene 60 mg oral DAILY 14. Ranitidine 150 mg PO DAILY Discharge Medications: 1. aspirin-caffeine 400-32 mg oral DAILY:PRN 2. Atorvastatin 20 mg PO QPM 3. Bifidobacterium infantis 4 mg oral DAILY 4. calcium carbonate 400 mg oral QID:PRN 5. DiphenhydrAMINE 25 mg PO DAILY:PRN itching 6. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID 7. Ibuprofen 100 mg PO Q8H:PRN Pain - Moderate 8. Losartan Potassium 50 mg PO DAILY 9. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 10. Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q24H 11. raloxifene 60 mg oral DAILY 12. Ranitidine 150 mg PO DAILY 13. Senna 8.6 mg PO DAILY 14. Vitamin D 1000 UNIT PO DAILY Discharge Disposition: Home Discharge Diagnosis: Primary diagnosis: - Pneumothorax Secondary diagnosis: - Non-small cell lung cancer Discharge Condition: Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. Followup Instructions: ___"}
{"hadm_id": "15032543-DS-5", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> No Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions <ATTENDING> ___ <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Chest pain <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> Left Heart Catheterization ___ CT-Guided Pleural Mass Biopsy ___ <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> ___ with h/o HTN and migraines presents with chest pain. She states that she was at home cleaning her mediation pillows before going to yoga class when she had ___ sharp/crushing substernal and left sided chest pain. Mild radiation into upper back and possibly slightly down left arm. Denies n/v/d, sob, diaphoresis. Called ___ because she was afraid she was having a heart attack. EMS arrived and gave her ASA 325, nitro SL which helped decrease her pain to ___ but gave her a headache. History of HTN but no other cardiac disease, this has never happened before and she is normally active taking walks and doing yoga with healthy diet. Family cardiac history significant only for early heart disease in paternal uncle. In the ED, initial VS were: 98.2, 90, 130/77, 16, 96% RA Exam unremarkable Labs showed: trop 0.4 then 0.29. CXR: Triangular opacity within the posterior aspect of the right medial lung for which further assessment with contrast-enhanced chest CT is recommended. Received ... ___ 19: 06 IV Metoclopramide 10 mg ___ ___ 19: 21 IV DiphenhydrAMINE 25 mg ___ ___ 22: 04 IV Heparin 3600 UNIT ___ ___ 22: 04 IV Heparin ___ Transfer VS were: 98.0, 62, 143/83, 16, 100% RA Decision was made to admit to medicine for further management. On arrival to the floor, patient reports that she is chest pain free and has no complaints except that she is anxious about the heparin because her husband had a intracranial bleed after receiving heparin in this hospital during cardiac cath (he had a h/o vascular disease and CABG x4). <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> HTN exercise induced asthma migraines seasonal allergies glaucoma s/p iridotomy <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Mother passed at ___. ___. Father: ___ at ___ of pancreatic CA Paternal uncle: heart attack age ___ Brothers healthy Physical ___: ADMISSION PHYSICAL EXAM: VS - 97.7, 147/93, 68, 16, 100% RA GENERAL: well appearing woman younger than stated age in NAD HEENT: AT/NC, EOMI, PERRL, anicteric sclera, pink conjunctiva, MMM, good dentition CARDIAC: RRR, S1/S2, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs LUNG: CTAB, no wheezes, rales, rhonchi, breathing comfortably without use of accessory muscles ABDOMEN: nondistended, +BS, nontender in all quadrants, no rebound/guarding, no hepatosplenomegaly EXTREMITIES: WWP, no edema, pulses +2 in UE and ___ bilaterally NEURO: CN II-XII grossly intact SKIN: no rashes or concerning lesions DISCHARGE PHYSICAL EXAM Vitals: afeb ___ ___ GENERAL: well appearing woman younger than stated age NECK: JVP 7cm CARDIAC: irregularly irregular, no m/r/g LUNG: non-labored, CTAB ABDOMEN: soft, NDNT EXTREMITIES: warm, no c/c/e NEURO: normal mental status, CN ___ intact, no ataxia SKIN: no diaphoresis, rashes or lesions <PERTINENT RESULTS> ADMISSION LABS - ___ 06: 09PM BLOOD WBC-7.3 RBC-4.35 Hgb-12.6 Hct-39.0 MCV-90 MCH-29.0 MCHC-32.3 RDW-13.2 RDWSD-43.7 Plt ___ ___: 57PM BLOOD ___ PTT-45.9* ___ ___ 06: 09PM BLOOD Glucose-92 UreaN-19 Creat-0.7 Na-137 K-4.5 Cl-102 HCO3-24 AnGap-16 ___ 06: 09PM BLOOD cTropnT-0.04* ___ 06: 09PM BLOOD CK-MB-4 INTERVAL LABS - Cardiac Markers: ___ 08: 57PM BLOOD cTropnT-0.29* ___ 07: 20AM BLOOD CK-MB-19* cTropnT-0.54* ___ 09: 47PM BLOOD CK-MB-9 cTropnT-0.21* ___ 07: 25AM BLOOD CRP-1.4 ___ 07: 20AM BLOOD TSH-3.8 ___ 07: 20AM BLOOD CA125-9 DISCHARGE LABS - ___ 08: 00AM BLOOD WBC-6.5 RBC-4.68 Hgb-13.7 Hct-42.2 MCV-90 MCH-29.3 MCHC-32.5 RDW-13.5 RDWSD-44.3 Plt ___ ___ 08: 00AM BLOOD Glucose-112* UreaN-14 Creat-0.7 Na-139 K-4.6 Cl-103 HCO3-26 AnGap-15 ___ 08: 00AM BLOOD Calcium-9.0 Phos-3.9 Mg-2.0 IMAGING & STUDIES - ECGs: ___: Normal sinus rhythm with PACs, normal axis and morphologies, no ST-T abnormalities ___: Atrial fibrillation @ 90, STD in V4-V5, TWI in V4-V6 Left Heart Cath ___ Impression: No angiographically apparent coronary artery disease TTE ___ Conclusions The left atrium is normal in size. No atrial septal defect is seen by 2D or color Doppler. The estimated right atrial pressure is ___ mmHg. Normal left ventricular wall thickness, cavity size, and regional/global systolic function (biplane LVEF = 65 %). There is no ventricular septal defect. Right ventricular chamber size and free wall motion are normal. The diameters of aorta at the sinus, ascending and arch levels are normal. The aortic valve leaflets (3) are mildly thickened. There is no aortic valve stenosis. Mild to moderate (___) aortic regurgitation is seen. The mitral valve leaflets are mildly thickened. Moderate (2+) mitral regurgitation is seen. The estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure is normal. There is no pericardial effusion. IMPRESSION: Normal left ventricular wall thickness with normal biventricular cavity size and regional/global systolic function. Mild to moderate tricuspid regurgitation. Moderate mitral regurgitation. Mild-moderate aortic regurgitation. ___ CHEST (PA & LAT) FINDINGS: Heart size is normal. The aorta is mildly unfolded. The hilar contours are normal. The pulmonary vasculature is normal. On the lateral view, a well- delineated triangular opacity is noted posteriorly along the right medial lung base, partially obscuring the right posterior hemidiaphragm. Lungs are otherwise clear. No pleural effusion or pneumothorax is seen. There are no acute osseous abnormalities. IMPRESSION: Triangular opacity within the posterior aspect of the right medial lung for which further assessment with contrast-enhanced chest CT is recommended. ___ CTA CHEST IMPRESSION: 1. Right lower lobe pleural-based mass measuring up to 6.4 cm. Differential considerations include primary neoplasm, or less likely an infectious or inflammatory process. 2. No evidence of pulmonary embolism or aortic abnormality. 3. Bronchial wall thickening, consistent with small airways disease. ___ CT Abdomen/Pelvis w & w/o contrast IMPRESSION: Stable large mass lower right chest, may represent solitary fibrous tumor of the pleura. There is a 0.6 cm indeterminate lesion left kidney, with central linear thin enhancement, follow-up retroperitoneal ultrasound, or MRI in 6 months recommended. There is 3.4 cm well-circumscribed left adnexal mass, may represent pedunculated fibroid versus ovarian neoplasm. Pelvic ultrasound recommended in further evaluation. ___ PELVIS U.S., TRANSVAGINAL IMPRESSION: 1. Normal ovaries. 2. Three fibroids are identified. The larger 2 fibroids correspond with structures visualized on the prior CT. Please note that on the ultrasound the questionable adnexal structure appears on the right side and on the prior CT of ___ this structure appears on the left side. The significance of this is uncertain. If further clinical evaluation is required a repeat ultrasound could be performed or if further delineation of the fibroid is needed an MRI could be performed. <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list may be inaccurate and requires futher investigation. 1. Vitamin B Complex w/C 1 TAB PO DAILY 2. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 3. Calcium 600 with Vitamin D3 (calcium carbonate-vitamin D3) 600 mg(1,500mg) -200 unit oral DAILY 4. Albuterol Inhaler ___ PUFF IH DAILY: PRN SOB/cough 5. Propranolol LA 60 mg PO DAILY 6. Sumatriptan Succinate 25 mg PO DAILY: PRN migraine <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Apixaban 5 mg PO BID RX *apixaban [Eliquis] 5 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth twice a day Disp #*60 Tablet Refills: *0 2. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY RX *aspirin 81 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth dailu Disp #*30 Tablet Refills: *0 3. Atorvastatin 80 mg PO QPM RX *atorvastatin 80 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth qpm Disp #*30 Tablet Refills: *0 4. Lisinopril 2.5 mg PO DAILY RX *lisinopril 2.5 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth daily Disp #*30 Tablet Refills: *0 5. OxyCODONE (Immediate Release) 5 mg PO Q8H: PRN Pain - Moderate Duration: 3 Days RX *oxycodone 5 mg 1 capsule(s) by mouth q8h: prn Disp #*9 Capsule Refills: *0 6. Propranolol LA 120 mg PO DAILY RX *propranolol [InnoPran XL] 120 mg 1 capsule(s) by mouth daily Disp #*30 Capsule Refills: *0 7. Albuterol Inhaler ___ PUFF IH DAILY: PRN SOB/cough 8. Calcium 600 with Vitamin D3 (calcium carbonate-vitamin D3) 600 mg(1,500mg) -200 unit oral DAILY 9. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 10. Vitamin B Complex w/C 1 TAB PO DAILY 11. HELD- Sumatriptan Succinate 25 mg PO DAILY: PRN migraine This medication was held. Do not restart Sumatriptan Succinate until you discuss with your doctor <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> PRIMARY DIAGNOSIS -= Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction SECONDARY DIAGNOSES -=== Pleural Mass Atrial Fibrillation <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Dear Ms. ___ It was a pleasure taking care of you at the ___ ___. You were admitted because you had chest pain. You were taken to the cath lab, where luckily they found no residual heart disease. You had an echocardiogram that also showed that your heart is pumping very well. We suggest continuing to take aspirin, propranolol and atorvastatin for now for heart protection. You were also found to have a heart rhythm called \"atrial fibrillation.\" This condition is associated with a risk of strokes. Apixaban, and other blood thinners, can help decrease this risk substantially. Please continue to take your apixaban. Please follow up with your primary care doctor about the results of your pleural biopsy. They will determine from there with what specialty to follow up with for ongoing monitoring and care. It was a pleasure taking care of you at ___. We wish you well! ", "bhc": "___ healthy other than HTN admitted for chest pain and trop elevation to 0.54. Cath found clean coronaries. Course complicated by new afib (asymptomatic), large pleural mass (incidentally found). # Chest pain with troponinemia but clean coronaries: Unclear etiology. Possibly transient coronary obstruction from embolism ___ afib. Only risk factors for CAD are well-controlled HTN and distant tobacco use ___ pack-years, quit ___ years ago). No signs on TTE to suggest stress cardiomyopathy. No viral symptoms to suggest myocarditis, CRP 1.4, ESR 6. PE ruled out. TTE without wall motion abnormalities. - new medications: ASA, atorva 80, lisinopril 2.5 - propranolol increased from 60 ->120 mg daily # Atrial fibrillation: New onset. Rate controlled on increased dose of propranolol. CHAD2Vasc2=3. Etiology unclear -- no signs/symptoms of ongoing ischemia, heart failure, infection, hyperthyroidism (TSH 3.8). - propranolol increased from 60->120mg daily - apixiban started # Pleural mass: Large (6 x 4 cm). Asymptomatic, incidentally found on CXR, confirmed on CTA. Likely malignancy. CT A/P with left adnexal mass but benign appearance on ultrasound (fibroids), no adenopathy or ascites. Underwent ___ biopsy, pathology pending. # Chronic migraines: Headaches this admission are identical to patient's chronic migraines. No red flag symptoms or neuro deficits to suggest stroke or metastasis. - oxycodone prn - avoid home sumatriptan due to coronary vasospasm risk Transitional Issues ===================== # New CAD meds: aspirin, atorvastatin, lisinopril # Atrial fibrillation -Started apixiban -Increased propranolol LA from 60 to 120 mg # Incidental Radiologic Findings: ## Pleural mass: f/u biopsy results ## Kidney lesion: CT read: There is a 0.6 cm indeterminate lesion left kidney, with central linear thin enhancement, follow-up retroperitoneal ultrasound, or MRI in 6 months recommended. ## Adnexal mass: Ultrasound appearance consistent with fibroid. CA125 within normal limits. - CT read: There is 3.4 cm well-circumscribed left adnexal mass, may represent pedunculated fibroid versus ovarian neoplasm. Pelvic ultrasound recommended in further evaluation. - Ultrasound read: 1. Normal ovaries. 2. Three fibroids are identified. The larger 2 fibroids correspond with structures visualized on the prior CT. Please note that on the ultrasound the questionable adnexal structure appears on the right side and on the prior CT of ___ this structure appears on the left side. The significance of this is uncertain. If further clinical evaluation is required a repeat ultrasound could be performed or if further delineation of the fibroid is needed an MRI could be performed. "}
{"hadm_id": "12627553-DS-13", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> SURGERY <ALLERGIES> No Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> sepsis <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> none <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> ___ w/HCV and EtOH cirrhosis, s/p liver transplant ___, now presenting with fever and hypotension with pressor requirement after HD today. Mr. ___ has had a complex course since his transplant, including wash-out for intra-abdominal hemorrhage, ESRD requiring dialysis, recurrent HCV, pneumonia with right empyema, and C. diff colitis. He was most recently discharged from ___ on ___ for his c.diff infection. He was doing well at rehab, and had HD today, which went without incident. After completion, they noted that he was hypotensive to the 80's and febrile to 103, and he was brought to our ED for evaluation. His wife reports his stools are still loose, but are decreased in amount and the character is improved since completing c. diff treatment. History is obtained from the patient's wife and ___, as the patient is a very poor historian and is mostly non-verbal currently <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> recurrent hepatitis C with the suspicion of fibrosing cholestatic hepatitis s/p ERCP on ___ with biliary stent placement HCV; VL on ___ was 32.6 million HCV/ETOH cirrhosis w/ hepatorenal syndrome s/p OLT ___ Intra-abdominal hemorrhage Malnutrition VRE UTI <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Mom survived MI age ___, Father died age ___ of alcoholic cirrhosis. One brother died age ___ of ?mesothelioma. Sister had ___ ___ yrs ago. Other four sibs are alive and well. One niece survived ruptured brain aneurism in her ___. No other family h/o liver disease, heart disease, or cancer. <PHYSICAL EXAM> PE on admission: Vitals: T 99.5, HR 128, BP 89/46, RR 16, O2 100% Gen: sleepy but arousable, oriented x3; sclerae anicteric CV: tachycardic, no appreciable murmur Resp: right-sided crackles and decreased breath sounds at base, dullness to percussion right base; left lung cta Abd: soft, non-tender, moderately distended, +fluid wave; incisions well-healed; - ___ sign Extr: warm, 1+ pulses DRE: no gross blood, guaiac negative PE on discharge: - no vital signs, no resppirtaion, no pulse, no heart sounds, no pupillary reflexes <PERTINENT RESULTS> imaging: ___ Dupplex abdomen 1. Patent portal and hepatic veins and hepatic arteries with normal systolic upstroke. 2. Coarsened liver with echogenic area in the left lobe compatible with a resolving hematoma or complex fluid collection. Simple hepatic cyst also seen. 3. Moderate-to-large amount of ascites. ___ CT abdomen/pelvis 1. Bilateral pleural effusions with compressive atelectasis. 2. Dobbhoff tube curled twice, once in the stomach, once in the esophagus. 3. Moderate to extensive ascites. 4. Diffuse colitis, nonspecific in appearance but certainly could be related to patient's known C. diff infection. ___ CXR Large bilateral pleural effusions and right basal atelectasis unchanged acutely. No pneumothorax. A feeding tube and a nasogastric tube ends in the upper stomach. Right jugular line tip projects over the low SVC. Heart is not enlarged. No pneumothorax. laboratory: ___ 07: 30PM BLOOD WBC-17.0*# RBC-2.70* Hgb-9.1* Hct-30.8* MCV-114*# MCH-33.8* MCHC-29.6* RDW-18.3* Plt Ct-80* ___ 06: 36PM BLOOD WBC-34.6* RBC-2.53* Hgb-8.7* Hct-26.7* MCV-105* MCH-34.5* MCHC-32.8 RDW-19.2* Plt Ct-88* ___ 01: 52AM BLOOD WBC-13.5*# RBC-2.90* Hgb-9.6* Hct-28.2* MCV-97 MCH-33.2* MCHC-34.2 RDW-19.3* Plt Ct-68* ___ 01: 42AM BLOOD WBC-9.8 RBC-3.54* Hgb-11.4* Hct-33.0* MCV-93 MCH-32.2* MCHC-34.6 RDW-19.4* Plt Ct-53* ___ 09: 27AM BLOOD WBC-12.4* RBC-3.21* Hgb-10.5* Hct-31.7* MCV-99* MCH-32.6* MCHC-33.0 RDW-19.8* Plt Ct-33* ___ 07: 30PM BLOOD ___ PTT-48.2* ___ ___ 01: 37PM BLOOD Plt Ct-92* ___ 01: 52AM BLOOD ___ PTT->150* ___ ___ 09: 27AM BLOOD ___ PTT-88.0* ___ ___ 09: 27AM BLOOD Plt Smr-VERY LOW Plt Ct-33* ___ 07: 30PM BLOOD Glucose-553* UreaN-18 Creat-2.5*# Na-123* K-3.2* Cl-92* HCO3-18* AnGap-16 ___ 02: 16AM BLOOD Glucose-72 UreaN-22* Creat-2.9* Na-135 K-3.4 Cl-99 HCO3-15* AnGap-24___ 01: 37PM BLOOD Glucose-43* UreaN-25* Creat-3.4* Na-133 K-4.0 Cl-97 HCO3-12* AnGap-28* ___ 01: 42AM BLOOD Glucose-89 UreaN-42* Creat-4.7* Na-129* K-3.1* Cl-99 HCO3-16* AnGap-17 ___ 04: 45PM BLOOD Glucose-93 UreaN-19 Creat-2.6*# Na-136 K-3.7 Cl-102 HCO3-26 AnGap-12 ___ 01: 33AM BLOOD Glucose-133* UreaN-23* Creat-2.4* Na-137 K-3.7 Cl-104 HCO3-25 AnGap-12 ___ 09: 27AM BLOOD Glucose-145* UreaN-25* Creat-2.7* Na-138 K-4.7 Cl-104 HCO3-27 AnGap-12 ___ 07: 30PM BLOOD ALT-79* AST-172* AlkPhos-214* TotBili-5.4* ___ 02: 16AM BLOOD ALT-104* AST-239* AlkPhos-188* Amylase-73 TotBili-5.5* ___ 01: 42AM BLOOD ALT-134* AST-197* AlkPhos-162* TotBili-13.0* ___ 01: 33AM BLOOD ALT-63* AST-83* AlkPhos-181* TotBili-21.3* ___ 09: 27AM BLOOD ALT-60* AST-76* AlkPhos-181* TotBili-22.0* ___ 07: 30PM BLOOD Albumin-1.8* Calcium-6.3* Phos-1.3* Mg-1.6 ___ 03: 03AM BLOOD Albumin-2.4* Calcium-8.1* Phos-2.8 Mg-1.9 ___ 09: 27AM BLOOD Albumin-2.5* Calcium-8.3* Phos-3.9# Mg-2.3 ___ 07: 41PM BLOOD pH-7.31* ___ 02: 41AM BLOOD Type-ART pO2-118* pCO2-29* pH-7.35 calTCO2-17* Base XS--7 ___ 08: 58AM BLOOD Type-ART Temp-36.4 pO2-50* pCO2-47* pH-7.35 calTCO2-27 Base XS-0 Intubat-NOT INTUBA ___ 12: 50PM BLOOD Type-ART pO2-160* pCO2-38 pH-7.39 calTCO2-24 Base XS--1 microbiology: ___ blood culture KLEBSIELLA PNEUMONIAE. IDENTIFICATION AND SENSITIVITIES PERFORMED ON CULTURE # ___ ___. ___ blood culture LACTOBACILLUS SPECIES. Isolated from only one set in the previous five days. Anaerobic Bottle Gram Stain (Final ___: Reported to and read back by ___ @ 1810 ON ___ - CC6C. GRAM POSITIVE ROD(S). IN CHAINS. ___ Bclx - pend ___ Bclx - pend ___ Bclx - pend <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> tylenol ___ prn fever, darbepoetin alpha 40 qweek, folic acid, HSQ, lispro sliding scale, reglan 5 iv q8, mycophenolate 180\", nepro TF 45/hr, nystatin swish/swallow\"\", zofran prn, promod syrup 10ml q8, protonix 40\", bactrim', tacrolimus 0.5/0.5, ursodiol 300\", renal vitamin <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> patient was CMO: - morphine gtt <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Expired <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> multiorgan system failure cardiac arrest <DISCHARGE CONDITION> death <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> not applicable ", "bhc": "Patient was admitted to the transplant surgery service with the bacteremia and sepsis. He was admitted to the ICU. Neuro: Since the time of admission patient was arousable and responded to commands. He remained at his baseline until the day he decompensated when his metal status has worsened and upon intubation, required no sedation. He received minimal pain medications for abdominal pain/discomfort. CV: The patient was initially stable from a cardiovascular standpoint. He required vasopressors upon admission, in the next ___ hours he was weaned of the pressors. He developed vasopressor requirement and became hemodynamically unstable approximately 48 hour prior to his demise. Following the aspiration event on the floor level of care on ___, patient was transferred to ICU, where he drooped his systolic blood pressure shortly after. He was on one vasopressor for the 24 hours, later on 2 pressors, yet his sbp was in ___. The family made a decision to withdraw life support including vasopressors on ___ in the early mid-afternoon. Patient expired several hours after. Pulmonary: The patient remained stable from a pulmonary standpoint initially. On ___ he vomited and aspirated. His oxygen saturation dropped and he was transferred to the ICU. His oxygen saturation was low, his mental status worsened, thus he was intubated. He remained intubated until he expired. GI/GU/FEN: Patient was found to have C. difficile colitis. He was fed via TPN. The electrolytes was initially repleated. The hyperkalemia was treated with kayaxylate. Patient also underwent CVVH. The CVVH no longer continued after ___, when patient was intubated, due to family wishes. Patient had diagnostis paracenthesis, which did not show SBP. ID: He was treated for C. difficile colitis initially with PO vancomycin and IV flagyl as well as tigacycine. The Klebsiella bacteremia was treated with meropenem. Prior to the cultures becoming available, patient also recieved amikacin and cefepime. He was afebrile for most of the hospital stay. Endocrine: The patient's blood sugar was monitored throughout his stay; insulin dosing was adjusted accordingly. Hematology: The patient's complete blood count was examined routinely. Prophylaxis: The patient received subcutaneous heparin and venodyne boots were used during this stay. As patient status declined rapidly on ___ following the aspiration event, family made a well thought out decision to minimize the patient's suffering as his prognosis was not favorable. Patient was initially made DNR status for about 24 hours. Next, the family made the patient CMO with continued ventilation on ___. Patient expired one to hours after all the vasopressors were stopped. He was started on morphine gtt. He expired comfortable, with no agonal breaths observed. The medical examiner as well as the family refused an autopsy. "}
{"hadm_id": "17967161-DS-29", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> SURGERY <ALLERGIES> lisinopril <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Peripheral vascular disease, right foot ulcer <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> Right lower extremity angiogram Percutaneous transluminal angioplasty of the femoral popliteal artery, proximal anastomosis, with a 3 mm x 40 mm balloon Percutaneous transluminal angioplasty of the anterior tibial artery, distal anastomosis, with a 2 mm x 40 mm balloon. <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> ___ with history of peripheral arterial disease, status post right above knee popliteal to DP bypass with greater saphenous vein four months ago for a non-healing foot wound infected. On a routine ultrasound, his bypass was found to be near occluded. See office notes from ___ and ___. He presents for direct admission and pre hydration for angio. Of note he has baseline CKD and EF on most recent echo ___. Per OMR, dry weight is 94kg. He has also been on a lovenox bridge for history of DVTs and LV thrombus. Currently, his only complain is chronic right back pain radiating down right leg. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> -SYSTOLIC CHF with EF of ___ on Echo ___ dry weight 94kg -COCAINE ABUSE - last use ___ per OMR discharge summaries -MULTIPLE DEEP VENOUS THROMBOPHLEBITIS s/p IVC filter in place, on chronic warfarin. Complicated by edema/phlebitis. -DIABETES MELLITUS TYPE II: Last Hgb A1c 9.6 in ___ -CORONARY ARTERY DISEASE s/p anterior ST elevation MI, s/p DES to the LAD. Had angina led to --> DESx2 to RCA in ___ -HYPERCHOLESTEROLEMIA -BENIGN PROSTATIC HYPERTROPHY -HYPERTENSION -CHRONIC KIDNEY DISEASE (baseline Cr of 1.5-2.0) -PERIPHERAL VASCULAR DISEASE s/p PTA of the R peroneal artery and s/p right ___ toe amputation c/b nonhealing amputation site requiring above-knee popliteal to dorsalis pedis bypass graft with reverse saphenous vein (___) -H/O ACUTE PANCREATITIS -H/O CEREBROVASCULAR ACCIDENT resulting in chronic R leg pain and weakness -H/O LV THROMBUS, resolved on warfarin cholecystectomy ___ -IVC filter placement ___, ___ -Thrombolysis of R iliofemoral venous system, stenting & angioplasty of R EIV stricture ___, ___ -I&D of LLE ulceration with subsequent closure (___) -RLE angiogram, R peroneal angioplasty ___, ___ -Diagnostic RLE angiogram ___, ___ -R ___ toe amputation & debridement ___, ___ -I&D & debridement of R foot wound ___, ___ -Debridement of R foot wound w placement of retention sutures ___, ___ - Right above-knee popliteal to dorsalis pedis bypass with reverse saphenous vein graft, Angioscopy of saphenous vein conduit, Right foot debridement. <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Sister died from myocardial infarction, multiple family members with cancer (though he cannot recall what kind) including brother, sister, multiple aunts, nephew Physical ___: Exam: 98.4 70 117/67 18 100 RA Gen: Well appearing & in NAD CV: reg rate Pulm: breathing comfortably, no respiratory distress Abd: soft nt nd no rebound/guarding R toe amp site looks clean/dry and intact. ___ right toe previously removed R: d/d/d/d L: p/d/d/d <PERTINENT RESULTS> ___ 06: 30AM BLOOD WBC-7.4 RBC-3.65* Hgb-10.2* Hct-33.7* MCV-92 MCH-27.9 MCHC-30.3* RDW-17.5* RDWSD-59.5* Plt ___ ___ 07: 20AM BLOOD WBC-8.2 RBC-3.43* Hgb-9.5* Hct-31.3* MCV-91 MCH-27.7 MCHC-30.4* RDW-17.4* RDWSD-57.5* Plt ___ ___ 01: 20PM BLOOD WBC-8.1 RBC-3.41* Hgb-9.4* Hct-30.9* MCV-91 MCH-27.6 MCHC-30.4* RDW-17.1* RDWSD-54.4* Plt ___ ___ 08: 00AM BLOOD WBC-7.7 RBC-3.70* Hgb-10.2* Hct-35.0* MCV-95 MCH-27.6 MCHC-29.1* RDW-17.2* RDWSD-58.3* Plt ___ ___ 06: 30AM BLOOD Plt ___ ___ 06: 30AM BLOOD PTT-87.1* ___ 03: 00PM BLOOD PTT-82.1* ___ 07: 20AM BLOOD Plt ___ ___ 08: 00AM BLOOD Glucose-186* UreaN-28* Creat-2.0* Na-137 K-4.7 Cl-102 HCO3-25 AnGap-15 ___ 06: 30AM BLOOD Glucose-230* UreaN-30* Creat-2.2* Na-137 K-4.8 Cl-100 HCO3-26 AnGap-16 ___ 07: 20AM BLOOD Glucose-225* UreaN-27* Creat-2.0* Na-137 K-4.8 Cl-102 HCO3-27 AnGap-13 ___ 08: 00AM BLOOD Glucose-200* UreaN-32* Creat-1.5* Na-135 K-4.7 Cl-101 HCO3-22 AnGap-17 ___ 10: 05PM BLOOD CK(CPK)-79 ___ 08: 00AM BLOOD Calcium-9.1 Phos-4.3 Mg-1.8 ___ 08: 00AM BLOOD Phos-3.5 Mg-1.9 ___ 07: 55AM BLOOD Calcium-9.0 Phos-3.9 Mg-2.0 ___ 06: 30AM BLOOD Vanco-18.3 ___ 07: 20AM BLOOD Vanco-15.9 <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Acetaminophen 1000 mg PO Q8H pain, fever 2. CarBAMazepine 100 mg PO QHS 3. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 4. Atorvastatin 80 mg PO QPM 5. Carvedilol 3.125 mg PO BID 6. Finasteride 5 mg PO DAILY 7. OxycoDONE (Immediate Release) 5 mg PO Q3H: PRN moderate pain 8. Pancrelipase 5000 1 CAP PO QIDWMHS 9. Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q12H 10. ammonium lactate 12 % topical BID 11. Artificial Tears ___ DROP BOTH EYES PRN dry eyes 12. Ascorbic Acid ___ mg PO BID 13. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID 14. FoLIC Acid 1 mg PO DAILY 15. Guaifenesin 5 mL PO Q6H: PRN cough 16. Lidocaine 5% Patch 1 PTCH TD QAM 17. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 18. Polyethylene Glycol 17 g PO DAILY 19. Senna 17.2 mg PO QHS 20. Gabapentin 600 mg PO QHS 21. Gabapentin 400 mg PO BID 22. Torsemide 10 mg PO DAILY 23. Warfarin 7.5 mg PO DAILY16 24. Juven (arginine-glutamine-calcium Hmb) ___ gram oral BID 25. solifenacin 10 mg oral DAILY 26. Glargine 38 Units Breakfast Insulin SC Sliding Scale using HUM Insulin 27. Creon 12 1 CAP PO TID W/MEALS 28. Lisinopril 2.5 mg PO DAILY <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Acetaminophen 1000 mg PO Q8H pain, fever 2. Artificial Tears ___ DROP BOTH EYES PRN dry eyes 3. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 4. Atorvastatin 80 mg PO QPM 5. CarBAMazepine 100 mg PO QHS 6. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID 7. Finasteride 5 mg PO DAILY 8. FoLIC Acid 1 mg PO DAILY 9. Gabapentin 600 mg PO QHS 10. Lisinopril 2.5 mg PO DAILY 11. OxycoDONE (Immediate Release) 5 mg PO Q3H: PRN moderate pain RX *oxycodone 5 mg ___ tablet(s) by mouth q4 Disp #*30 Tablet Refills: *0 12. Pancrelipase 5000 1 CAP PO QIDWMHS 13. Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q12H 14. Polyethylene Glycol 17 g PO DAILY 15. Senna 17.2 mg PO QHS 16. Torsemide 10 mg PO DAILY 17. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 18. Gabapentin 400 mg PO BID 19. Glargine 38 Units Breakfast Insulin SC Sliding Scale using HUM Insulin 20. Clopidogrel 75 mg PO DAILY 21. Warfarin 7.5 mg PO DAILY16 22. Sulfameth/Trimethoprim DS 1 TAB PO BID Duration: 10 Days 23. Carvedilol 3.125 mg PO BID 24. Creon 12 1 CAP PO TID W/MEALS 25. Juven (arginine-glutamine-calcium Hmb) ___ gram oral BID 26. solifenacin 10 mg oral DAILY <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Extended Care Facility: ___ <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Peripheral arterial disease Nonhealing right foot ulcer <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Non weight bearing on Right Leg <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Dear ___ were admitted to ___ and underwent a ___ have now recovered from surgery and are ready to be discharged. Please follow the instructions below to continue your recovery: MEDICATION: - Take Aspirin 325mg (enteric coated) once daily - If instructed, take Plavix (Clopidogrel) 75mg once daily - Continue all other medications ___ were taking before surgery, unless otherwise directed - ___ make take Tylenol or prescribed pain medications for any post procedure pain or discomfort WHAT TO EXPECT: It is normal to have slight swelling of the legs: - Elevate your leg above the level of your heart with pillows every ___ hours throughout the day and night - Avoid prolonged periods of standing or sitting without your legs elevated - It is normal to feel tired and have a decreased appetite, your appetite will return with time - Drink plenty of fluids and eat small frequent meals - It is important to eat nutritious food options (high fiber, lean meats, vegetables/fruits, low fat, low cholesterol) to maintain your strength and assist in wound healing - To avoid constipation: eat a high fiber diet and use stool softener while taking pain medication ACTIVITIES: - When ___ go home, ___ may walk and use stairs - ___ may shower (let the soapy water run over groin incision, rinse and pat dry) - Your incision may be left uncovered, unless ___ have small amounts of drainage from the wound, then place a dry dressing or band aid over the area - No heavy lifting, pushing or pulling (greater than 5 lbs) for 1 week (to allow groin puncture to heal) - After 1 week, ___ may resume sexual activity - After 1 week, gradually increase your activities and distance walked as ___ can tolerate - No driving until ___ are no longer taking pain medications CALL THE OFFICE FOR: ___ - Numbness, coldness or pain in lower extremities - Temperature greater than 101.5F for 24 hours - New or increased drainage from incision or white, yellow or green drainage from incisions - Bleeding from groin puncture site SUDDEN, SEVERE BLEEDING OR SWELLING (Groin puncture site) - Lie down, keep leg straight and have someone apply firm pressure to area for 10 minutes. If bleeding stops, call vascular office ___. If bleeding does not stop, call ___ for transfer to closest Emergency Room. AMPUTATION DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS ACTIVITY: - On the side of your amputation ___ are non weight bearing for ___ weeks. - ___ should keep this amputation site elevated when ever possible. - ___ may use the opposite foot for transfers and pivots. - No driving until cleared by your Surgeon. - No heavy lifting greater than 20 pounds for the next 3 weeks. BATHING/SHOWERING: - ___ may shower when ___ get home - No tub baths or pools / do not soak your foot for 4 weeks from your date of surgery WOUND CARE: - Sutures / Staples may have been removed before discharge. If they are not, an appointment will be made for ___ to return for staple removal. - When the sutures are removed the doctor may or may not place pieces of tape called steri-strips over the incision. These will stay on about a week and ___ may shower with them on. If these do not fall off after 10 days, ___ may peel them off with warm water and soap in the shower. CAUTIONS: - If ___ smoke, please make every attempt to quit. Your primary care physician can help with this. Smoking causes narrowing of your blood vessels which in turn decreases circulation. DIET: - Low fat, low cholesterol / if ___ are diabetic \u0096 follow your dietary restrictions as before CALL THE OFFICE FOR: ___ - Bleeding, redness of, or drainage from your foot wound - New pain, numbness or discoloration of the skin on the effected foot - Fever greater than 101 degrees, chills, increased redness, or pus draining from the incision site. Weigh yourself every morning, call MD if weight goes up more than 3 lbs. ", "bhc": "Mr. ___ underwent an angiogram on ___ which showed Patent right superficial femoral artery, greater than 90% stenosis of the femoral popliteal, proximal vein graft anastomosis and greater than 90% stenosis of the distal vein graft at the distal anastomosis with the dorsalis pedis. He then underwent balloon angioplasty of the proximal and distal portions of the anastomoses. he tolerated the procedure well with no complications and was transferred to the PACU in stable condition. He had a right foot XR on ___ which showed osteomyelitis so on ___, he went to the OR with podiatry for an amputation of his right ___ metatarsal head due to a nonhealing foot ulcer. He tolerated that procedure well with no complications. Prior to his procedures, on ___, he had a new Mobitz I on EKG and cardiology recommended that we hold his carvedilol. He was started on coumadin on ___ and his heparin drip was continued. His most recent INR was 1.2 on ___ after he received 7.5mg of coumadin.he will be bridged with levenox upon discharge with INR checks after he leaves the hospital. He complained of a groin hematoma which was duplexed and found to not be an issue after review with the attending. He was started on Vanc/Cefepime and Flagyl on ___ for coverage of the coag+ staph aureus and corynebacterium growing on his cultures. He will be discharged home on a 10 day course of bactrim. He was ambulating, tolerating a regular diet and voiding. His pain was controlled on oral pain medications so he was discharged home with follow up instructions with podiatry and Vascular Surgery. His INR will be checked in rehab and he has an appointment on ___ with his PCP ___ who has agreed to manage his coumadin. His Cr is at his baseline of ___ but it will be checked in rehab and also trended afterwards to ensure that it continues to trend down "}
{"hadm_id": "11917349-DS-5", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> Tylenol/Codeine No.3 / Percocet / Tetracycline / Sulfa (Sulfonamides) / Aloe ___ ___ <CHIEF COMPLAINT> neck pain <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> none. <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> ___ y/o F h/o NIDDM, epilepsy, HTN, chronic pain c/b opioid dependence transferred from ___ after presenting with neck pain. She had the abrupt onset of acute on chronic anterior/posterior/left acute dull, squeezing neck pain after rolling over in bed two days ago. Her pain radiates down the left arm and is associated with left arm parasthesias, both of which are chronic. She denies new weakness or bowel or bladder incontinence. Admission V/S were unremarkable and labs were notable for Na 126 (was 122 at OSH) which has corrected to 137 with NS, and Hct 33% which is slightly above her b/l ~30%. She did not tolerate a cervical spine MRI due to nausea. The consulting neurology team suspected that her symptoms were most consistent with worsening of her chronic cervical myelopathy with associated neck spasm, and recommended a soft cervical collar to be worn at night, current pain regimen, and ___. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> NIDDM c/b gastroparesis - A1c 6.3% in ___ Epilepsy Atypical chest pain - nl cath ___ Grade 4 reflux esophagitis ___ EGD) HTN Hyperlipidemia Chronic back pain s/p discectomy x 2 c/b opioid dependence Bilateral ovarian serous cystadenoma, R ovarian ___ tumor, endometriosis Seasonal allergies <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Mother - COPD Father - MI at ___, DM Brother - MI in early ___ <PHYSICAL EXAM> ADMISSION PHYSICAL EXAM: V/S: Wt 147.7 lbs T 97.8 BP 98/60 HR 60 RR 20 O2sat 97% RA FSG 118 GEN: Well-appearing middle-aged woman in NAD HEENT: sclera anicteric, OP clear with MMM, disconjugate gaze, PERRL NECK: limited ROM in extension/lateral movement ___ pain, no cervical spine TTP; 2+ carotid pulses, no bruit/thrill LUNGS: CTAB no w/r/r CV: reg rate nl S1S2 no m/r/g ABD: soft, NTND normoactive BS EXT: warm, dry +PP no c/c/e NEURO: Drowsy but arousable to appropriate level of alertness, converses appropriately and attentive; CN II-XII intact; ___ strength in BLE/BUE ___ sensation to light touch intact in all distributions; 2+ bi/tri/BR/patellar DTR's, 1+ ankle DTR; no pronator drift; finger-to-nose testing intact; gait not assessed <PERTINENT RESULTS> ___ 04: 20PM BLOOD WBC-10.3# RBC-3.99*# Hgb-12.0 Hct-33.2* MCV-83# MCH-30.2 MCHC-36.2* RDW-14.2 Plt ___ ___ 04: 20PM BLOOD Neuts-76.8* Lymphs-17.8* Monos-4.5 Eos-0.5 Baso-0.4 ___ 07: 30AM BLOOD WBC-7.4 RBC-4.15* Hgb-12.0 Hct-35.4* MCV-85 MCH-28.8 MCHC-33.8 RDW-14.5 Plt ___ ___ 03: 02PM BLOOD WBC-6.3 RBC-3.56* Hgb-10.7* Hct-31.0* MCV-87 MCH-30.0 MCHC-34.4 RDW-14.5 Plt ___ ___ 06: 35AM BLOOD WBC-5.6 RBC-3.56* Hgb-10.7* Hct-30.9* MCV-87 MCH-30.1 MCHC-34.7 RDW-14.7 Plt ___ ___ 04: 20PM BLOOD ___ PTT-32.4 ___ ___ 04: 20PM BLOOD Glucose-96 UreaN-4* Creat-0.4 Na-126* K-4.0 Cl-94* HCO3-24 AnGap-12 ___ 07: 30AM BLOOD Glucose-94 UreaN-3* Creat-0.4 Na-137 K-4.2 Cl-104 HCO3-24 AnGap-13 ___ 06: 35AM BLOOD Glucose-83 UreaN-3* Creat-0.5 Na-137 K-4.0 Cl-105 HCO3-24 AnGap-12 ___ 04: 30PM URINE Color-Yellow Appear-Clear Sp ___ ___ 04: 30PM URINE Blood-TR Nitrite-NEG Protein-NEG Glucose-NEG Ketone-NEG Bilirub-NEG Urobiln-NEG pH-7.0 Leuks-NEG ___ 04: 30PM URINE ___ Bacteri-FEW Yeast-NONE ___ 03: 00PM URINE Color-Yellow Appear-Hazy Sp ___ ___ 03: 00PM URINE Blood-SM Nitrite-POS Protein-NEG Glucose-NEG Ketone-TR Bilirub-NEG Urobiln-NEG pH-6.5 Leuks-TR ___ 03: 00PM URINE ___ Bacteri-MANY Yeast-NONE ___ . ___ 3: 00 pm URINE ___. URINE CULTURE (Preliminary): GRAM NEGATIVE ROD(S). >100,000 ORGANISMS/ML.. . ___ CXR Normal appearance of the lung parenchyma, no parenchymal disease. Normal shape and aspect of the cardiac silhouette and the hilar and mediastinal contours. . ___ C-SPINE X-RAY FINDINGS: Normal bone density, the physiologic cervical lordosis is markedly decreased. There is a relatively sharp angle at the level of C5 and C6, there appears to be a fusion between C6 and C7. No evidence of compression fractures, no evidence of pathologic vertebral displacement. The anteflexion, however, is markedly reduced overall. Mild dorsal spondylosis of the entire C-spine. Minimal arthrosis of the intravertebral joints. . ___ C-SPINE MRI W/O CONTRAST - Preliminary Report !! PFI !! 1. Congenital block vertebral body. At C6 through T1 with partial obliteration of the dorsal intervertebral disc spaces; there is no motion at this segment, as demonstrated on the concurrent flexion/extension series, which may relate to abnormal motion at the C5-6 and more superior levels, and the degenerative changes, as detailed above. 2. C5-6: Multifactorial canal narrowing with no spinal cord deformity or intrinsic signal abnormality; bilateral neural foraminal narrowing with possible bilateral exiting C6 nerve root impingement. 3. C4-5: Disc - left endplate/uncovertebral complex severely encroaches on the left neural foramen, likely impinging upon the exiting left C5 nerve root. 4. Unremarkable STIR sequence with no acute-appearing abnormality. . ___ MRA NECK - Preliminary Report !! PFI !! Unremarkable enhanced study of the cervical vessels with no specific evidence of vertebral or carotid arterial dissection or flow-limiting stenosis and overall unremarkable appearance to the aortic arch. <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> fentanyl patch 150 mcg/hr simvastatin 20 mg daily tizanidine 2 mg BID ASA 81 mg daily pantoprazole 40 mg BID metoclopramide 10 mg QID tegretol 400 BID (recent decrease from ___ estradiol 0.0375 patch Q72H alendronate 70 mg weekly - NOT TAKING carafate 1 gram QID vicodin 2 tab TID diazepam 7 mg TID senna BID lidoderm patch lactulose prn glyburide 2.5 mg daily - NOT TAKING imdur 60 mg daily miralax MSIR 45 mg TID <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Cipro 250 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO twice a day for 3 days. Disp: *6 Tablet(s)* Refills: *0* 2. Duragesic 75 mcg/hr Patch 72 hr Sig: Two (2) patches Transdermal every ___ (72) hours: Do not drive or drink alcohol while using this medication. 3. Simvastatin 20 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO once a day. 4. Tizanidine 2 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO BID (2 times a day): Do not drive or drink alcohol while using this medication. 5. Aspirin 81 mg Tablet, Chewable Sig: One (1) Tablet, Chewable PO DAILY (Daily). 6. Pantoprazole 40 mg Tablet, Delayed Release (E.C.) Sig: One (1) Tablet, Delayed Release (E.C.) PO Q12H (every 12 hours). 7. Metoclopramide 10 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO QIDACHS (4 times a day (before meals and at bedtime)). 8. Carbamazepine 200 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO BID (2 times a day). 9. Estradiol 0.0375 mg/24 hr Patch Semiweekly Sig: One (1) patch Transdermal Q72 hours (). 10. Sucralfate 1 gram Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO QID (4 times a day). 11. Senna 8.6 mg Tablet Sig: ___ Tablets PO BID (2 times a day). 12. Polyethylene Glycol 3350 100 % Powder Sig: One (1) packet PO DAILY (Daily). 13. Lidocaine 5 %(700 mg/patch) Adhesive Patch, Medicated Sig: One (1) Adhesive Patch, Medicated Topical DAILY (Daily): remove after 12 hours. 14. Diazepam 2 mg Tablet Sig: 3.5 Tablets PO TID (3 times a day) as needed for pain: Do not drive or drink alcohol while using this medication. 15. Imdur 60 mg Tablet Sustained Release 24 hr Sig: One (1) Tablet Sustained Release 24 hr PO at bedtime. 16. Morphine 30 mg Tablet Sig: 1.5 Tablets PO TID (3 times a day) as needed for pain: Do not drive or drink alcohol while using this medication. 17. Hydrocodone-Acetaminophen ___ mg Tablet Sig: ___ Tablets PO TID (3 times a day) as needed for pain: Do not drive or drink alcohol while using this medication. 18. Lactulose 10 gram/15 mL Syrup Sig: Thirty (30) ML PO Q8H (every 8 hours) as needed for constipation. <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Primary 1) Cervical spondylosis 2) Hyponatremia 3) Uncomplicated cystitis 4) Chronic pain Secondary 1) Non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus 2) Epilepsy 3) Grade IV Reflux esophagitis 4) Hypertension 5) Hyperlipidemia <DISCHARGE CONDITION> clinically improved with stable vital signs. <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> You were admitted to the hospital with neck pain, likely due to a muscle strain on top of chronic degenerative arthritis in your cervical spine. An MRI of your neck and cervical spine did NOT show any problems with the spinal cord or blood vessels. You were also diagnosed with a urinary tract infection. Please take ciprofloxacin twice daily for 3 days, through ___, ___. No changes were made to your other medications. Please continue taking your medications as prescribed. Please call the office of Dr. ___ at ___ for an appointment within 1 week. Please call the ___ Department of Neurology at ___ for an appointment at your earliest convenience. Please call your physician or return to the Emergency Department if you experience fever, chills, sweats, dizziness, lightheadedness, chest pain, palpitations, cough, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, flank or back pain, difficulty with urination, diarrhea, or bloody or dark stools. ", "bhc": "#Cervical spondylosis - The patient's symptoms were most attributable to neck muscle strain and spasm on top of her chronic degenerative disc disease. Plain films of the C-spine showed normal bone density, decreased physiologic cervical lordosis, a relatively sharp angle at the level of C5 and C6, a fusion between C6 and C7, mild dorsal spondylosis of the entire C-spine, and minimal arthrosis of the intravertebral joints but no evidence of compression fracture or pathologic vertebral displacement. C-spine MRI preliminarily showed congenital block vertebral body at C6 through T1 with partial obliteration of the dorsal intervertebral disc spaces, multifactorial canal narrowing with no spinal cord deformity or intrinsic signal abnormality at C5-C6, bilateral neural foraminal narrowing with possible bilateral exiting C6 nerve root impingement, and at C4-C5 left endplate/uncovertebral complex encroachment on the left neural foramen with likely impingement upon the exiting left C5 nerve root. MRA of the neck was normal. Pain and spasm was well-controlled on the patient's outpatient medication regimen. The patient was advised not to drive or drink alcohol while taking her usual medications. She was advised to follow up with neurology at her earliest convenience. . #Hyponatremia - Corrected with saline administration. . #Uncomplicated cystitis - Preliminary urine culture grew >100K GNR. Given cipro x 3 days since prior GNR isolates have been sensitive to FQ's. . #Chronic pain - Continued the outpatient regimen. . #Non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus - Blood sugars were well-controlled on an insulin sliding scale. . #Epilepsy - No acute issues. Continued tegretol. . #Reflux esophagitis - Continued carafate and PPI. . #Hypertension - Blood pressure well-controlled on imdur. . #Hyperlipidemia - Continued statin. "}
{"hadm_id": "19005323-DS-22", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> cefazolin / Tegaderm <ATTENDING> ___ <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Leukopenia <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> None <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> Mr. ___ is a ___ with history of HCV/alcohol cirrhosis c/b HCC s/p deceased donor liver transplant ___ who presents for further management and evaluation of leukopenia (WBC 1.9 with ANC 264). Pt received some lab work yesterday at ___. He was found to have leukopenia (WBC 1.9) and neutropenic (264). Currently, CMV VL and G6PD are still pending. Otherwise, labs notable for Cr 1.0 (1.4, 1.3), AST 48 (57, 60), ALT 55 (65, 77), tacro level 6.8 currently on tacrolimus 1.5mg bid. Unfortunately, patient was not able to receive neupogen d/t insurance issues. He is been admitted for further workup of leukopenia and neupogen administration. Of note, he endorsed baseline arthralgias, secondary to severe arthritis. In addition, he had myalgias with perhaps rhinorrhea. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> - EtOH and HCV cirrhosis, diuretic refractory ascites currenlty getting paracenteses every 2 weeks, currently being evaluated for liver transplantation - Umbillical hernia - Chronic abdominal pain - Hypertension - GERD - Elevated creatinine - RBBB - Right inguinal hernia - Microscopic hematuria, OSH cystoscopy unremarkable - S/p TACE on ___, RFA on ___, laminectomy in ___ (reoperated for infection) <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> - Brother died age ___ of HCC, alcohol abuse, - Brother died age ___ of MI <PHYSICAL EXAM> Admission <PHYSICAL EXAM> Vitals: General: T 97.6 BP 160/88 HR 85 R 20 SpO2 99%/RA 218 lbs HEENT: sclerae anicteric, PERRL Neck: supple, no JVD Heart: regular, normal S1 & S2 without murmurs appreciated Lungs: clear to auscultation bilaterally Abdomen: well-healing RUQ scar from liver transplant, large abdomen, soft, non-tender, not tympanitic, normal bowel sounds Extremities: warm, well-perfused Skin: diffuse macular rash on all 4 extremities and chest without papules, petechiae or purpura Neurological: face symmetric, moving all extremities well, oriented x4 Discharge <PHYSICAL EXAM> Vitals: 97.9 ___ 18 100%/RA Weight: 98.9 kg <- 218 lbs General: Pleasant, resting comfortably, NAD HEENT: sclerae anicteric, NCAT Neck: supple Heart: regular, normal S1 & S2 without murmurs appreciated Lungs: clear to auscultation bilaterally Abdomen: well-healing RUQ scar from liver transplant, large abdomen, soft, non-tender Extremities: warm, well-perfused, no edema. Skin: Resolved diffuse macular rash on all 4 extremities and chest without papules, petechiae or purpura. Congenital derm lesion starting above L buttock. Neurological: face symmetric, moving all extremities well, oriented x4 <PERTINENT RESULTS> ADMISSION LABS ___ 11: 45PM BLOOD WBC-1.8* RBC-4.03* Hgb-11.8* Hct-35.4* MCV-88 MCH-29.3 MCHC-33.3 RDW-15.7* RDWSD-50.3* Plt ___ ___ 11: 45PM BLOOD Neuts-17* Bands-0 Lymphs-68* Monos-10 Eos-3 Baso-0 Atyps-2* ___ Myelos-0 AbsNeut-0.31* AbsLymp-1.26 AbsMono-0.18* AbsEos-0.05 AbsBaso-0.00* ___ 11: 45PM BLOOD ___ PTT-28.4 ___ ___ 04: 22AM BLOOD Parst S-NEGATIVE ___ 11: 45PM BLOOD Glucose-109* UreaN-22* Creat-0.9 Na-140 K-3.6 Cl-107 HCO3-25 AnGap-12 ___ 11: 45PM BLOOD ALT-63* AST-50* LD(LDH)-183 AlkPhos-103 TotBili-0.4 ___ 11: 45PM BLOOD Calcium-9.0 Phos-3.1 Mg-1.4* ___ 04: 22AM BLOOD tacroFK-10.1 DISCHARGE LABS ___ 04: 18AM BLOOD WBC-2.1* RBC-4.35* Hgb-12.7* Hct-38.7* MCV-89 MCH-29.2 MCHC-32.8 RDW-15.9* RDWSD-50.6* Plt ___ ___ 04: 18AM BLOOD Neuts-24* Bands-5 ___ Monos-5 Eos-3 Baso-2* Atyps-10* ___ Myelos-0 NRBC-2* AbsNeut-0.61* AbsLymp-1.28 AbsMono-0.11* AbsEos-0.06 AbsBaso-0.04 ___ 04: 18AM BLOOD Hypochr-NORMAL Anisocy-NORMAL Poiklo-NORMAL Macrocy-NORMAL Microcy-NORMAL Polychr-NORMAL ___ 04: 18AM BLOOD Glucose-91 UreaN-18 Creat-0.9 Na-138 K-3.8 Cl-106 HCO3-23 AnGap-13 ___ 04: 22AM BLOOD ALT-64* AST-55* LD(LDH)-180 AlkPhos-104 TotBili-0.4 ___ 04: 18AM BLOOD Calcium-9.1 Phos-3.0 Mg-1.5* ___ 04: 18AM BLOOD tacroFK-8.8 <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Dear Mr. ___, You were seen at ___ because your white blood cells were low, which puts you at risk of infection. We did not find evidence of infection and we think your low cell counts are due to your medication, which may also be causing your rash. You were put on a medication to help your white blood cells increase and some of your home medications were switched. Please see below for your update medication list. Please call your doctor or come to the emergency department if you experience fevers or worsening headache with neck stiffness, light sensitivity, or vomiting. Please have labs drawn on ___, which will be sent to ___ and Dr. ___ ___. It was a pleasure taking care of you and we wish you all the best. Happy holidays, Your ___ team ", "bhc": "Mr. ___ is a ___ with history of HCV/alcohol cirrhosis complicated by ___ s/p deceased donor liver transplant on ___ who presents for further management and evaluation of leukopenia (WBC 1.9 with ANC 264) and rash. ACTIVE ISSUES # Leukopenia/ Neutropenia: Most likely due to immunosuppressive drugs including tacrolimus or mycophenolate, which are known to cause marrow suppression, or TMP-SMX, which is known to cause agranulocytosis. His symptoms are temporally correlated with a switch in his cellcept formulation. The patient is also at risk for opportunistic infections, particularly CMV, however CMV was negative. Other viral serologies still pending. Given recent tick bite, tick-borne illness workup sent, negative to date. His TMP-SMX was stopped ___ for neutropenia and he was switched to Dapsone (G6PD negative) for PCP ___. Inpatient, he was treated with Neupogen 480 mcg daily with improvement in his counts, though insurance appeal for outpatient coverage pending at discharge. On discharge, he should be on the previous formulation of cellcept which he tolerated. He will need labs on ___ for monitoring prior to transplant follow-up appt next ___. # Liver failure, s/p diseased donor liver transplant: history of HCV/alcohol cirrhosis s/p DD liver transplant on ___, on tacrolimus and mycophenolate for immunosuppression. Pt has history of HCC and TACE x2 prior to transplantation. He was continued on tacrolimus at his home dose of 1.5mg bid but decreased his mycophenolate to 500mg (decreased ___. He was continued valgancyclovir for CMV PPX and his Bactrim was switched to dapsone as above. # Rash: The patient presented with a macular, migratory, pruritic, lasting only a few hours at a time. It also affects palms. DDx included drug rash vs. less likely viral (CMV neg). ___ be associated with new formulation of mycophenolate as symptoms seemed to start after he switched to a different generic formulation or from Bactrim. The rash was present on admission but resolved rapidly. # HA: The patient developed a bitemporal frontal HA not different from usual. No meningeal signs or fever. Likely tension HA, tacrolimus level was 10->8.8. CHRONIC ISSUES # HTN: Continued on amlodipine and metoprolol # GERD: chronic and stable. Continued on famotidine ======================================= TRANSITIONAL ISSUES: - Was treated with Neupogen inpatient. ___ need additional dose(s) after discharge, insurance issues being coordinated with ___ and pharmacy - Needs labs (CBC with diff, Chem10, Liver panel, Tacrolimus) drawn ___ prior to appointment next week - Switched back to old formulation of mycophenolate as new formulation may be causing side effects, insurance issues being coordinated with ___ and pharmacy. Patient was given 1mo supply per insurance override; this will need to be addressed at follow-up. - Bactrim was discontinued for leukopenia - Dapsone was started as an alternate to Bactrim for PCP ___. G6PD was negative based on QUEST labs. - Patient should follow up with Transplant ___ clinic appointment ___. - Infectious work up including EBV, HHV-6 viral loads, anaplasma serologies/PCR are pending - Hepatitis C treatment to be discussed at liver followup - Started on supplemental oral magnesium # Code: Full # Contact: wife, ___, ___ ___ on Admission: The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Acetaminophen 1000 mg PO BID:PRN PAIN 2. Metoprolol Tartrate 50 mg PO BID 3. Amlodipine 10 mg PO DAILY 4. Famotidine 20 mg PO BID 5. Mycophenolate Mofetil 500 mg PO QID 6. Tacrolimus 1.5 mg PO Q12H 7. ValGANCIclovir 900 mg PO Q24H 8. TraMADOL (Ultram) 50 mg PO DAILY:PRN ARTHRITIS 9. Sodium Polystyrene Sulfonate 15 gm PO DAILY:PRN ELEVATED K 10. Sulfameth/Trimethoprim DS 1 TAB PO DAILY Discharge Medications: 1. Magnesium Oxide 400 mg PO DAILY Do not take tacrolimus within 2 hours of magnesium RX *magnesium oxide 400 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth daily Disp #*30 Tablet Refills:*0 2. Outpatient Lab Work Please check CBC with diff, Chem 10, Liver panel (AST/ALT/Alk Phos/TBili), and Tacrolimus level and fax to ___ and Dr. ___ (___) ICD10 Z94.4 3. Acetaminophen 1000 mg PO BID:PRN PAIN 4. Amlodipine 10 mg PO DAILY 5. Famotidine 20 mg PO BID 6. Metoprolol Tartrate 50 mg PO BID 7. Mycophenolate Mofetil 500 mg PO BID RX *mycophenolate mofetil 500 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth twice a day Disp #*60 Tablet Refills:*0 8. Tacrolimus 1.5 mg PO Q12H 9. TraMADOL (Ultram) 50 mg PO DAILY:PRN ARTHRITIS 10. ValGANCIclovir 900 mg PO Q24H 11. Dapsone 100 mg PO DAILY RX *dapsone 100 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth Daily Disp #*30 Tablet Refills:*0 12. Sodium Polystyrene Sulfonate 15 gm PO DAILY:PRN ELEVATED K Discharge Disposition: Home Discharge Diagnosis: Neutropenia Rash, possibly due to drug side effect Status post liver transplant Discharge Condition: Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. Followup Instructions: ___"}
{"hadm_id": "11162709-DS-18", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> Sulfa (Sulfonamide Antibiotics) / Augmentin / lisinopril / ciprofloxacin <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Dysuria, Fever <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> None <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> Mr. ___ is a ___ year old male with PMH notable for hx of left ICA aneurysm rupture in ___ s/p clipping, complicated by residual aneurysm. s/p pipeline coiling on ___, BPH, HTN, HLD, and known Plavix resistance who presents with dysuria. Of note, pt. was recently admitted from ___ to ___ for an elective embolization of a LICA aneurysm. Pt. underwent pipeline embolization L ICA on ___ without difficulty. Post-op course complicated by acute urinary retention (pt. had bladder scan which showed 1 liter of retained urine). Pt. had a straight cath, continued to retain and required foley catheter placement on ___. Pt. was discharged with the foley in place with plan to have foley removed as an outpatient and to have a voiding trial. Pt's foley was removed on ___ without difficulty, Dr. ___ (___). Pt. voided without issue after removal of foley. Since this time, pt. notes dysuria, specifically tenderness to the urethra region when urinating since the time of removal. He also notes increased urinary urgency. He typically urinates once overnight, however over the last several days has urinated ___ / night. Fevers/chills began on the morning of presentation. Pt. woke up in the afternoon, felt ongoing chills, temperature was noted to be 102.1. Pt. denies any blood in his urine when foley was placed or since removal, noted normal colored urine. ___ RN was contacted and recommended referral to the ED. Pt. otherwise denies lightheadedness, dizziness, CP, SOB, cough, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, or changes in bowel habits. In the ED, initial vital signs were: 101.3, 126, 149/84, 16, 98% on RA - Exam was notable for: well healed R groin angio site, no other significant findings - Labs were notable for: WBC 18.3, H/H stable (possibly contracted), INR 1.2, Creatinine 1.3, Bun 23, Mg 1.7, Phos 2.5, INR 1.2, lactate 1.6, UA notable for positive nitrites, positive leuks, >182 WBC, 12 RBC - Imaging: CXR unremarkable - Bladder scan with only 60cc PVR, as such no foley was inserted - The patient was given: 2L NS, Tylenol, and Cipro. During the cipro infusion, pt. had a local skin reaction (phlebitis) with itching and redness that moved into the elbow. Cipro was stopped. Pt. tolerated ceftriaxone 1G IV x1 without difficulty. - BCx/UCx pending - Consults: Neurosurgery was consulted as an FYI given recent procedure. They recommend that if blood cultures are positive to consult ID given that recently surgically implanted hardware cannot be removed. Vitals prior to transfer were: 98.2, 91, 126/75, 18, 96% on RA Upon arrival to the floor, pt. feels improved although he does note ongoing tenderness in the urethra. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> # Hx of left ICA aneurysm rupture in ___ s/p clipping (residual metal clip in place), complicated by residual aneurysm. s/p pipeline coiling on ___ complicated by acute urinary retention requiring foley # BPH with hx. of urinary obstruction # HLD # HTN # Known Plavix resistance (platelet aggregation testing done on ___ showed clopidogrel resistance) # Hx. of Nephrolithiasis # Hx. of vocal cord paralysis # Hyperacusis # Hx. of hyperplastic colon polyps <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Father with ___ and ___. Mother with ___ Disease <PHYSICAL EXAM> ON ADMISSION: VITALS: 97.9, 139/70, 89, 16, 99% on RA GENERAL: Pleasant, well-appearing, in no apparent distress. HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic, no conjunctival pallor or scleral icterus, PERRLA, EOMI, OP clear. NECK: Supple, no LAD, no thyromegaly, JVP flat. CARDIAC: RRR, normal S1/S2, no murmurs rubs or gallops. PULMONARY: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, without wheezes or rhonchi. ABDOMEN: Normal bowel sounds, soft, non-tender, non-distended, no organomegaly, no suprapubic tenderness EXTREMITIES: Warm, well-perfused, no cyanosis, clubbing or edema. SKIN: Without rash. NEUROLOGIC: A&Ox3, CN II-XII grossly normal, normal sensation, with strength ___ throughout. ON DISCHARGE: VITALS: 98.5, 110s-140s/70s-80s, 80s-90s, ___, 96-98% on RA GENERAL: Pleasant, well-appearing, in no apparent distress. HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic, no conjunctival pallor or scleral icterus, PERRLA, EOMI, OP clear. NECK: Supple, no LAD, no thyromegaly, JVP flat. CARDIAC: RRR, normal S1/S2, no murmurs rubs or gallops. PULMONARY: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, without wheezes or rhonchi. ABDOMEN: Normal bowel sounds, soft, non-tender, non-distended, no organomegaly, no suprapubic tenderness EXTREMITIES: Warm, well-perfused, no cyanosis, clubbing or edema. GU: Mild erythema around meatus SKIN: Without rash. NEUROLOGIC: A&Ox3, CN II-XII grossly normal, normal sensation, with strength ___ throughout. <PERTINENT RESULTS> ON ADMISSION: ___ 08: 00PM BLOOD WBC-18.3*# RBC-4.64 Hgb-15.4 Hct-42.1 MCV-91 MCH-33.2* MCHC-36.6 RDW-12.6 RDWSD-41.4 Plt ___ ___ 08: 00PM BLOOD Neuts-81.8* Lymphs-5.3* Monos-12.0 Eos-0.0* Baso-0.2 Im ___ AbsNeut-15.00* AbsLymp-0.97* AbsMono-2.19* AbsEos-0.00* AbsBaso-0.03 ___ 08: 00PM BLOOD Hypochr-NORMAL Anisocy-NORMAL Poiklo-NORMAL Macrocy-NORMAL Microcy-NORMAL Polychr-NORMAL ___ 08: 00PM BLOOD ___ PTT-34.5 ___ ___ 08: 00PM BLOOD Glucose-143* UreaN-23* Creat-1.3* Na-138 K-3.8 Cl-105 HCO3-19* AnGap-18 ___ 08: 00PM BLOOD Calcium-9.6 Phos-2.5* Mg-1.7 ___ 08: 08PM BLOOD Lactate-1.6 K-3.6 ON DISCHARGE: ___ 05: 06AM BLOOD WBC-10.7* RBC-4.70 Hgb-15.3 Hct-43.5 MCV-93 MCH-32.6* MCHC-35.2 RDW-12.8 RDWSD-43.8 Plt ___ ___ 05: 06AM BLOOD Glucose-112* UreaN-19 Creat-1.2 Na-136 K-4.0 Cl-102 HCO3-22 AnGap-16 ___ 05: 06AM BLOOD Calcium-9.4 Phos-2.8 Mg-2.0 MICROBIOLOGY ___ 09: 05PM URINE Color-Yellow Appear-Hazy Sp ___ ___ 09: 05PM URINE Blood-TR Nitrite-POS Protein-30 Glucose-NEG Ketone-NEG Bilirub-NEG Urobiln-NEG pH-6.5 Leuks-LG ___ 09: 05PM URINE RBC-12* WBC->182* Bacteri-MANY Yeast-NONE Epi-0 ___ 9: 05 pm URINE CULTURE (Preliminary): GRAM NEGATIVE ROD(S). >100,000 ORGANISMS/ML.. ___ BLOOD CULTURES NGTD x2 OTHER STUDIES: ___ CXR: No acute cardiopulmonary abnormality. <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Amlodipine 5 mg PO DAILY 2. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 3. Fenofibrate 200 mg PO DAILY 4. Finasteride 5 mg PO QHS 5. Tamsulosin 0.4 mg PO DAILY 6. TiCAGRELOR 90 mg PO BID <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Amlodipine 5 mg PO DAILY 2. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 3. Fenofibrate 200 mg PO DAILY 4. Finasteride 5 mg PO QHS 5. Tamsulosin 0.4 mg PO DAILY 6. TiCAGRELOR 90 mg PO BID 7. Cefpodoxime Proxetil 200 mg PO Q12H RX *cefpodoxime 200 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth every twelve (12) hours Disp #*15 Tablet Refills: *0 <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> PRIMARY: Urosepsis SECONDARY: Left ICA aneurysm s/p coiling Benign prostate hypertrophy <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Dear Mr. ___, It was a pleasure taking care of you at ___. You were admitted because you were experiencing fevers and pain with urination. You were found to have a urinary tract infection, which is the likely cause of your symptoms. You were treated with an IV antibiotic with improvement in your symptoms. You will need to complete a course of oral antibiotics, please see your medication list for instructions. Your follow-up appointments with your PCP and neurosurgery are scheduled below. We wish you the best, Your ___ Care Team ", "bhc": "___ yoM with PMH notable for hx of left ICA aneurysm rupture in ___ s/p clipping, residual aneurysm s/p pipeline coiling on ___ complicated by urinary retention, BPH, HTN, HLD, and known Plavix resistance who presented with fevers and dysuria. His urinalysis was grossly positive with leukocytosis consistent with urosepsis. This was thought to be due to foley placement for 7 days due to urinary retention after procedure for aneurysm. He was treated with IV ceftriaxone with improvement in symptoms. He remained afebrile during his hospital course, and leukocytosis downtrended to 10.7 by time of discharge. His urine culture grew gram negative rods. He is to complete 10-day course of cefpodoxime for complicated UTI. CHRONIC ISSUES: =============== #Recent L ICA Coiling Angio Procedure: Procedure done on ___. Outpatient NSGY working on approving ticagrelor. He was continued on aspirin and ticagrelor as an inpatient #CKD: Pt. with creatinine of 1.3, at baseline. BUN slightly elevated likely pre-renal azotemia in the setting of sepsis ___ urinary source. He received 2L IVF in the ED. His medications were renally dosed. #BPH: Continued home tamsulosin and finasteride #HLD: Continued home fenofibrate #HTN: Continued home amlodipine TRANSITIONAL ISSUES: ==================== - New medication: Cefpodoxime 200mg q12h x8 days (last day ___ - Urine culture growing gram negative rods. Inpatient team will follow-up on speciation and sensitivities. - Patient developed skin rash ___ min after receiving IV ciprofloxacin - Please continue to work on insurance approval for ticagrelor - Code status: Full - Contact: ___ (daughter, ___ "}
{"hadm_id": "12972442-DS-58", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> Ace Inhibitors / Neurontin / atorvastatin / cefepime / vancomycin / pregabalin / fluconazole / penicillin G <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> dyspnea, ___ <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> none <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> ___ female with history of renal transplant, CKD, CAD, COPD presenting with shortness of breath. Patient reports few weeks of gradually worsening shortness of breath. Also with productive ___ And rhinorrhea. Denies fever, chills, chest pain, abdominal pain, leg pain, unusual leg swelling, history DVT/PE, recent travel, recent surgery, recent sick contacts. In the ED, initial vitals were: 98.1 72 148/82 20 100% Non-Rebreather - Exam notable for: Diffusely rhonchorous Mild tachypnea Active ___ - Labs notable for: Flu B positive Trop 0.05 Mg 1.2 HCO3 20 (gap 15) VBG 7.37/40/39 Lactate 1.4 - Imaging was notable for: CXR PA & Lat Mild pulmonary edema with small bilateral pleural effusions. - Patient was given: Duonebs x 3 Prednisone 60 mg po Levofloxacin 750 mg IV 2 mg of Mag - Vitals prior to transfer: 98.0 71 151/67 28 100% Nasal Cannula Upon arrival to the floor, patient reports she feels overall better. States that the most bothersome symptom has been ___, which started ___. It is dry. Right now her wheezing feels better. She is surprised she has the flu- the only other time she had flu she had diffuse body pains, which are not present this time. REVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Complete ROS obtained and is otherwise negative. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> - CAD: 3 vessel disease, previously recommended for CABG - CHF (LVEF 45% on ___ - Pulmonary hypertension - Chronic descending aortic dissection - ESRD s/p living related renal transplant in ___ - COPD - Hypertension - GERD - s/p TAH/BSO - s/p appendectomy - s/p ventral hernia repair in ___ <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Mother with MI at ___, father with MI at ___. No history of kidney failure. <PHYSICAL EXAM> ADMISSION PHYSICAL EXAM: -======= Vital Signs: 98.0 148/61 72 22 96RA General: Alert, oriented, no acute distress HEENT: Sclerae anicteric, MMM, oropharynx clear, EOMI, PERRL. Neck: Supple. JVP not elevated. no LAD CV: Regular rate and rhythm. Normal S1+S2, no murmurs, rubs, gallops. Lungs: incredibly wheezy and rhonchorous throughout Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, non-distended, bowel sounds present, no organomegaly, no rebound or guarding GU: No foley Ext: Warm, well perfused, 2+ pulses, no clubbing, cyanosis or edema Neuro: CNII-XII intact, ___ strength upper/lower extremities, grossly normal sensation, 2+ reflexes bilaterally, gait deferred. DISCHARGE PHYSICAL EXAM: -======= 97.6, 144 / 81, 62 18 97 Ra GENERAL: NAD, alert, interactive HEENT: NC/AT, sclerae anicteric, MMM. Oropharynx without e/o thrush LUNGS: Breathing more comfortably, inspiratory wheezes and rhonchi throughout all lung fields with poor air movement; no focal findings. HEART: RRR, nl S1 S2, no m/r/g. JVP not visible ABDOMEN: NABS, soft/ND, mild TTP over LLQ (site of kidney transplant) EXTREMITIES: WWP, no peripheral edema NEURO: awake, A&Ox3, moving all extremities <PERTINENT RESULTS> ADMISSION LABS -= ___ 07: 13PM ___ PTT-37.6* ___ ___ 07: 13PM PLT COUNT-147* ___ 07: 13PM NEUTS-69.7 LYMPHS-16.1* MONOS-13.1* EOS-0.5* BASOS-0.4 IM ___ AbsNeut-3.98 AbsLymp-0.92* AbsMono-0.75 AbsEos-0.03* AbsBaso-0.02 ___ 07: 13PM WBC-5.7 RBC-3.42* HGB-10.8* HCT-34.1 MCV-100* MCH-31.6 MCHC-31.7* RDW-16.4* RDWSD-60.2* ___ 07: 13PM CALCIUM-7.8* PHOSPHATE-4.4 MAGNESIUM-1.2* ___ 07: 13PM CK-MB-2 cTropnT-0.05* ___ 07: 13PM CK(CPK)-69 ___ 07: 13PM GLUCOSE-91 UREA N-24* CREAT-1.7* SODIUM-138 POTASSIUM-4.7 CHLORIDE-102 TOTAL CO2-20* ANION GAP-21* ___ 07: 45PM LACTATE-1.4 ___ 08: 59PM ___ PO2-39* PCO2-40 PH-7.37 TOTAL CO2-24 BASE XS--1 ___ 09: 45PM OTHER BODY FLUID FluAPCR-NEGATIVE FluBPCR-POSITIVE * STUDIES ======= CT Trachea without contrast ___ IMPRESSION: 1. Suboptimal evaluation of the lung parenchyma due to respiratory motion artifact, there is complete collapsibility of bronchus intermedius and near-complete collapse of bilateral upper lobe bronchi. 2. 38% decrease of tracheal area at the sternal notch and 62% decrease at approximately 2 cm above the carina. Persistent severe bronchomalacia with mainstem bronchi measuring less than 3 mm with near-complete collapse on expiration. However, as noted above, respiratory motion results in incomplete analysis of inspiratory and expiratory measurements. 3. Multiple new bilateral parenchymal opacities, with more confluent within the bilateral lower lobes, are concerning for aspiration with some element of mucoid impaction. 4. Stable subsolid lesion with ground-glass opacity and cystic foci, currently measuring 18 x 10 mm, previously 16 x 13 mm. Given the degree of respiratory motion artifact, these measurements are approximation. 5. Moderate-to-severe centrilobular emphysema. 6. Persistent prominence of the main pulmonary artery, measuring up to 3.1 cm. Given patient's known background moderate to severe centrilobular emphysema, this is likely a sequela of pulmonary arterial hypertension. CXR ___ IMPRESSION: Decreased small pleural effusions. No new consolidation. CXR ___ IMPRESSION: In comparison with study of ___, there is little change in the degree of enlargement of the cardiac silhouette and pulmonary edema with continued small bilateral pleural effusions and compressive atelectasis at the bases. CXR ___ IMPRESSION: Mild pulmonary edema with small bilateral pleural effusions. DISCHARGE LABS -= ___ 09: 50AM BLOOD WBC-11.2* RBC-3.39* Hgb-10.3* Hct-32.7* MCV-97 MCH-30.4 MCHC-31.5* RDW-16.2* RDWSD-58.2* Plt ___ ___ 09: 50AM BLOOD Plt ___ ___ 09: 50AM BLOOD Glucose-111* UreaN-51* Creat-1.7* Na-134 K-4.2 Cl-94* HCO3-28 AnGap-16 ___ 09: 50AM BLOOD Calcium-8.9 Phos-3.6 Mg-1.9 Tacro levels: - ___ 09: 50AM BLOOD tacroFK-10.1 ___ 09: 45AM BLOOD tacroFK-7.8 ___ 10: 00AM BLOOD tacroFK-11.9 ___ 09: 50AM BLOOD tacroFK-5.9 ___ 10: 40AM BLOOD tacroFK-6.7 ___ 09: 10AM BLOOD tacroFK-7.4 ___ 09: 08AM BLOOD tacroFK-5.4 ___ 08: 50AM BLOOD tacroFK-5.8 ___ 09: 15AM BLOOD tacroFK-5.4 ___ 06: 10AM BLOOD tacroFK-3.1* <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Acetaminophen 500 mg PO Q8H: PRN Pain - Mild 2. albuterol sulfate 90 mcg/actuation inhalation Q4H: PRN dyspnea 3. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 4. AzaTHIOprine 50 mg PO DAILY 5. Calcitriol 0.5 mcg PO DAILY 6. Carvedilol 25 mg PO BID 7. Cinacalcet 60 mg PO DAILY 8. Citalopram 20 mg PO DAILY 9. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID: PRN constipation 10. Fluticasone Propionate NASAL 1 SPRY NU BID 11. Fluticasone-Salmeterol Diskus (500/50) 1 INH IH BID 12. GuaiFENesin ER 1200 mg PO Q12H 13. Ipratropium-Albuterol Neb 1 NEB NEB Q6H 14. Isosorbide Mononitrate (Extended Release) 60 mg PO QHS 15. Lidocaine 5% Ointment 1 Appl TP DAILY feet and hands 16. Nitroglycerin SL 0.4 mg SL Q5MIN: PRN chest pain 17. Omeprazole 20 mg PO BID 18. sevelamer CARBONATE 800 mg PO TID W/MEALS 19. Tacrolimus 1.5 mg PO Q12H 20. Torsemide 20 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 21. darbepoetin alfa in polysorbat 100 mcg/0.5 mL injection monthly 22. ipratropium bromide 0.03 % nasal BID 23. miconazole nitrate 2 % topical DAILY 24. Nutricap (multivit-iron-min-folic acid) 1 mg oral DAILY 25. umeclidinium 62.5 mcg/actuation inhalation DAILY <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Benzonatate 100 mg PO TID: PRN ___ 2. Nystatin Oral Suspension 5 mL PO QID 3. PredniSONE 40 mg PO DAILY Duration: 2 Days take through ___. PredniSONE 20 mg PO DAILY Duration: 3 Days take ___ 5. PredniSONE 10 mg PO DAILY Duration: 3 Days take ___ 6. PredniSONE 5 mg PO DAILY Duration: 3 Days take ___ - ___ 7. Tacrolimus 1 mg PO Q12H 8. Acetaminophen 500 mg PO Q8H: PRN Pain - Mild 9. albuterol sulfate 90 mcg/actuation inhalation Q4H: PRN dyspnea 10. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 11. AzaTHIOprine 50 mg PO DAILY 12. Calcitriol 0.5 mcg PO DAILY 13. Carvedilol 25 mg PO BID 14. Cinacalcet 60 mg PO DAILY 15. Citalopram 20 mg PO DAILY 16. darbepoetin alfa in polysorbat 100 mcg/0.5 mL injection monthly 17. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID: PRN constipation 18. Fluticasone Propionate NASAL 1 SPRY NU BID 19. Fluticasone-Salmeterol Diskus (500/50) 1 INH IH BID 20. GuaiFENesin ER 1200 mg PO Q12H 21. ipratropium bromide 0.03 % nasal BID 22. Isosorbide Mononitrate (Extended Release) 60 mg PO QHS 23. Lidocaine 5% Ointment 1 Appl TP DAILY feet and hands 24. miconazole nitrate 2 % topical DAILY 25. Nitroglycerin SL 0.4 mg SL Q5MIN: PRN chest pain 26. Nutricap (multivit-iron-min-folic acid) 1 mg oral DAILY 27. Omeprazole 20 mg PO BID 28. sevelamer CARBONATE 800 mg PO TID W/MEALS 29. Torsemide 20 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 30. umeclidinium 62.5 mcg/actuation inhalation DAILY 31.Outpatient Lab Work N18.6 End Stage Renal Disease please draw tacrolimus trough level and Chem 10 on ___. Fax results to ___ Nephrology Attn: Dr. ___ ___ <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Extended Care Facility: ___ <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Primary Diagnosis Influenza COPD Exacerbation Secondary Diagnosis Tracheobronchomalacia Thrush Hypertension Diabetes Depression GERD End stage renal disease s/p renal transplant <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Out of Bed with assistance to chair or wheelchair. <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Dear Ms. ___, It was a pleasure taking care of you during your hospitalization. Briefly, you were hospitalized with the flu. You were treated for the flu and for an exacerbation of your COPD. You were given steroids and your breathing improved. You will require a stay in rehab at ___ to help with your breathing and improve your strength. We wish you the best, Your ___ Treatment Team Weigh yourself every morning, call MD if weight goes up more than 3 lbs. ", "bhc": "___ with hx of COPD, ESRD s/p renal transplant, CAD p/w dyspnea and ___ and a positive influenza B swab. Thought to be a COPD exacerbation due to influenza B infection in the context of immunosuppression, with some component of mild pulmonary edema. During her hospital stay, she also developed oral thrush with associated sore throat. She has now completed treatment for influenza B and her respiratory status is stable. Her oral thrush and sore throat have resolved. TRANSITIONAL ISSUES =================== - Please draw tacrolimus trough level and Chem 10 on ___. Fax results to ___ Nephrology Attn: Dr. ___ ___ - Anticipate rehabilitation stay to be less than 30 days - Needs ongoing chest ___ to help bring up mucus - Prednisone taper outline in post discharge medication list. Will start ___: 40 x3 days, 20 x3 days, 10 x3 days, and 5 x3 days - Recommend aggressive mucolytic therapy with guiafenasin and chest ___ BID - Recommend humidified oxygen to help thin secretions. - Continue Nystatin for 14 days to end on ___ - tacro dose changed to 1 mg BID on ___ -CODE: Full (confirmed) -CONTACT: ___ (granddaughter, HCP) ___ # ___, influenza B, COPD: On arrival, Ms. ___ was mildly tachypneic, with a supplemental O2 requirement and dyspnea. She was soon transitioned to room air after receiving prednisone and duonebs. Over the course of her hospital stay,she received a 5-day course of Tamiflu and 5-day course of prednisone 40 to treat her influenza B and COPD exacerbation. She began on duonebs q4h, which were weaned to ipratropium nebs q6h and albuterol nebs PRN. She continued to receive her home Advair. When she continued to have some dyspnea and wheezing with occasional oxygen requirement after receiving this treatment, we re-started predisone 60 with a planned wean of 60 x3 days, 40 x3 days, 20 x3 days, 10 x3 days, and 5 x3 days. We also performed a CT chest, which showed significant retained mucus in the lungs and potential aspiration. We started chest ___, humidified air and standing guanfesin. Her respiratory status is now stable, requiring albuterol nebs a few times a day in addition to her home medications. # CHF with reduced EF: EF 45% per ___ echo. No visible JVD or peripheral edema. Estimated dry weight 123 lbs. Recorded weights were feared to be inaccurate with scale malfunction. Received torsemide ___ and ___. Plan to continue 20 mg QoD starting ___. continued home carvedilol, ASA, isosorbide mononitrate. # Sore throat, thrush: Treated with nystatin, plaques resolved by day of discharged. Plan total 14 day course of nystatin. # Hyponatremia: Thought to be hypovolemic in setting of over-diuresis. Improved with optimization of volume status. # ESRD s/p LRRT: Cr at baseline. Followed by Renal. Tacro dose changed to 1 mg BID as level >10 on ___. Continued azathioprine, cinacalcet, sevelamir, calcitriol. Planning to have Chem 10 and tacro level drawn on ___ as above. # CAD: new T-wave inversion in aVF on EKG and slightly elevated trop on admission; now normalized. Pt denied chest pain, palpitations. Elevated trop most likely in presence of known CAD and poor renal function. continued home carvedilol, ASA, isosorbide mononitrate #Tracheobronchomalacia: severe appearance on CT imaging. thought to have no indication for intervention after discussion with IP. Chronic issues: #GERD: cont'd Omeprazole BID #RLL lung lesion: Scheduled for repeat CT Chest ___ to eval progression. #Anemia of chronic disease: On Aranesp, last injection ___ #Depression: cont'd citalopram #HTN: Coreg, Imdur as above "}
{"hadm_id": "19960839-DS-17", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> No Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> leukocytosis (bandemia) and confusion <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> None <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> A ___ male with a PMH of polycythemia ___ and frontal dementia was transferred from a nursing home. Because pt was quite confusion, I was unable to obtain a history from him. All history came from the chart. He was transferred to our ED because of combative behavior in the nursing home. In the meantime, he was found to have high WBC counts (59.6) with band cells (5%). He also complained of left jaw pain for the past one week. He stated that he was hit by a lady. I discussed with ___ attending, who would like to r/o infection first. She would like hold hydroxyurea tonight. Review of Systems: (+) Per HPI (-) Review of Systems: No fever, chill, weight loss. HEENT: No headache, sinus tenderness, rhinorrhea or congestion. CV: No chest tightness, palpitations. GI: No diarrhea. No recent change in bowel habits, no hematochezia or melena. GUI: No dysuria or change in bladder habits. MSK: No arthritis, arthralgias, or myalgias. NEURO: No numbness/tingling in extremities. PSYCH: No feelings of depression or anxiety. All other review of systems negative. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> CHF, EF >55% polycythemia ___, now myelofibrosis, on anagralide Vascular disease s/p R ___ digit osteo treated with 6 week unasyn leg ulcers on hydrea TIA ___, did not tolerate aggrenox due to burising CKD, stage III IBS, intermit diarrhea frontal dementia anxiety depression osteopenia HTN <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> No family history of mental illness <PHYSICAL EXAM> VS: 96 166/74 66 18 97%RA GEN: disoriented and confusion HEENT: PERRLA. MMM. no LAD. no JVD. neck supple. No cervical LAD. No oral lesions, no OP erythema Cards: RR S1/S2 normal. Cannot appreciate murmur, no gallop/rub Pulm: No dullness to percussion, CTAB no crackles or wheezes Abd: BS+, soft, NT, no rebound/guarding, no HSM Extremities: wwp, no edema. DPs, PTs 2+. Skin: he had multiple wounds in his extremities, which was wrapped by dry gauze Neuro: unable to exam. <PERTINENT RESULTS> ___ 07: 45PM BLOOD WBC-53.7*# RBC-3.23* Hgb-9.8* Hct-31.9* MCV-99* MCH-30.3 MCHC-30.6* RDW-22.3* Plt ___ ___ 07: 45PM BLOOD Neuts-92* Bands-2 Lymphs-2* Monos-2 Eos-1 Baso-1 ___ Myelos-0 ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD WBC-51.0* RBC-3.13* Hgb-9.6* Hct-31.9* MCV-102* MCH-30.7 MCHC-30.2* RDW-21.3* Plt ___ ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD Neuts-94.0* Lymphs-3.6* Monos-1.8* Eos-0.3 Baso-0.3 ___ 01: 00PM BLOOD WBC-49.1* RBC-3.32* Hgb-10.1* Hct-33.0* MCV-100* MCH-30.5 MCHC-30.7* RDW-21.7* Plt ___ ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD WBC-41.5* RBC-2.97* Hgb-9.3* Hct-28.9* MCV-97 MCH-31.3 MCHC-32.2 RDW-20.5* Plt ___ ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD Neuts-92.9* Lymphs-4.6* Monos-1.8* Eos-0.4 Baso-0.3 ___ 05: 25AM BLOOD WBC-41.4* RBC-2.99* Hgb-9.2* Hct-28.8* MCV-97 MCH-31.0 MCHC-32.1 RDW-20.4* Plt ___ ___ 05: 25AM BLOOD Neuts-93.3* Lymphs-4.7* Monos-1.3* Eos-0.3 Baso-0.3 ___ 07: 45PM BLOOD Hypochr-2+ Anisocy-3+ Poiklo-2+ Macrocy-1+ Microcy-NORMAL Polychr-1+ Ovalocy-1+ Stipple-1+ Tear ___ ___ 08: 21PM BLOOD ___ PTT-31.1 ___ ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD ___ PTT-34.0 ___ ___ 07: 45PM BLOOD Glucose-107* UreaN-35* Creat-1.3* Na-140 K-4.1 Cl-105 HCO3-24 AnGap-15 ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD Glucose-48* UreaN-35* Creat-1.2 Na-140 K-3.2* Cl-105 HCO3-27 AnGap-11 ___ 07: 45PM BLOOD ALT-33 AST-42* AlkPhos-203* TotBili-0.2 ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD ALT-34 AST-34 LD(LDH)-375* AlkPhos-251* TotBili-0.2 ___ 07: 45PM BLOOD Calcium-8.2* Phos-3.1 Mg-2.0 ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD Albumin-2.9* Calcium-7.7* Phos-3.0 Mg-1.9 UricAcd-6.3 ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD TSH-6.1* ___ 01: 00PM BLOOD T4-4.5* T3-70* calcTBG-0.90 TUptake-1.11 T4Index-5.0 Free T4-1.0 ___ head ct without contrast: 1. No acute intracranial abnormality. 2. Degenerative changes in the temporomandibular joint on the left. ___ cxr pa/lateral: 1. Blunting of the right costophrenic angle may represent a right pleural effusion. 2. Hyperinflated lungs consistent with COPD with no focal areas of consolidation. ___ ecg: Sinus rhythm. Low limb lead voltage. Compared to the previous tracing of ___ there is no change. <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> allopurinol ___ mg daily Lisinopril 5mg daily metoprolol tartrate 50 mg daily LASIX 40MG DIALY doxazosin 1mg QHS sertraline 50mg daily Anagrelide 0.5 mg Oral Capsule Bid Buspar 5mg daily FINASTERIDE 5 MG daily folic acid 1mg daily <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. allopurinol ___ mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY (Daily). 2. lisinopril 5 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO once a day. 3. metoprolol tartrate 50 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY (Daily). 4. furosemide 40 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY (Daily). 5. doxazosin 1 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO HS (at bedtime). 6. sertraline 50 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY (Daily). 7. anagrelide 0.5 mg Capsule Sig: Three (3) Capsule PO bid (). 8. buspirone 10 mg Tablet Sig: 0.5 Tablet PO DAILY (Daily). 9. finasteride 5 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY (Daily). 10. folic acid 1 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY (Daily). 11. aspirin 81 mg Tablet, Chewable Sig: One (1) Tablet, Chewable PO DAILY (Daily). 12. acetaminophen 500 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO TID (3 times a day) for 1 weeks: then as needed after 1 week. <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Extended Care Facility: ___ <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> polycythemia ___ ___ Condition: Mental Status: Confused - always. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - requires assistance or aid (walker or cane). <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> You had very high WBC because of the polycythemia ___. You were treated with Hydroxyurea and seen by Oncology. your WBC improved to 40. Also, you were thought initially to have abnormal white cells (immature forms called bands) in your blood but this turned out not to be the case. You had no clear infection. ", "bhc": "This is a ___ man with polycythemia ___ and frontal dementia who was transferred from a nursing home because of leukocytosis and confusion. he was noted to have a bandemia at his nursing facility however on repeat on arrival to ___ had 2% bands. His WBC was 53 which is close to his baseline per his oncologist in the 40-50 range. he was treated with three days of hydroxyurea, his WBC on discharge was 41 with 0% bands. Prolonged treatment with hydroxyurea can significantly worsen his wounds and therefore it has been used sparingly. He was initially thought to have rec'd a incorrect dose of anegralide at the nursing facility but after further review it seems he was getting the correct dose. His confusion improved with discontinuation of oxycodone. Tylenol was used for pain for left TMJ arthritis. He should follow up with ENT should this continue despite tylenol standing for the next week. He is CKD III so ibuprofen was not attempted. Tramadol was attempted but he seemed to still be confused with this medication. Mental status was at baseline on discharge. He claimed trauma to the jaw from an assault at his NH but this could not be verified because of his frontal dementia and confusion. His CT of his head seemed to suggest this was more of a degenerative change. Wound consult was initiated and his wounds were noted to be advanced but not infected, recommendations are included in the page 1. "}
{"hadm_id": "14711846-DS-15", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> Gold Salts / Penicillins / Remicade / Erythromycin Base <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> shortness of breath <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> None <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> Ms. ___ is a ___ yoF with a PMHx of HTN, RA (on prednisone/methotrexate/orencia) who presented with 1 day hx of shortness of breath. She as in her usual state of health until the morning of ___ when she awoke for no known reason at 4AM. After moving to the couch, she noted dyspnea, which progressively worsened throughout the day. Her SOB was associated with a dry cough and malaise. In addition to SOB, she noted a sharp and pleuritic shoulder pain at the R AC joint, which lasted for several hours and resolved spontaneously. The pain was ___, non-radiating, not worsened with motion, exacerbated by deep breathing and occurred and not limiting her range of motion. She reported a feeling of dizziness on ambulating to and from the cough and one episode of vomiting (non-bloody, non-bilious). She Her measured temp at home was 97. She denies fevers, chills, myalgias, recent trauma, night sweats, headache, vision changes, rhinorrhea, congestion, sore throat, abdominal pain, diarrhea, constipation, BRBPR, melena, hematochezia, dysuria, or hematuria. She presented to ___ where VS were T98.3, HR 79, 117/60, 13, SpO2 96% on RA, 93% with ambulation. CXR demonstrated a diffuse pulmonary infiltrate with RUL and RLL areas of consolidation, and Ms. ___ was given 750mg IV levofloxacin for presumed CAP. She was transferred to ___ for admission given lack of bed availability at ___. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> PAST MEDICAL & SURGICAL HISTORY: Community Acquired Pneumonia in ___ (RUL, sp cefpodoxime and azithro) Rheumatoid arthritis (dx'd ___ prior) Osteoarthritis Depression Cataracts Cerebral aneurysm, s/p removal ___ yrs ago Surg Hx: Cerebral aneurism removal ___ yrs ago s/p R THR s/p L shoulder arthroplasty s/p cerical facet injection <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Father died of pneumonia in ___ Mother died of Lung Cancer at age ___. <PHYSICAL EXAM> Admission Physical: VITALS: 97.8 BP 127/63, 70, 20, 96% 2L NC, No orthostasis GENERAL: NAD, well-composed, talkative and pleasant HEENT: PERRL, EOMI, dry MM, no conj injection, no oropharyngeal erythema, swelling or lesions NECK: No JVD, LAD, thyromegally, or carotid bruits LUNGS: Crackles diffuse in b/l lung fields HEART: RRR, normal S1 S2, ___ systolic ejection murmur in RUSB ABDOMEN: Soft, NT, NABS, no organomegaly EXTREMITIES: ulnar deviation, swan deformity c/w RA, no ___ edema NEUROLOGIC: A+OX3, CNII-XII intact, Romberg negative, gait normal, strength ___ diffusely, sensation intactto LT throughout. Discharge Physical: O: VS: 98.4/97.5, 138/75 (116-134/56-75), 66 (66-78), 96% on RA; ___ 2500/2975 GENERAL: NAD, well-composed, talkative and pleasant HEENT: PERRL, EOMI, MMM, no conj injection, no oropharyngeal erythema, swelling or lesions NECK: No JVD, LAD, thyromegally, or carotid bruits LUNGS: Crackles diffuse in b/l lung fields - persistent HEART: RRR, normal S1 S2, ___ systolic ejection murmur in RUSB ABDOMEN: Soft, NT, NABS, no organomegaly EXTREMITIES: ulnar deviation, swan deformity c/w RA, very trace ___ edema (ankle) NEUROLOGIC: A+OX3, CNII-XII intact, strength ___ diffusely, sensation intactto LT throughout. <PERTINENT RESULTS> As per BI-N: WBC 20.2 with leukocytic predominance (PMN 91%) and hyponatremia with Na of 132. H/H was 11.5/35 Admissions Labs: ___ 07: 00AM BLOOD WBC-16.3*# RBC-3.45* Hgb-10.9* Hct-34.2*# MCV-99*# MCH-31.7 MCHC-32.0 RDW-15.1 Plt ___ ___ 07: 00AM BLOOD Neuts-90.4* Lymphs-5.3* Monos-2.2 Eos-1.6 Baso-0.4 ___ 07: 00AM BLOOD Glucose-84 UreaN-10 Creat-0.5 Na-144 K-4.6 Cl-112* HCO3-27 AnGap-10 ___ 07: 00AM BLOOD LD(LDH)-169 ___ 07: 00AM BLOOD Calcium-7.9* Phos-2.1* Mg-1.9 ___ 07: 00AM BLOOD VitB12-366 Folate-15.6 Discharge Labs: ___ 07: 35AM BLOOD WBC-9.5 RBC-3.73* Hgb-11.8* Hct-36.9 MCV-99* MCH-31.5 MCHC-31.9 RDW-14.8 Plt ___ ___ 07: 35AM BLOOD Neuts-75.0* Lymphs-17.2* Monos-3.8 Eos-3.2 Baso-0.8 ___ 07: 35AM BLOOD Glucose-84 UreaN-13 Creat-0.7 Na-140 K-4.5 Cl-100 HCO3-31 AnGap-14 ___ 07: 35AM BLOOD ALT-15 AST-16 AlkPhos-54 ___ 07: 35AM BLOOD Calcium-9.2 Phos-3.1 Mg-1.9 ___ 07: 35AM BLOOD TSH-2.8 Microbiology: ___: Urine Legionella Ag: NEGATIVE FOR LEGIONELLA SEROGROUP 1 ANTIGEN. Blood cultures: ___: Pending Echo ___: The left atrium is elongated. There is mild symmetric left ventricular hypertrophy with normal cavity size. Regional left ventricular wall motion is normal. Overall left ventricular systolic function is normal (LVEF>55%). The estimated cardiac index is normal (>=2.5L/min/m2). Doppler parameters are indeterminate for left ventricular diastolic function. There is no left ventricular outflow obstruction at rest or with Valsalva. Right ventricular chamber size and free wall motion are normal. The diameters of aorta at the sinus, ascending and arch levels are normal. The aortic valve leaflets (3) are mildly thickened but aortic stenosis is not present. No aortic regurgitation is seen. The mitral valve leaflets are mildly thickened. There is no mitral valve prolapse. The tricuspid valve leaflets are mildly thickened. There is borderline pulmonary artery systolic hypertension. There is no pericardial effusion. IMPRESSION: Mild symmetric left ventricular hypertrophy with preserved global and regional biventricular systolic function. Borderline elevated pulmonary artery systolic hypertension. EKG ___: Sinus rhythm. Borderline left axis deviation. Compared to the previous tracing of ___ no significant change. CXR from OSH ___ - ___: There is an ill-defined parenchymal infiltrate involving the posterior aspects of both the right upper lobe and the right lower lobe. There is also slight increased density centrally in the left lung. The pulmonary vasculature, which was difficult to evaluate because of the widespread parenchymal changes, is prominent compatible with acute congestive failure. The cardiac size and configuration are within normal limits. There is a hiatal hernia present, as before. IMPRESSION: CHANGES COMPATIBLE WITH EXTENSIVE BRONCHOPNEUMONIC INFILTRATES POSTERIORLY IN THE RIGHT LUNG WITH ACCOMPANYING CHANGES OF ACUTE CONGESTIVE FAILURE. <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> Preadmission medications listed are correct and complete. Information was obtained from Patient list. 1. Nabumetone 750 mg PO BID 2. Omeprazole 10 mg PO BID 3. Orencia *NF* (abatacept;<br>abatacept (with maltose)) 750 mg Subcutaneous monthly 4. Leucovorin Calcium 10 mg IV QWEEKLY 5. Methotrexate 17 mg PO 1X/WEEK (MO) 6. PredniSONE 8 mg PO DAILY 7. BusPIRone 45 mg PO QAM 8. Gabapentin 900 mg PO TID 9. Escitalopram Oxalate 30 mg PO DAILY 10. Nortriptyline 20 mg PO HS 11. Lisinopril 5 mg PO DAILY 12. Hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg PO DAILY hold for sbp < 90, 13. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 14. Hydrocodone-Acetaminophen (5mg-500mg ___ TAB PO BID: PRN PAIN, QOD pain 15. Acetaminophen w/Codeine ___ TAB PO Q6H: PRN pain hold for sedation, rr < 12 16. Estrogens Conjugated 0.3 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 17. Estrogens Conjugated 0.625 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 18. MedroxyPROGESTERone Acetate 2.5 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 19. MedroxyPROGESTERone Acetate 5 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 20. Methocarbamol 750 mg PO TID: PRN pain 21. Rhinocort Aqua *NF* (budesonide) 32 mcg/actuation NU 2 sprays daily 22. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 23. Ferrous Sulfate 325 mg PO DAILY 24. Calcium Carbonate 1000 mg PO DAILY 25. Vitamin D 800 UNIT PO DAILY 26. BusPIRone 15 mg PO AT NOON 27. BusPIRone 15 mg PO HS <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Acetaminophen w/Codeine ___ TAB PO Q6H: PRN pain hold for sedation, rr < 12 2. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 3. BusPIRone 45 mg PO QAM 4. BusPIRone 15 mg PO AT NOON 5. BusPIRone 15 mg PO HS 6. Calcium Carbonate 1000 mg PO DAILY 7. Escitalopram Oxalate 30 mg PO DAILY 8. Estrogens Conjugated 0.3 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 9. Estrogens Conjugated 0.625 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 10. Ferrous Sulfate 325 mg PO DAILY 11. Gabapentin 900 mg PO TID 12. Hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg PO DAILY hold for sbp < 90, 13. Hydrocodone-Acetaminophen (5mg-500mg ___ TAB PO BID: PRN PAIN, QOD pain 14. Lisinopril 5 mg PO DAILY 15. MedroxyPROGESTERone Acetate 2.5 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 16. MedroxyPROGESTERone Acetate 5 mg PO EVERY OTHER DAY 17. Methocarbamol 750 mg PO TID: PRN pain 18. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 19. Nabumetone 750 mg PO BID 20. Nortriptyline 20 mg PO HS 21. Omeprazole 10 mg PO BID 22. PredniSONE 8 mg PO DAILY 23. Vitamin D 800 UNIT PO DAILY 24. Levofloxacin 750 mg PO DAILY RX *levofloxacin 750 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth daily Disp #*3 Tablet Refills: *0 25. Leucovorin Calcium 10 mg IV QWEEKLY 26. Methotrexate 17 mg PO 1X/WEEK (MO) 27. Orencia *NF* (abatacept;<br>abatacept (with maltose)) 750 mg Subcutaneous monthly 28. Rhinocort Aqua *NF* (budesonide) 32 mcg/actuation NU 2 sprays daily <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home With Service Facility: ___ <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Primary: Pneumonia Secondary: Rheumatoid arthritis, pulmonary edema <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Dear Ms. ___, It was a pleasure to take care of you at ___. You were admitted for shortness of breath and was found to have crackles in your lungs. You were found to have a high white cell count and X-ray findings consistent with pneumonia and/or pulmonary edema. You received antibiotics for your pneumonia and your white count fell. You received Lasix for diuresis and your breathing and oxygen saturation improved. Again, it was a pleasure to take care of you at ___. ", "bhc": "Ms. ___ was admitted for diagnosis and treatment of her shortness of breath. She remained afebrile. Outside records were obtained from ___ and CXR showed evidence of possible pulm edema. # Shortness of breath: DDx was broad, given hx of RA and immunosuppresion. Pleuritic chest pain was thought to be radiation from lung process (given negative extremity exam). CAP was considered most likely etiology of SOB. Less likely etiologies included: legionella (esp considering hyponatremia although urine ag negative)), TB (no hx of exposure, prior PPD negative). She was also treated for pulm edema suggested by CXR (however, echo was not consistent with CHF and EKG not suggestive of MI/ACS as cause of CHF). Her leukocytosis resolved (16.3-->10.8) on Levofloxacin. Pt also rec'd Lasix w good urine output. Crackles improved on hospital day 3. - Levofloxacin to 750mg PO per day for CAP (___) Inactive Issues: #Macrocytosis Likely ___ MTX therapy. B12 and folate wnl. # RA: Followed by rheumatologist. On prednisone, orencia, relafen, leukovorin, and methotrexate and vicodin, robaxin, and tylenol 3 for pain. # Post-menopausal symptoms On medroxyprogesterone and estrogen # Depression On lexapro, gabapentin, nortryptiline, and buspirone # HTN On lisinopril and hctz # Health maintenance - continue ASA, Fe, MVI Transitional Issues: - Consider CT after resolution of infectious process given likely recurrence of PNA in R lobe (post-obstructive PNA possible). - Pls ___ blood cx from ___ (negative as of ___ - Pt on Levofloxacin until ___. Pls confirm that full course completed. "}
{"hadm_id": "18477272-DS-18", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> OBSTETRICS/GYNECOLOGY <ALLERGIES> Penicillins / Bactrim <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Menorrhagia <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> Diagnostic laparoscopy, total vaginal hysterectomy, cystoscopy <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> Ms ___ is a ___ yo presented with menorrhagia. Since about ___ ___ has had increasingly erratic and heavy bleeding. Underwent HSC, D&C, and Mirena placement in ___ which initially was well tolerated. HSC and US showed normal uterus, no fibroids or polyps. Pattern seemingly perimenopausal. Unfortuantely she resumed heavy irregular bleeding and found the side effects of Mirena intolerable, including mood change and terrible bloating and breast tenderness. Discussed other medical options and ablation and she prefers definitive surgical management. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> - Anxiety states panic disorder - Murmur nl echo, no SBE prophylaxis - Anemia, vitamin B12 deficiency - Headache, migraine occasional aura, N/V - Irritable bowel syndrome - Hypercholesterolemia Past Surgical History: - Tonsillectomy - Delivery - cesarean sect, delivery only C Sect, Delivery Only - Past surgical history of: ___ sinus surgery - Past surgical history of: ___ right toe surgery - Hysteroscopy - endomet bx &/or polypectomy ___ - Insert - iud ___ OB history: ___ Para Term Preterm Abortions TAB SAB Ect Mult Living ___ 3 CS x 1, VBAC x 2. Largest was 7.5 lbs. GYN History Menses irregular and Heavy Bleeding: dysfunctional uterine bleeding Contraception: tubal ligation ___: unknown STDs: no past history Pap history normal paps. Last was ___ Incontinence symptoms urge only. No stress incontinence. Prolapse symptoms none Menopause none <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> - Breast Cancer Mother dx'___ in ___; reoccurred in ___, died age ___ - Seizures/Convulsions Brother died week after ___ birthday <PHYSICAL EXAM> Physical Examination on Discharge: VSS Gen: NAD, comfortable CV: RRR Pulm: CTAB Abd: soft, nondistended, nontender, + BS Ext: warm well pefused, nontender <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Paroxetine 40 mg PO DAILY 2. ClonazePAM 0.5 mg PO DAILY 3. Cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg IM/SC QWEEK X 4 WEEKS THEN QMONTH 4. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 5. Ibuprofen Dose is Unknown PO Frequency is Unknown <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Ibuprofen 600 mg PO Q6H: PRN pain RX *ibuprofen 600 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth every 6 hours Disp #*60 Tablet Refills: *0 2. Paroxetine 40 mg PO DAILY 3. Acetaminophen 1000 mg PO Q6H: PRN pain 4. ClonazePAM 0.5 mg PO DAILY 5. Cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg IM/SC QWEEK X 4 WEEKS THEN QMONTH 6. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY 7. Hydrocodone-Acetaminophen (5mg-500mg) ___ TAB PO Q6H: PRN pain RX *hydrocodone-acetaminophen 5 mg-500 mg ___ tablet(s) by mouth q6 hours Disp #*50 Tablet Refills: *0 8. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID: PRN constipation RX *docusate sodium 100 mg 1 capsule(s) by mouth twice a day Disp #*60 Capsule Refills: *2 <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Menorrhagia <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Ms. ___, You were admitted to the GYN service after undergoing the procedures listed below. The team feels that you have recovered well and are ready for discharge. Please follow the below instructions: General instructions: * Take your medications as prescribed. * Do not drive while taking narcotics. * Take a stool softener such as colace while taking narcotics to prevent constipation * Do not combine narcotic and sedative medications or alcohol * Do not take more than 4000mg acetaminophen (APAP) in 24 hrs * No strenuous activity until your post-op appointment * Nothing in the vagina (no tampons, no douching, no sex) for 6 weeks * No heavy lifting of objects >10lbs for 6 weeks. * You may eat a regular diet Incision care: * You may shower and allow soapy water to run over incision; no scrubbing of incision. No bath tubs for 6 weeks. * If you have steri-strips, leave them on. They will fall off on their own or be removed during your followup visit. To reach medical records to get the records from this hospitalization sent to your doctor at home, call ___. ", "bhc": "On ___, Ms. ___ was admitted to the gynecology service after undergoing diagnostic laparoscopy, total vaginal hysterectomy, cystoscopy. Please see the operative report for full details. Her post-operative course was uncomplicated. Immediately post-op, her pain was controlled with dilaudid and toradol. On post-operative day 1, her urine output was adequate so her foley was removed and she voided spontaneously. Her diet was advanced without difficulty and she was transitioned to vicodin, ibuprofen. By post-operative day 1, she was tolerating a regular diet, voiding spontaneously, ambulating independently, and pain was controlled with oral medications. She was then discharged home in stable condition with outpatient follow-up scheduled. "}
{"hadm_id": "18150344-DS-16", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> ___ <ATTENDING> ___ <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Bright red blood per rectum . Date/time of initial encounter: ___ 20: 15 <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> none <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> ___ F h/o colon ca, breast ca who presents with bright red blood per rectum. The patient had 3 episodes between 8am and 10am. The amount was enough to turn the toilet water red. She has not had any bleeding since then. She denies any abd pain or n/v during these episodes. She does however report the she had a 15 min episode of crampy abd pain last night that was relieved by a large non-bloody, non-melanotis loose brown BM. Denies f/c, dizziness/lightheadedness. Reports having one preivous episode of self limited bleeding (only once) in ___ of this year during a trip to ___. The patient has been followed by Dr. ___ ___ at ___ and her last colonoscopy was ___ years ago. . ROS: -Constitutional: [X]WNL []Weight loss []Fatigue/Malaise []Fever []Chills/Rigors []Nightweats []Anorexia -Eyes: [X]WNL []Blurry Vision []Diplopia []Loss of Vision []Photophobia -ENT: [X]WNL []Dry Mouth []Oral ulcers []Bleeding gums/nose []Tinnitus []Sinus pain []Sore throat -Cardiac: [X]WNL []Chest pain []Palpitations ___ edema []Orthopnea/PND []DOE -Respiratory: [X]WNL []SOB []Pleuritic pain []Hemoptysis []Cough -Gastrointestinal: []WNL []Nausea []Vomiting []Abdominal pain []Abdominal Swelling [X]Diarrhea []Constipation []Hematemesis []Hematochezia []Melena -Heme/Lymph: [X]WNL []Bleeding []Bruising []Lymphadenopathy -GU: [X]WNL []Incontinence/Retention []Dysuria []Hematuria []DIscharge []Menorrhagia -Skin: [X]WNL []Rash []Pruritus -Endocrine: [X]WNL []Change in skin/hair []Loss of energy []Heat/Cold intolerance -Musculoskeletal: [X]WNL []Myalgias []Arthralgias []Back pain -Neurological: [X] WNL []Numbness of extremities []Weakness of extremities []Parasthesias []Dizziness/Lightheaded []Vertigo []Confusion []Headache -Psychiatric: [X]WNL []Depression []Suicidal Ideation -Allergy/Immunological: [] WNL [X]Seasonal Allergies . <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> Colon cancer s/p resection Breast cancer s/p bilateral mastectomy Osteoporosis <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Notable for diabetes. No known family history of cancer. <PHYSICAL EXAM> <PHYSICAL EXAM> Appearance: NAD Vitals: T: 96.7 BP: 157/96 HR: 78 RR: 18 O2: 99 % RA Eyes: EOMI, PERRL, conjunctiva clear, noninjected, anicteric, no exudate ENT: Moist Neck: No JVD, no LAD, no thyromegaly, no carotid bruits Cardiovascular: RRR, nl S1/S2, no m/r/g Respiratory: CTA bilaterally, comfortable, no wheezing, no ronchi, no rales Gastrointestinal: soft, non-tender, non-distended, no hepatosplenomegaly, normal bowel sounds Musculoskeletal/Extremities: no clubbing, no cyanosis, no joint swelling, no edema in the bilateral extremities Neurological: Alert and oriented x3, fluent speech, no pronator drift, no asterixis, sensation WNL, CNII-XII intact Integument: warm, no rash, no ulcer Psychiatric: appropriate, pleasant Hematological/Lymphatic: No cervical, supraclavicular, axillary, or inguinal lymphadenopathy . <PERTINENT RESULTS> ___ 01: 00PM WBC-7.5 RBC-4.41 HGB-14.1 HCT-41.8 MCV-95 MCH-32.1* MCHC-33.9 RDW-13.1 ___ 01: 00PM NEUTS-75.8* ___ MONOS-3.0 EOS-1.4 BASOS-0.3 ___ 01: 00PM PLT COUNT-276 ___ 01: 00PM ___ PTT-27.1 ___ ___ 01: 00PM GLUCOSE-108* UREA N-17 CREAT-0.9 SODIUM-139 POTASSIUM-4.8 CHLORIDE-103 TOTAL CO2-23 ANION GAP-18 ___ 01: 00PM cTropnT-<0.01 ___ 01: 00PM CK-MB-3 ___ 01: 04PM HGB-14.8 calcHCT-44 . <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> -ALBUTEROL SULFATE [VENTOLIN] - 90 mcg/Actuation Aerosol - 2 puffs po q6h as needed for prn -ALENDRONATE - 70 mg Tablet - 1 Tablet(s) by mouth once a week -DOXYCYCLINE HYCLATE - (Prescribed by Other Provider) - 100 mg Capsule - 1 Capsule(s) by mouth BID for 21 days begun ___ -IMIPRAMINE HCL - 10 mg Tablet - 2 Tablet(s) by mouth at bedtime -LORAZEPAM - 0.5 mg Tablet - ___ Tablet(s) by mouth qam and 1 po qpm as needed for prn -PREDNISONE - 1 mg Tablet - 5 Tablet(s) by mouth qd X 2 weeks, then 4 po qd X 2 weeks, then 3 po qd X 2 weeks, then 2 po qd X 2 weeks, then 1 po qd X 2 weeks, then off -CALCIUM-VITAMIN D3-VITAMIN K - (Prescribed by Other Provider) -500 mg-200 unit-40 mcg Tablet, Chewable - 1 Tablet(s) by mouth twice a day -CHOLECALCIFEROL (VITAMIN D3) [VITAMIN D-3] - (OTC) - 2,000 unit Tablet - 1 Tablet(s) by mouth once a day <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Doxycycline Hyclate 100 mg Capsule Sig: One (1) Capsule PO Q12H (every 12 hours). 2. Prednisone 5 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY (Daily): (resume your taper). 3. Imipramine HCl 10 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO HS (at bedtime). 4. Albuterol Sulfate 90 mcg/Actuation HFA Aerosol Inhaler Sig: Two (2) Inhalation every six (6) hours as needed for shortness of breath or wheezing. 5. Alendronate 70 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO once a week. 6. Lorazepam 0.5 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO HS (at bedtime) as needed for INSOMNIA. 7. Calcium-Vitamin D3-Vitamin K 500-200-40 mg-unit-mcg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO twice a day. 8. Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) 2,000 unit Capsule Sig: One (1) Capsule PO once a day. <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> # Lower GI bleed - resolved # h/o colon cancer # asthma # lyme disease (presumed based on your deer tick bite) <DISCHARGE CONDITION> stable <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> You were admitted to observe your noted prior bright blood from your rectum - as you know you did not re-bleed here - your blood counts remained stable - and your volume status was stable at time of discharge (us checking your blood pressures while standing up). <br> Please resume all your prior home medications INCLUDING your doxycycline for your presumed lyme disease treatment - AND importantly call your gastroenterologist immediately as below to set-up a follow-up evaluation. <br> If your bleeding re-starts - please call your provider ___ gastroenterologists for further instructions - if worsenes, feeling new lightheadedness/dizziness sx - or any other concerning symptom like shortness of breath, chest pain, new fevers - please return to the hospital for further evaluation. ", "bhc": "___ F h/o colon ca, breast ca who presents with bright red blood per rectum, bleeding stopped since presentation - given IVF from ED - admitted for monitoring - Hct mildly lower at 39.1 at time of discharge (suspect hemodilution) - no further bleeding sx. Orthostatics repeated prior to discharge and were NEGATIVE. <br> # Bright red blood per rectum: ? diverticulosis vs int hemorrhoidal vS AVMs vs mass. Vital signs are stable. Given history - strained night prior with large BM - followed later by BRBPR without pain - more consistant with hemorrhoidal - however with h/o colon CA with last colonscopy ___ yrs ago - recommend GI eval in near future as outpt. Monitored q8h overnigh and on tele. The patient has not had any further episodes in hospital. Pt noted afebrile - low susp for infx etiology. Given pt's h/o colon CA - most appropriate for likely full colonoscopy by pt's outpt Gastroenterologist - pt states in contact - will get appointment to be seen asap. Without further bleeding sx, neg orthostatics stable for discharge now with outpatient colonscopy to be done in next ___ weeks (pt states can contact her gastroenterologist right away.) <br> # Asthma: Stable. Continue nebs and prednisone taper as prescribed recently. <br> # Lyme disease: Continue doxycycline. ***Note pt initially concerned that this abx would have been the cause given only new medication - assured less likely and given prior outpt treatment concern (with evident deer tick brought into office!) - would cont outpt plan and cont abx for full course. <br> # FEN: diet as tolerated . # PPX: No heparin b/c of GI bleeding. Pneumoboots while in bed here - ambulating without problem however. . # FULL CODE . # Dispo: d/c to home - with GI f/u "}
{"hadm_id": "18917761-DS-35", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> SURGERY <ALLERGIES> tacrolimus <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> symptomatic splenomegaly <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> ___ Splenic artery Embolization ___ pseudoaneurysm embolization ___ ERCP with stent <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> Mr. ___ is a ___ yo male s/p liver transplant on ___ for NASH cirrhosis who is presenting today as a planned admission for pain control after a mid-pole splenic embolization for symptomatic splenomegaly. His post transplant course has been complicated by significant immunodeficiency, pancytopenia derived from thalassemia, pyruvate kinase deficiency and a profound splenomegaly. Mr. ___ was hospitalized recently for a pneumonia likely developed in the setting of immunodeficiency as well as sinusitis. He was treated with IV vancomycin, Zosyn, erythromycin and ceftriaxone for a total of seven days. He has been getting weekly aranesp shots by hematology. He is currently on CSA 100mg BID and Creat 2.5-3.0.m PLT count is 37, so he required 2u FFP prior to the procedure today. On presentation, Mr. ___ denies any pain (abdominal, groin, or otherwise), nausea, vomiting, fevers, chills, headaches, chest pain, diarrhea, or constipation. He does endorse some SOB, however, this has been stable for years. He is voiding and stooling normally. This morning he took 6u of Lantus instead of his normal dose of 12u, otherwise he took all of his medications as prescribed. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> 1. ___ cirrhosis complicated by mild ascites, portal hypertension in the form esophageal varices and splenomegaly. - S/p variceal bleed s/p banding ___ - S/p TIPS ___ and TIPS redo/downsize ___ d/t recurrent hepatic encephalopathy and TIPS stenosis - S/p OLT ___ from ___ year old ABO compatible standard criteria brain dead donor 2. S/p OLT ___: No history of rejection, no history of BK, high CMV risk D+/R-. On tacrolimus monotherapy. For PPX he is on atovoquone from Bactrim due to persistent leukopenia. 2. Pyruvate kinase deficiency, complicated by chronic hemolysis. 3. Cholelithiasis, likely related to pigment stones from chronic hemolysis. 4. Beta thalassemia. 5. History of iron deficiency on iron supplementation. 6. Diabetes mellitus, on insulin. 7. History of migraines. 8. Chronic back pain and leg pain status post fall in ___. 9. Gout. 10. History of right cheek basal cell carcinoma excised in ___. <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> There is a family history of thalassemia trait and arthritis. There is no family history of liver disease or GI cancers. No famly history of CAD/MI. <PHYSICAL EXAM> P/E: VS: T: 97.2 P: 59 BP: 162/76 RR: 18 O2sat: 95 RA GEN: WD, WN in NAD HEENT: NCAT, anicteric, MMM, EOMI CV: RRR PULM: non-labored, no respiratory distress ABD: soft, NT, ND, no mass, +splenomegaly. 1.5 cm incisional hernia present. L inguinal hernia present. Groin: R groin puncture site with angioseal present over top. no swelling present, area slightly tender to palpation. minimal shadowing present on dressing. EXT: WWP, 2+ radial pulses, palpable femoral pulses b/l and palpable DP pulse b/l. NEURO: A&Ox3, no focal neurologic deficits <PERTINENT RESULTS> Admission Labs: ___ 07: 43AM BLOOD WBC-2.3* RBC-3.11* Hgb-7.1* Hct-23.6* MCV-76* MCH-22.8* MCHC-30.1* RDW-17.1* RDWSD-47.3* Plt Ct-28* ___ 07: 43AM BLOOD ___ ___ 07: 43AM BLOOD Creat-3.0* ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD Glucose-103* UreaN-32* Creat-2.7* Na-139 K-4.7 Cl-109* HCO3-18* AnGap-17 ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD ALT-20 AST-51* AlkPhos-54 TotBili-2.4* DirBili-1.4* IndBili-1.0 ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD Calcium-7.8* Phos-3.3 Mg-1.3* ___ 05: 20AM BLOOD Cyclspr-64* Discharge Labs: ___ 03: 23AM BLOOD WBC-6.1# RBC-3.32* Hgb-8.2* Hct-24.9* MCV-75* MCH-24.7* MCHC-32.9 RDW-18.6* RDWSD-49.2* Plt Ct-44* ___ 03: 23AM BLOOD ___ PTT-27.2 ___ ___ 03: 23AM BLOOD Glucose-120* UreaN-40* Creat-3.0* Na-136 K-5.4* Cl-106 HCO3-14* AnGap-21* ___ 03: 23AM BLOOD ALT-<5 AST-8 AlkPhos-56 Amylase-25 TotBili-5.0* ___ 03: 23AM BLOOD Calcium-8.0* Phos-3.8 Mg-1.7 ___ 06: 08AM BLOOD Cyclspr-122 ___ 05: 58AM BLOOD Cyclspr-125 ___ 03: 23AM BLOOD Cyclspr-197 ___ 05: 46AM BLOOD Hapto-49 <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. CycloSPORINE (Neoral) MODIFIED 100 mg PO Q12H 2. Albuterol Inhaler ___ PUFF IH Q4H: PRN sob/wheeze/cough 3. Atovaquone Suspension 1500 mg PO DAILY 4. Nephrocaps 1 CAP PO DAILY 5. Aranesp (in polysorbate) (darbepoetin alfa in polysorbat) 100 mcg/0.5 mL injection 1X/WEEK ___ 6. Vitamin D ___ UNIT PO 1X/WEEK (MO) 7. Filgrastim 300 mcg SC PRN: AS DIRECTED BY TRANSPLANT CLINIC neutropenia 8. Fludrocortisone Acetate 0.1 mg PO 3X/WEEK (___) 9. FoLIC Acid 4 mg PO DAILY 10. Glargine 12 Units Breakfast Insulin SC Sliding Scale using HUM Insulin 11. Metoprolol Succinate XL 150 mg PO BID 12. Ondansetron 8 mg PO DAILY: PRN nausea. takes prior to am meds 13. Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q12H 14. Acetaminophen ___ mg PO Q8H: PRN Pain - Mild <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. HYDROmorphone (Dilaudid) 2 mg PO Q8H: PRN Pain - Moderate RX *hydromorphone 2 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth every eight (8) hours Disp #*10 Tablet Refills: *0 2. Glargine 12 Units Breakfast Insulin SC Sliding Scale using HUM Insulin 3. Acetaminophen ___ mg PO Q8H: PRN Pain - Mild do not take more than 3000mg of Tylenol in 24 hours 4. Albuterol Inhaler ___ PUFF IH Q4H: PRN sob/wheeze/cough 5. Aranesp (in polysorbate) (darbepoetin alfa in polysorbat) 100 mcg/0.5 mL injection 1X/WEEK ___ 6. Atovaquone Suspension 1500 mg PO DAILY 7. CycloSPORINE (Neoral) MODIFIED 100 mg PO Q12H 8. Filgrastim 300 mcg SC PRN: AS DIRECTED BY TRANSPLANT CLINIC neutropenia 9. Fludrocortisone Acetate 0.1 mg PO 3X/WEEK (___) 10. FoLIC Acid 4 mg PO DAILY 11. Metoprolol Succinate XL 150 mg PO BID 12. Nephrocaps 1 CAP PO DAILY 13. Ondansetron 8 mg PO DAILY: PRN nausea. takes prior to am meds 14. Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q12H 15. Vitamin D ___ UNIT PO 1X/WEEK (MO) <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Symptomatic Splenomegaly s/p splenic artery embolization. anemia ___ nephropathy <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Please have your labs drawn tomorrow, ___ MEDICATION: - Continue all other medications you were taking before surgery, unless otherwise directed - You make take Tylenol or prescribed pain medications for any post procedure pain or discomfort WHAT TO EXPECT: - It is normal to feel tired and have a decreased appetite, your appetite will return with time - Drink plenty of fluids and eat small frequent meals - It is important to eat nutritious food options (high fiber, lean meats, vegetables/fruits, low fat, low cholesterol) to maintain your strength and assist in wound healing - To avoid constipation: eat a high fiber diet ACTIVITIES: - When you go home, you may walk and use stairs - You may shower (let the soapy water run over groin incision, rinse and pat dry) - The groin puncture site may be left uncovered, unless you have small amounts of drainage from the wound, then place a dry dressing or band aid over the area - No heavy lifting, pushing or pulling (greater than 5 lbs) for 1 week (to allow groin puncture to heal) - No driving until you are no longer taking pain medications CALL THE OFFICE FOR: ___ - Numbness, coldness or pain in lower extremities - Temperature greater than 101.5F for 24 hours - New or increased drainage from incision or white, yellow or green drainage from incisions - Bleeding from groin puncture site SUDDEN, SEVERE BLEEDING OR SWELLING (Groin puncture site) - Lie down, keep leg straight and have someone apply firm pressure to area for 10 minutes. If bleeding stops, call Interventional Radiology office ___. If bleeding does not stop, call ___ for transfer to closest Emergency Room. ", "bhc": "Mr. ___ was admitted to the Transplant Surgery Service under Dr. ___ on ___ for pain control after a splenic artery embolization done by the Interventional Radiology service. He received PO Dilaudid and standing tylenol for pain control during his hospital stay. On the morning of ___, his Hct was 19.4, a repeat Hct was 18.6. He was given 2u pRBCs. A post-transfusion Hct was 22. He spiked to 102.7 for which he was pan-cultred. CXR was negative for pneumonia. IV Zosyn was started for empiric coverage. Right groin puncture site pain was persistent. U/S of the area identified a 2.5 cm partially thrombosed right groin pseudoaneurysm, with a neck measuring 3 mm in width. Hct decreased to 19 again. IV Vitamin K was given for INR of 2.9. An abdominal CT was also done demonstrating just ascites. Post CT, creatinine increased to 3.9. He was bloused with IV fluid for low urine output. ___ then embolized the pseudoaneurysm on ___. Postop, embolization, right groin appeared ecchymotic with positive distal pulses. He had less pain in the right groin. Hct was down to 17. Two units of PRBC were transfused with post hct of 23. Postop transfusion, he had an 8 beat run of VTach that resolved. Calcium repletion was given for calcium of 7.5. Hct stabilized at ___. LFTs were notable of rise in t.bili to 3.9. ERCP was done on to interrogate the metal stent on ___. It was noted that the previously placed metal stent had migrated, a CBD anastomotic stricture was noted and a new MS was placed. He tolerated this procedure well and diet was advanced to regular the next day. A repeat U/S of the groin was done on ___lood flow at the neck of the pseudoaneurysm which may represent a small arterial branch vessel. No documented flow within the body of the pseudoaneurysm. He continued to have discomfort at this site as well as abdominal and back pain for which he was receiving po Dilaudid. He was taking a total of ___ per day of the Dilaudid. The plan was to send him home with a script for dilaudid 2mg prn every 8 hours with only 10 tabs. Of note, the anemia/hemolysis w/u was negative. He received a total of 3 units of prbc. Leukopenia was improving. Blood and urine cultures were negative to date. Creatinine increased and this was likely related to the contrast given during the angiogram. Creatinine increased to 3.9, but had decreased to 3.0 on the day of discharge. Potassium was elevated to 5.4. He was instructed to follow a low potassium diet, have his labs checked on ___ and to resume the Florinef. Weight was 77kg up from admission weight of 71kg. Urine output was averaging 400cc/day. A repeat right groin U/S should be done one week from ___ to assess pseudoaneurysm. At time of discharge, vitals signs were stable, he was a regular diet, ambulating independently. "}
{"hadm_id": "16083092-DS-11", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> NEUROLOGY <ALLERGIES> Penicillins / Compazine / Toradol <ATTENDING> ___ <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Left facial pain <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> None <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> HPI: The pt is a ___ yo RHW w/ hx of migraines, TMJ syndrome s/p surgery, recurrent otitis externa no presents with an episode of sudden onset L sided facial pain and headache. Today at 2 an she awoke her from sleep with a sharp, like a knife in nature pain on the left side of her face. In addition she describes this as a head in vice, not throbbing, around temporal area and radiation to behind L ear and into the maxilla. She took 15mg PO of morphine w/o relief. She reported associated nausea, but no emesis, no photo/phonophobia. There was no numbness, paresthesias, weakness, changes in vision, vertigo or hearing. There is no trauma associated with this. Because the pain was intractable, she came to the ED. On arrival, she received IV morphine, Ativan and Valium w/o relief of the pain. Her pain is ___ and does not radiate. She endorses pain at her occiput as well. Of note, she had a similar episode last week, was admitted to ___ where she reportedly had a negative head CT and LP, was treated with IVF and Dilaudid and was discharged home. At this time, she endorses mild TMJ pain with mastication. There is no ear pain, but she notes a decreased hearing on L over the past 3 months. 1 mo ago, she was treated for a \"raging\" ear infection for which she was treated with ABx. She also has a history of migraines w/ associated L sided weakness (states that resolved). Her current symptoms are entirely different from her migraines (throbbing, scintillations, photo/phonophobia). . Neuro ROS: ( - ) loss of vision, blurred vision ( - ) diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia ( - ) lightheadedness, vertigo, tinnitus ( - ) difficulties producing or comprehending speech ( - ) focal weakness, numbness, paraesthesia ( - ) bowel or bladder incontinence or retention ( - ) difficulty with gait General ROS: ( - ) recent fever or chills, night sweats or recent weight loss. ( - ) cough, shortness of breath ( - ) chest pain or tightness, palpitations ( - ) vomiting ( - ) diarrhea, constipation or abdominal pain ( - ) recent change in bowel or bladder habits. ( - ) arthralgias or myalgias <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> - intermittent otitis externa on her left ear - TMJ pain, s/p surgical repair - Migraine - Rotator cuff tendinopathy with possible bursitis. - Bicipital tendinopathy. - hysterectomy s/p lysis of adhesions ___ SBO in ___ - s/p cholecystectomy - s/p appendectomy <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> [ + ] CVA - Gmother [ + ] CAD - Gmother [ + ] Cancer - mother, cervical/breast ca [ + ] Intracerebral aneurysms - Grandmother. <PHYSICAL EXAM> Vitals: T: ___ P: 76 R: 16 BP: 136/78 SaO2: 98% RA General: Awake, anxious, tears streaming down face. HEENT: NC/AT, no scleral icterus noted, MMM. B/l injected sclera. TTP at L occipital notch, TTP at TMJ on L and external canal erythema and irritation. She mild TTP at L maxilla. Neck: Supple, no carotid bruits appreciated. Full ROM. Pulmonary: CTA bilaterally Cardiac: RR, nl. S1S2, no M/R/G Abdomen: soft, NT/ND. Extremities: warm, dry, no edema Pulses: 2+ radial, DP bilaterally. Skin: no rashes or lesions noted. MSK: no tenderness to palpation of C/T/L spine. Neurologic: -Mental Status: Alert, oriented x 3. Able to relate history without difficulty. Attention: ___ but not ___ backward (missed ___. Language: Fluent Repetition and comprehension intact. Normal prosody. No paraphasic errors. Naming: low frequency objects intact. Reads without difficulty. Speech was not dysarthric. Able to follow both midline and appendicular commands. Registers 3 objects and recalls ___ at 5 minutes, ___ w/ prompting. There was no evidence of apraxia or neglect. -Cranial Nerves: I: Olfaction not tested. II: PERRL 4 to 2mm and brisk. VFF to confrontation. Funduscopic exam revealed no papilledema. III, IV, VI: EOMI without nystagmus. Normal saccades. V: Facial sensation intact to light touch and pin-prick. VII: mild LNLF flattening, resolves w/ affective smile and grin. VIII: Hearing intact to finger-rub bilaterally, but decreased on L. IX, X: Palate elevates symmetrically. XI: ___ strength in trapezii and SCM bilaterally. XII: Tongue protrudes in midline. -Motor: Normal bulk, tone throughout. No pronator drift bilaterally. No tremor, noted. Delt Bic Tri WrE FFl FE L 5 5 5 4+ 5 4+ R 5 5 5 5 5 5 IP Quad Ham TA ___ ___ ___ L 5- 5 4+ 5- 5 4+ 4+ R 5 5 5 5 5 4+ 4+ -Sensory: Light touch - intact Pinprick - intact Cold sensation - intact Vibratory sense - intact Proprioception - intact No extinction to DSS. -DTRs: Bi Tri ___ Pat Ach L 2 2 2 2 1 R 2 2 2 2 1 Plantar response: RIGHT - flexor LEFT - flexor -Coordination: No intention tremor. No dysmetria on FNF or HKS bilaterally. -Gait: Good initiation. Narrow-based, normal stride and arm swing. Able to walk in tandem without difficulty. Romberg absent. <PERTINENT RESULTS> ___ 04: 10AM BLOOD WBC-6.6 RBC-4.34 Hgb-11.8* Hct-37.7 MCV-87 MCH-27.3 MCHC-31.4 RDW-13.3 Plt ___ ___ 03: 12AM BLOOD Neuts-51.6 ___ Monos-4.3 Eos-4.6* Baso-0.9 ___ 03: 12AM BLOOD ___ PTT-30.0 ___ ___ 04: 10AM BLOOD Glucose-129* UreaN-11 Creat-0.7 Na-142 K-3.8 Cl-109* HCO3-28 AnGap-9 CT Head ___: No acute intracranial hemorrhage or mass effect. Small foci of air are noted in the paraspinal soft tissues, spinal canal, extradural in location at the level of atlanto-occipital region (series 102b/im 322 and 326) which are of uncertain origin and significance if there is no h/o trauma- may be seen secondary to IV injection, if any- correlate with history; new from prior CT Head study of ___. MRI/MRA/MRV Head ___ (Verbal report from radiology): Unremarkable except for infundibular origin or vessels, but no sign of aneurysm. <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> ALENDRONATE [FOSAMAX] - 70 mg Tablet - 1 Tablet(s) by mouth weekly as needed for for bones MORPHINE - 15 mg Tablet - 1 Tablet(s) by mouth three times a day as needed for pain (chronic for abdomina and pelvic pain) NEOMYCIN-POLYMYXIN-HC - 3.5 mg/mL-10,000 unit/mL-1 % Drops, Suspension - 4 drops(s) in ear three times a day OMEPRAZOLE - (Prescribed by Other Provider) - Dosage uncertain CALCIUM CARBONATE-VITAMIN D3 - 600 mg-400 unit Tablet - 1 Tablet(s) by mouth twice a day as needed for for bones <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Butalbital-Acetaminophen-Caff 50-325-40 mg Tablet Sig: ___ Tablets PO Q4H (every 4 hours) as needed for pain. Disp: *12 Tablet(s)* Refills: *0* 2. Neomycin-Polymyxin-HC 3.5-10,000-1 mg-unit/mL-% Drops, Suspension Sig: Four (4) Drop Otic TID (3 times a day). 3. Omeprazole 20 mg Capsule, Delayed Release(E.C.) Sig: One (1) Capsule, Delayed Release(E.C.) PO DAILY (Daily). 4. Calcium Carbonate 500 mg Tablet, Chewable Sig: One (1) Tablet, Chewable PO DAILY (Daily). 5. Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) 400 unit Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY (Daily). 6. Docusate Sodium 100 mg Capsule Sig: One (1) Capsule PO TID (3 times a day). 7. Senna 8.6 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO BID (2 times a day). 8. Polyethylene Glycol 3350 17 gram/dose Powder Sig: Seventeen (17) g PO DAILY (Daily) as needed for constipation. 9. Lactulose 10 gram/15 mL Syrup Sig: Thirty (30) ML PO DAILY (Daily) as needed for constipation. 10. Morphine 15 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO QID (4 times a day) as needed for pain. 11. Oxcarbazepine 150 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO BID (2 times a day). Disp: *120 Tablet(s)* Refills: *2* 12. Dilaudid 2 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO twice a day as needed for pain for 3 doses. Disp: *3 Tablet(s)* Refills: *0* <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Trigeminal neuralgia TMJ Migraine <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> You were admitted with left facial pain which is likely due to a combination of TMJ, irritation of the nerve called trigeminal neuralgia, and possibly migraine as well as prior ear infections. Your MRI was normal. Take all medications as directed to prevent worsening or recurrence of the pain. Follow-up with Dr. ___ in the pain clinic for further recommendations, as well as a dentist or oromax___ surgeon specializing in TMJ disease. You were admitted with left facial pain which is likely due to a combination of TMJ, irritation of the nerve called trigeminal neuralgia, and possibly migraine as well as prior ear infections. Your MRI was normal. Take all medications as directed to prevent worsening or recurrence of the pain. Follow-up with Dr. ___ in the pain clinic for further recommendations, as well as a dentist or oromaxillofacial surgeon specializing in TMJ disease. ", "bhc": "The patient was admitted for evaluation and management of her facial pain. MRI/MRA/MRV brain was unremarkable, and the etiology of the pain was thought to be a combination of trigeminal neuralgia, temperomandibular joint disease, and migraine. The patient frequently demanded narcotics during her admission, reporting that \"nothing works except dilaudid (or IV medications).\" She was noted to complain of significantly more discomfort when medical personnel were in the room. There had apparently been some concern for drug-seeking behavior in the past. One-time doses of dilaudid 2mg were given several times when the patient complained of severe pain; she also received morphine and ativan due to pain and anxiety prior to her MRI. She was started on oxcarbazepine (Trileptal) for pain control of trigeminal neuralgia. She was discharged with a prescription for oxcarbazepine as well as dilaudid 2mg PRN (3 tabs total), with instructions to follow-up with pain clinic and dental surgery. The MRI/MRA/MRV was discussed with neuroradiology prior to discharge, but the final read is pending. "}
{"hadm_id": "17656866-DS-8", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> PSYCHIATRY <ALLERGIES> No Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> \"I've been trying to figure out why I did what I did\" <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> No invasive or surgical procedure however a CTA head/neck was done given mechanism of injury which showed atherosclerotic disease but no vascular injury. Patient was successfully extubated on hospital day #2 and diet was advanced. Home medications were resumed. He was followed by the psychiatry consult team and placed on a ___ for continued suicidal ideation and multiple symptoms of depression. <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> ___ is a ___ male with a history of severe major depressive disorder, with at least 3 previous suicide attempts, also with chronic back and leg pain, CAD s/p 5-vessel bypass and 2 stents, ulcerative colitis, who was transferred from an outside hospital after being found unresponsive at home after a suicide attempt via hanging. Per OMR notes, the patient was found by his girlfriend at 6: 30 ___ with a belt around his neck in the basement. Unresponsive. Police were on the scene and immediately started CPR. The pt was resuscitated after approximately 20 minutes and was taken to ___ where he was intubated. He was transferred to the ___ trauma ICU on ___. At ___ a repeat trauma survey and review of OSH images revealed no additional injuries. A CTA head/neck was done given mechanism of injury which showed atherosclerotic disease but no vascular injury. Patient was successfully extubated on hospital day #2 and diet was advanced. Home medications were resumed. He was followed by the psychiatry consult team and placed on a ___ for continued suicidal ideation and multiple symptoms of depression. Patient was then transferred to the floor, where he remained hemodynamically stable, was ambulating and voiding without assistance. On ___ he was medically cleared for psychiatric admission and was transferred to ___ for safety, stabilization, medication management and possibly ECT. On Deac4 admission interview patient states, \"I've been thinking a lot about what happened and trying to figure out why I did what I did\" referring to his suicide attempt several days ago. He states that since he has been at the hospital he feels well-supported and more comfortable, and denies feeling depressed at this time. He reports that he has been telling jokes and laughing a lot with staff on the medical service but also wonders if he may be doing this to cover up deeper feelings of sadness. He reports that his mood has often fluctuated between high and low. Regarding suicidality, he denies current SI and says that chronic pain plays a large role in these thoughts. He states that his recent attempt was impulsive, and that \"I can't say whether I would [attempt suicide] again; here I feel safe but if I were at home that may be a different story.\" He formerly considered his two grandchildren \"to be the reason I am alive\" however he saw them the morning before he attempted suicide \"and that clearly didn't stop me.\" He reports that he would alert unit staff if his feelings worsen. Of note, earlier today to the psychiatry consult team, patient endorsed continued SI as well as decreased interest or pleasure in activities, difficulty with sleep and appetite, low motivation, low energy, guilt, feeling like he is a burden on his girlfriend, hopelessness, and helplessness. Patient denies HI. He reports seeing occasional flashes of light out of the corners of his eyes; this is new since the suicide attempt; no hx of migraine and no current headache. Otherwise denies AVH. Also recently has been feeling as if someone is behind him but he turns around and no one is there. Denies paranoia or hypervigilance. Denies current or past symptoms of mania. Patient was oriented to the unit and informed to contact staff with questions or issues. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> Chronic back/leg pain CAD (s/p 5-vessel bypass and 2 stents) HTN HLD UC GERD Rheumatoid arthritis <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Medical: Mother with CHF ___: Patient reports history of depression in his mother. States his brother died of hepatitis but \"he gave up at the end and stopped taking his medications\". <PHYSICAL EXAM> 188.2lbs Heart: regular rate and rhythm, normal S1 and S2, no murmurs appreciated Lungs: clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes, rales or rhonchi appreciated Abdomen: non-tender to palpation, normoactive bowel sounds x4, non-distended, no guarding, masses or rebound Extremities: warm and well perfused, no edema in bilateral lower extremities; site of femoral IVs inspected bilaterally - significant bruising on L leg but both insertion sites appear clean without drainage or erythema Neuro: pupils constricted and reactive to direct and near (2mm-1mm); EOMI; muscles of facial expression intact and symmetric bilaterally; shoulder-shrugs symmetric; light touch diffusely intact; strength ___ throughout UE and ___ ___: *station and gait: within normal limits *tone and strength: within normal limits <PERTINENT RESULTS> ___: -CBC: RBC: 3.6 Hgb 10.5 Hct: 32.4 -BMP: Na 140 K 4.5 Cl 101 HCO3 35 BUN: 10 Cr: 0.8 AnGap: 9 Glucose: 112 ALT: 10 AST: 15 AlkP: 36 Tbili: 0.9 Albumin: 3.5 Ca: 9.1 Phos: 4.1 Mg: 2.2 VPA: 61 ___: MOCA - Scored ___ with some discrepancies and adjustment for educational level (Normal greater than or equal to ___. Lowest performance on Memory, Delayed Recall, and Language/Repetition. ___: WBC: 5.1 HGB: 9.8* HCT: 30.1* Plt Count: 138* ___: Na: 142 K: 3.6 Cl: 108 CO2: 26 BUN: 12 Creat: 0.8 Glucose: 103* ___: Ca: 8.3* Mg: 1.9 PO4: 3.5 ___: Benzodiazepine: POS Barbiturate: NEG Opiate: NEG Cocaine: NEG Amphetamine: NEG Methadone: NEG <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. ClonazePAM 1 mg PO QID: PRN anxiety 2. Gabapentin 1200 mg PO TID 3. PredniSONE 5 mg PO DAILY 4. Atorvastatin 80 mg PO QPM 5. TraZODone 100 mg PO QHS 6. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 7. OxycoDONE (Immediate Release) 10 mg PO Q4H: PRN breakthrough pain 8. OxyCODONE SR (OxyconTIN) 40 mg PO Q6H 9. DiCYCLOmine 10 mg PO TID 10. Metoprolol Succinate XL 12.5 mg PO DAILY 11. Clopidogrel 75 mg PO DAILY 12. Methotrexate 15 mg PO 1X/WEEK (MO) <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY 2. Atorvastatin 80 mg PO QPM 3. ClonazePAM 1 mg PO TID: PRN anxiety 4. DiCYCLOmine 10 mg PO TID 5. Clopidogrel 75 mg PO DAILY 6. Gabapentin 1200 mg PO TID 7. Methotrexate 15 mg PO 1X/WEEK (MO) 8. Metoprolol Succinate XL 12.5 mg PO DAILY 9. OxyCODONE SR (OxyconTIN) 40 mg PO Q6H 10. OxycoDONE (Immediate Release) 10 mg PO Q4H: PRN breakthrough pain 11. PredniSONE 5 mg PO DAILY 12. TraZODone 50 mg PO QHS: PRN insomnia 13. TraZODone 50 mg PO QHS 14. Cyanocobalamin 50 mcg PO DAILY RX *cyanocobalamin (vitamin B-12) [Vitamin B-12] 50 mcg 1 tablet(s) by mouth once a day Disp #*14 Tablet Refills: *0 15. Divalproex (DELayed Release) 1500 mg PO DAILY RX *divalproex [Depakote] 500 mg 3 tablet(s) by mouth once a day Disp #*42 Tablet Refills: *0 16. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID RX *docusate sodium [Colace] 100 mg 1 capsule(s) by mouth twice a day Disp #*28 Capsule Refills: *0 17. HydrOXYzine 50 mg PO Q4H: PRN anxiety RX *hydroxyzine HCl 50 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth every four (4) hours Disp #*28 Tablet Refills: *0 18. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY RX *multivitamin [Chewable Multi Vitamin] 1 tablet(s) by mouth once a day Disp #*30 Tablet Refills: *0 19. OLANZapine 10 mg PO QHS RX *olanzapine 10 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth HS Disp #*14 Tablet Refills: *0 20. OLANZapine 5 mg PO BID: PRN agitation, anxiety RX *olanzapine 5 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth twice a day Disp #*14 Tablet Refills: *0 <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home With Service Facility: ___ <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Major Depressive Disorder, Severe, Recurrent Cognitive disorder (s/p Anoxic brain injury) <DISCHARGE CONDITION> -Mental Status: Clear and coherent -Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive -Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> You were hospitalized at ___ for suicidal ideation and attempt. We adjusted your medications, and you are now ready for discharge and continued treatment with ___ services and extensive aftercare (PCP, ___, cognitive neurology, brain injury rehab clinic). -Please follow up with all outpatient appointments as listed - take this discharge paperwork to your appointments. -Please continue all medications as directed. -Please avoid abusing alcohol and any drugs--whether prescription drugs or illegal drugs--as this can further worsen your medical and psychiatric illnesses. -Do not drive until cleared by your PCP due to potential for danger given head injury and sedating medications and recent changes to your mental status -Please contact your outpatient psychiatrist or other providers if you have any concerns. -Please call ___ or go to your nearest emergency room if you feel unsafe in any way and are unable to immediately reach your health care providers. *It was a pleasure to have worked with you, and we wish you the best of health.* ", "bhc": "GLOBAL ASSESSMENT: ___ w/ multiple medical problems including chronic pain, narcotic usage, and vascular disease who is s/p a nearly lethal suicide attempt by hanging. He suffered signs/symptoms c/w anoxic brain injury and has resultant cognitive problems. Mr. ___ underwent a fairly lengthy psychiatric admission aimed at reducing his modifiable risk factors for suicide and enhancing his supports and educating his family about his illness and prognosis. During this long hospital course, Mr. ___ progressed in terms of mood and behaviors. After his initial outburst upon arrival to the unit, he had no behavioral problems and complied with treatment in the form of mood stabilizers (Depakote, Klonopin), and antipsychotics (Zyprexa). He also had 1 ECT treatment and underwent a number of neurological and cognitive tests. Mr. ___ in 2 lengthy family meetings and did well in them during which time the team educated patient and family about diagnosis, risk, and treatment plan along w/ discharge planning and outpatient recommendations to best mitigate risk. SAFETY: The pt. was placed on 15 minute checks on admission and remained here on that level of observation throughout. He was unit-restricted. ___: Patient became agitated when informed he could not receive oxycontin and PRN oxycodone at same time; physical altercation with nursing and security where patient swung at security and strangled a nurse. Required 4-point restraints and ___ IM. Team & staff addressed this and patient agreeable with treatment plan. There were no further incidents beyond this point and Mr. ___ had a fairly benign admission with no violence following this initial encounter. He agreed to medication, tolerated it well, and was safe throughout the remainder of his admission. LEGAL: ___ CONSULTS: Pain team Neurology Neuropsychology service ___ opinion by ___ medical director PSYCHIATRIC: #MDD, recurrent, severe w/ psychotic features Patient presented with MSE notable for dysphoric and restricted affect. He endorsed depressed mood although without suicidal ideation on unit, citing his history of impulsive violent behavior toward self and others but paradoxically stating grandchildren as the reason to live yet not being future-oriented toward wanting to live himself. Pt continued on home meds including Trazodone 100 mg PO/NG QHS for sleep with additional 50mg PO QHS PRN insomnia added. ECT discussed as next treatment option, patient provided information for consent and seen by ECT team and medical team for clearance. After altercation with staff after not being able to take oxycodone and oxycontin at the same time, Ativan 1mg PO QID PRN anxiety in place of Clonazepam 1 mg PO QID PRN anxiety. Haldol also increased from 5mg to 10mg PO BID schedule and 5mg PO BID PRN agit/anxiety. Of note, he did endorse recent paranoid thoughts vs. visual hallucinations of a \"man in a cowboy hat\" in his peripheral vision that he intermittently stated he started noticing several weeks prior to admission, which he stated was not distressful during hospitalization while on haldol. Benztropine 1mg PO TID PRN EPS/stiffness also continued. Divalproex was increased from 500mg PO BID to ___ daily after altercation and for ECT. VPA level 61. Hydroxyzine 50mg PO q4h PRN anxiety was also ordered after altercation. After having one ECT treatment, however, Mr. ___ became very delirious and upset by the treatment. He declined to have any further ECT and the team stopped treatment at his request. Mr. ___ went on to have a \"great\" mood while in the hospital and did not c/o dysphoria or suicidal thoughts. He responded well to the antipsychotics which were gradually tapered from Haldol over to Zyprexa, as he did experience notable EPS in the form of tremors which were not satisfactorily alleviated w/ Benztropine. Once off the Haldol for about a week, he experienced resolve of EPS and felt much better w/ no residual hallucinations/delusions. Mr. ___ was stable on Depakote, Zyprexa and Klonopin. He tolerated medications and had no side effects. He met w/ the team daily, worked with nursing and sat through lengthy family meetings and participated in a meaningful way. By the end of his admission, he was even attending groups and was an active member. #Cognitive Disorder - Anoxic Brain Injury Prior to ECT, the team performed a MOCA on Mr. ___, on which he scored a ___ on ___. We immediately consulted the neuropsychology team to perform more in-depth memory/cognitive tests. Dr. ___ that Mr. ___ failed the cognitive tests and showed an inability to learn new memories or to recall past ones. Lowest performance on Memory, Delayed Recall, and Language/Repetition. Dr. ___ w/ the team that Mr. ___ results are highly consistent with an anoxic brain injury. A family meeting was held in which we discussed these results w/ patient and his son. In addition, we strongly recommended aftercare referrals to CNU and brain injury clinic that could further assess and treat this condition. GENERAL MEDICAL CONDITIONS: # Anemia ___ RBC: 3.6, Hgb 10.5, Hct: 32.4 checked and uptrending and closer to baseline when compared to previous Hgb 9.8 and Hct 30.1 on ___. Baseline ~ ___. Most likely ___ malabsorption from ulcerative colitis. Patient with adequate dietary intake while on unit. - Consult medicine if downtrending and/or if patient symptomatic - ___ consider nutrition consult # Elevated HCO3 HCO3 35 today ___. Most likely metabolic response/renal compensation for hypoventilation and lower O2 sats (95% today) secondary to level of opiates on board. Team discussed with pt's PCP who stated this is baseline for pt and without contraindication for ECT # Chronic pain - Continued Gabapentin 1200mg PO TID, oxycontin 40 mg PO Q6H and oxycodone 10mg PO Q4H PRN for breakthrough pain. Patient was not to take oxycodone and oxycontin at the same time due to concern for sedation/respiratory depression. -Dr. ___ endorsed concern regarding patient's misuse of opiates and concern that patient may have acquired non-prescribed opiates in the past. He was not in favor of suboxone for Mr. ___ at this time, b/c he had tried in the past with no benefit and in fact, had reportedly abused the medication. The team consulted w/ the pain service at ___ who recommended that we not change his narcotic regimen right now as it would likely lead to escalation in violence. They agreed to f/u with the patient as an outpatient. This plan was shared w/ Dr. ___ pt's PCP who is currently managing pain. He appreciated us getting the pain consult and for keeping him updated about the patient's progress. # HLD - Continued home atorvastatin # UC - Continued home dicyclomine #CAD - Continued home ASA 81 and metoprolol # Rheumatoid arthritis - Continued home Methotrexate 15mg PO weekly and Prednisone 5mg PO QD #Other home medications - Constipation: Colace 100mg PO BID, Senna 8.6mg PO BID PRN - B12 deficiency: cyanocobalamin 50 mcg PO daily - Continued MVIs PSYCHOSOCIAL: #) GROUPS/MILIEU: Mr. ___ was very active in the milieu. He tended to wake up early and sit in the dayroom w/ peers watching tv. Often, the team would find him falling asleep in the dayroom but he was easily redirected. His family visited often and he had good meetings w/ them without any incidents. He also attended OT groups, especially during last week of admission, during which time he was a very active participant and engaged well w/ peers and staff. #) COLLATERAL CONTACTS: - ___ (son, ___: ___ - ___ (ex-girlfriend): ___ - Dr. ___, ___ #) FAMILY INVOLVEMENT: - The family (son and GF) were extremely involved during ___ hospital stay. The team spoke w/ them on the phone, interacted during their regular visits to the unit, and came to 2 formal lengthy meetings w/ the treatment team (each lasting about 1 hour). The family meetings were aimed at educating family and patient about risk, diagnosis and discharge and treatment plan. Family felt patient was doing very well and advocated for d/c. They understood our instruction that pt is high risk for repeat suicide attempt and we reviewed a safety plan in detail. They agreed to call ___ immediately at any worrisome signs of pt's safety. They also understood importance of f/u as we have directed. #) INTERVENTIONS: -ECT - Total of 1 treatment -Medications: Enhanced support w/ psychiatric medications and ongoing general medical medications and analgesics -Consults: Neurology; Pain service; Neuropsyhiatry/Neurocognitive Service -Groups/therapy; provided individual/group and milieu therapy -Aftercare planning; Referrals placed to a variety of sources including; pain service, ___ clinic; brain injury clinic; PCP; Psychiatry and Psychotherapy. -___ Referral: The team submitted a ___ application for the patient to receive services, ex: community-based support. -Family Meetings -Second opinion regarding patient's readiness for d/c and satisfactory mitigation of risk was completed by Dr. ___ ___ director)who supported d/c as there was no further benefit to be gained from a locked inpatient admission. Risk was mitigated to a reasonable degree and pt was d/c home with support of director, interdisciplinary staff (no staff member verbalized opposition of discharge). INFORMED CONSENT: -Haldol, Zyprexa, Depakote, Klonopin Team discussed the indications for, intended benefits of, and possible side effects and risks of these medications, and risks and benefits of possible alternatives, including not taking the medications, with this patient. We discussed the patient's right to decide whether to take this medication as well as the importance of the patient's actively participating in the treatment and discussing any questions about medications with the treatment team, and answered the patient's questions. The patient appeared able to understand and consented to begin the medication. RISK ASSESSMENT: #) Chronic/Static Risk Factors: - Chronic mental illness, co-morbid chronic medical illnesses, hx of suicide attempts (by OD and hanging, all within the past ___ years), age, gender, chronic pain, brain injury. #) Modifiable Risk Factors: - Medication non-compliance: pt tolerated this med regimen well and agreed to c/w meds. His depression was adequately treated and he felt well at time of d/c. - lack of connection to outpatient mental health treaters: aftercare planning - many referrals were made including to psychiatric care and a psychiatric partial hospital and ___ services, among multiple other things - Opioid dependence: pain consult was appreciated during admission and they will f/u in clinic w/ patient. Appt was made for f/u with pain service and also Dr. ___ - ___ brain injury - pt was referred to ongoing services within ___ and brain injury center. (he indicated he might have trouble attending appointments due to distance from home and the team offered to arrange a PT1 - pt and son refused stating that they would make something work within the family. All were advised that pt should not drive until cleared by PCP and verbalized understanding) - loss of long term relationship (end of a decades long relationship): ___ was involved in pt's care and was supportive - aftercare - we recommended a ___ and pt agreed - ___ ___ agreed to f/u with patient upon d/c for daily assessments regarding medication management and safety. #) Protective Factors: - Somewhat help-seeking and future oriented for serving as father figure for son and grandchildren - Lack of suicidal thoughts/intent/plan at time of d/c and during weeks preceding d/c along w/ participation in d/c planning, f/u care and future orientation - ___ faith PROGNOSIS: Poor - ___ is a ___ male with a history of severe major depressive disorder, with at least 3 previous suicide attempts, also with chronic back and leg pain, CAD s/p 5-vessel bypass and 2 stents, ulcerative colitis. He has now suffered an anoxic brain injury following last hanging attempt, and he is very impulsive. If patient f/u as directed, he will be able to improve his prognosis over time. "}
{"hadm_id": "12413577-DS-4", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> OBSTETRICS/GYNECOLOGY <ALLERGIES> Prozac / Zoloft / Paxil / Ciprofloxacin / Metronidazole <ATTENDING> ___ <CHIEF COMPLAINT> uterine prolapse, rectocele <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> Hysteropexy (using Elevate system from AMS), posterior colporrhaphy, insertion of biologic graft in the posterior compartment <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> This patient is a ___, gravida 2, para 1, who was referred to me by Dr. ___ vaginal prolapse. The patient was complaining of being able to palpate her cervix at the entrance of the vagina. She also reported some prolapse in the vagina that she \"thinks is a pouch\" and having difficulty reducing it. She was examined and found to have a third-degree uterine prolapse and a second-degree rectocele. The patient was fitted initially for a pessary which she had trouble keeping in and eventually elected to proceed with surgical management, given that for the short time that the pessary was in she had relief of her symptoms. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> PAST MEDICAL HISTORY: 1. Ulcerative colitis 2. Anxiety/Depression PAST SURGICAL HISTORY 1. Ileoanal pouch procedure PAST OB HISTORY G2P1011 Vaginal: 1 <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Her family history is unremarkable for Ovarian or Colon cancer. PGM and MGM with Breast Cancer <PHYSICAL EXAM> Upon discharge: AF VSS NAD RRR CTAB Abd soft, NT, ND, no r/g GU minimal vaginal bleeding Ext no TTP, no edema <PERTINENT RESULTS> ___ 08: 23PM WBC-14.7*# RBC-4.38 HGB-13.1 HCT-40.6 MCV-93 MCH-30.0 MCHC-32.4 RDW-13.5 ___ 08: 23PM ___ PTT-30.7 ___ <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Venlafaxine XR 150 mg PO DAILY 2. Clonazepam 1 mg PO Frequency is Unknown 3. Aripiprazole 10 mg PO DAILY 4. Amoxicillin-Clavulanic Acid Dose is Unknown PO Q12H <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. Aripiprazole 10 mg PO DAILY 2. Clonazepam 1 mg PO TID 3. Venlafaxine XR 150 mg PO DAILY 4. Oxycodone-Acetaminophen (5mg-325mg) ___ TAB PO Q4H: PRN pain RX *oxycodone-acetaminophen 5 mg-325 mg ___ tablet(s) by mouth every four (4) hours Disp #*40 Tablet Refills: *0 5. Amoxicillin-Clavulanic Acid ___ mg PO Q12H 6. Ibuprofen 600 mg PO Q6H: PRN pain RX *ibuprofen 600 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth every six (6) hours Disp #*20 Tablet Refills: *0 7. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID RX *docusate sodium [Colace] 100 mg 1 capsule(s) by mouth twice a day Disp #*60 Capsule Refills: *0 <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Uterine prolapse, rectocele, posterior enterocele, weakened rectovaginal fascia <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Dear Ms. ___, You are being discharged home after undergoing the procedures listed below. Please follow these instructions: General instructions: * Take your medications as prescribed. * Do not drive while taking narcotics. * Take a stool softener such as colace while taking narcotics to prevent constipation * Do not combine narcotic and sedative medications or alcohol * Do not take more than 4000mg acetaminophen (APAP) in 24 hrs * No strenuous activity until your post-op appointment * Nothing in the vagina (no tampons, no douching, no sex), no heavy lifting of objects >10lbs for 6 weeks. * You may eat a regular diet Incision care: * You may shower and allow soapy water to run over incision; no scrubbing of incision. No bath tubs for 6 weeks. Call your doctor for: * fever > 100.4 * severe abdominal pain * difficulty urinating * vaginal bleeding requiring >1 pad/hr * abnormal vaginal discharge * redness or drainage from incision * nausea/vomiting where you are unable to keep down fluids/food or your medication To reach medical records to get the records from this hospitalization sent to your doctor at home, call ___. ", "bhc": "On ___, Ms. ___ was admitted to the gynecology service after undergoing Hysteropexy (using Elevate system from AMS), posterior colporrhaphy, insertion of biologic graft in the posterior compartment. Please see the operative report for full details. Her post-operative course was uncomplicated. Immediately post-op, her pain was controlled with Iv dilaudid and toradol. On post-operative day 1, her urine output was adequate so her foley was removed and a formal voiding trial was performed. She passed her voiding trial with the following values: 300cc instilled, 250cc voided, 50cc straight catheterized; 300cc instilled, 250cc voided, 80cc bladder scanned. Her diet was advanced without difficulty and she was transitioned to percocet and motrin. By post-operative day 1, she was tolerating a regular diet, voiding spontaneously, ambulating independently, and pain was controlled with oral medications. She was then discharged home in stable condition with outpatient follow-up scheduled. "}
{"hadm_id": "18094090-DS-35", "note": "<SEX> F <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> Penicillins / Aspirin / Motrin / Tylenol / clindamycin / doxycycline / erythromycin base / Sulfa (Sulfonamide Antibiotics) / azithromycin / NSAIDS (Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drug) / hydromorphone / nicotine <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> shortness of breath, hip pain s/p fall, headache <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> None <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> Patient is a ___ year old woman with history of paradoxical vocal cord dysfunction, asthma/COPD (reportedly intubated 14 times but no verified), severe HTN, cocaine abuse, DVT/PE (on xarelto), question of somatization disorder, who presents to the ED c/o dyspnea x 1 week. She states that she has had worsening shortness of breath and wheezing for the past week and is now taking Duonebs 4x/day and Albuterol nebs 12x/day with minor relief. States that she had her most recent exacerbation 6 months ago. Associated sx are chest tightness and lightheadedness. Reports dry cough and sxs of chest congestion which she usually gets during change of sesaons. Otherwise denies fever, chills. Per chart review patient has longstanding history of asthma but also had questionable historical components including history of 14 intubations that could not be verified. Paradoxical vocal cord movement (PVCM) was deemed to be a realistic possibility given the patient's history of psychiatric disorders and refractory asthma and has been suggested in the past. Also reports she has had increased swelling in left leg, for which she presented to ___ last week. Per her report she had a ___ which showed DVT (not accurate) ROS also positive for headache. Patient states that 5 days ago, she also developed L retro-orbital headache radiating to the L upper cheek w photophobia consistent w previous headaches that she gets when bp is elevated. States that today she woke up with a headache and checked her bp, which was in the 200s. Reports her BPs are usually quite high (SBP 170-200). Also states that today she was walking up her steps when she fell. Per witness report, she \"lost consciousness\" for seconds. Denies head and neck injury. States that she developed L groin pain that radiates to her mid-thigh s/p fall, aggravated by movement. States that she has not taken any meds for pain because she is allergic to many ___ pain meds. Denies chest pain, palpitations, lightheadedness prior to the episode. In the ED, initial VS were: 98.1 ___ 24 ED labs were normal Imaging showed normal hip films. CXR with no infiltrates. Patient was given: duonebs, solumedrol, azithro REVIEW OF SYSTEMS: A ten point ROS was conducted and was negative except as above in the HPI. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> - Question of somatization disorder vs factitious disorder: Patient has history of reporting symptoms or health problems which were not confirmed (see psych note ___ - Psychotic Disorder NOS: patient reports she has \"bipolar schizophrenia\" but per previous psych notes no psychosis outside of active substance abuse, followed by Mass Mental Health - History of self injury - Asthma: reportedly intubated 14 times (not verified) - Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus since age ___ - Hypertension - Diverticulosis - Obstructive Sleep Apnea, on CPAP . The following diagnosis are ones that she reports, but have not been medically corroborated: - Seizure disorder - Psychotic disorder NOS - DVT - PE - CVA <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Father: ___, diabetes, psychiatric disorders (died at age ___ of MI) Mother: HTN, asthma, seizure disorders Per OMR patient's sister died of intracerebral aneurysmal bleed <PHYSICAL EXAM> Gen: NAD, morbidly obese, sitting up comfortably, not using accessory muscles Eyes: EOMI, sclerae anicteric ENT: MMM, OP clear Cardiovasc: JVD difficult to assess, RRR, no MRG, full pulses Resp: normal effort, no accessory muscle use, prolonged expiratory phase but no wheezing or crackles or rhonchii. GI: obese, soft, NT, ND, BS+ MSK: No significant kyphosis. No palpable synovitis. LLE calf tender to palpation Skin: No visible rash. No jaundice. Neuro: AAOx3. No facial droop. Psych: Anxious, tangential speech Exam on discharge: itals: 98.4 117/79 94 16 96 RA Gen: NAD, sitting in chair in NAD, morbidly obese Eyes: EOMI, sclerae anicteric ENT: MMM, OP clear ___: RRR, no MRG, full pulses, no edema Resp: normal effort, no accessory muscle use, lungs CTA ___. No wheezing. GI: soft, NT, ND, BS+ Skin: No visible rash. No jaundice. Neuro: AAOx3. No facial droop. Psych: Flat affect <PERTINENT RESULTS> ___ 04: 45PM URINE UCG-NEGATIVE ___ 04: 45PM URINE UCG-NEGATIVE ___ 04: 45PM URINE bnzodzpn-NEG barbitrt-NEG opiates-NEG cocaine-NEG amphetmn-NEG oxycodn-NEG mthdone-NEG ___ 04: 45PM URINE BLOOD-NEG NITRITE-NEG PROTEIN-NEG GLUCOSE-NEG KETONE-NEG BILIRUBIN-NEG UROBILNGN-2* PH-6.5 LEUK-MOD ___ 04: 45PM URINE RBC-1 WBC-3 BACTERIA-NONE YEAST-NONE EPI-4 TRANS EPI-<1 ___ 03: 18PM LACTATE-1.1 ___ 02: 55PM GLUCOSE-146* UREA N-10 CREAT-0.7 SODIUM-138 POTASSIUM-3.9 CHLORIDE-99 TOTAL CO2-29 ANION GAP-14 ___: 55PM ALT(SGPT)-16 AST(SGOT)-13 ALK PHOS-78 TOT BILI-0.2 ___ 02: 55PM ALBUMIN-3.9 CALCIUM-8.7 PHOSPHATE-2.6* MAGNESIUM-1.8 ___ 02: 55PM WBC-7.6 RBC-4.54 HGB-12.5 HCT-39.4 MCV-87 MCH-27.5 MCHC-31.7* RDW-14.9 RDWSD-47.6* ___ 02: 55PM NEUTS-50.6 ___ MONOS-7.4 EOS-0.9* BASOS-0.1 NUC RBCS-0.3* IM ___ AbsNeut-3.83 AbsLymp-3.08 AbsMono-0.56 AbsEos-0.07 AbsBaso-0.01 CTA: The exam is limited due to technical factors, such as patient body habitus. No evidence of central pulmonary embolism, and evaluation of the pulmonary arteries to the segmental and subsegmental level is limited. Bibasilar atelectasis; otherwise, no acute cardiopulmonary process. ___ (___- reviewed via partners gateway) Negative for DVT <MEDICATIONS ON ADMISSION> The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Fluticasone Propionate NASAL 2 SPRY NU DAILY 2. Fluticasone Propionate 110mcg 2 PUFF IH BID 3. Fluticasone-Salmeterol Diskus (500/50) 1 INH IH BID 4. Ipratropium-Albuterol Neb 1 NEB NEB Q6H 5. Lisinopril 40 mg PO DAILY 6. Montelukast 10 mg PO DAILY 7. Omeprazole 40 mg PO DAILY 8. Phenytoin Sodium Extended 300 mg PO DAILY 9. QUEtiapine Fumarate 100 mg PO DAILY 10. ZIPRASidone Hydrochloride 80 mg PO BID 11. FLUoxetine 60 mg PO DAILY 12. NPH 26 Units Breakfast NPH 26 Units Bedtime 13. Senna 8.6 mg PO BID: PRN constipation <DISCHARGE MEDICATIONS> 1. NPH 35 Units Breakfast NPH 35 Units Bedtime Regular 10 Units Breakfast Regular 10 Units Bedtime 2. Albuterol 0.083% Neb Soln 1 NEB IH Q6H: PRN sob 3. Fluticasone Propionate 110mcg 2 PUFF IH BID 4. Fluticasone Propionate NASAL 2 SPRY NU DAILY 5. Fluticasone-Salmeterol Diskus (500/50) 1 INH IH BID 6. Gabapentin 300 mg PO TID 7. Ipratropium-Albuterol Neb 1 NEB NEB Q6H 8. Lisinopril 40 mg PO DAILY 9. Montelukast 10 mg PO DAILY 10. Omeprazole 40 mg PO DAILY 11. QUEtiapine Fumarate 300. mg PO QHS 12. QUEtiapine Fumarate 50 mg PO DAILY 13. Senna 8.6 mg PO BID: PRN constipation <DISCHARGE DISPOSITION> Home <DISCHARGE DIAGNOSIS> Asthma/Dyspnea <DISCHARGE CONDITION> Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. <FOLLOWUP INSTRUCTIONS> ___ <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Ms. ___, You were admitted with shortness of breath, likely due to asthma. You had a CT scan of your chest which did not show a blood clot or other signs of infection. You were treated with nebulizers and your symptoms improved. It is important that you take your medications every day. Please follow up with your PCP. When you see your PCP, please make sure to bring all of your medications to make sure we have an updated list in our system. We wish you the best, Your ___ care team ", "bhc": "Patient is a ___ year old woman with history of paradoxical vocal cord dysfunction, asthma/COPD (reportedly intubated 14 times but no verified), severe HTN, cocaine abuse, question of somatization disorder, who presents to the ED c/o dyspnea x 1 week. # Dyspnea Initially presented with shortness of breath and wheezing, however appeared comfortable with good air movement, normal O2 sats, decent peak flow (~450), and no signs of bronchospasm on exam. DDx included PE given reported hx of DVT/PE and LLE swelling, which was ruled out with CTPA (pursued given Wells score 6 - tachycardia, signs of DVT, ?prior hx of DVT). The patient was managed on her home inhalers and given nebulizers with some improvement in her symptoms. She as saturation well on room air and had no wheezing on exam on discharge. # LLE swelling: Reported hx of DVT and that she is on xarelto. ___ at ___ negative and CTA on admission negative for PE. The patient was not continued on anticoagulation (per discussion with her pharmacy- not picked up since ___ # HTN: Normotensive on arrival, now improved. BP was well controlled on Lisinopril 40mg daily, # Fall # Left hip pain: Films negative for fractures The patient's pain was controlled with Tylenol. Per admitting provider; PMP notes 5 providers giving 4 scripts for oxy and benxos over the past year. As such, opiates were avoided. The patient's gapabentin was continued. # Diabetes: A1C 7.7%, insulin dependent, morbidly obese. No signs of microvascular complcations. - Continue NPH 35 units in the morning and 30 units in the evening with supper with regular insulin. Maintained on NPH with sliding scale while hospitalized. # Obstructive sleep apnea. She says that she does not currently have access to a CPAP machine. She reports that she has had difficulty making appointments with Sleep Medicine. Should follow up as outpatient #Psychiatric illness Unclear if has current treaters. No signs of psychosis during hospitalization. The patient was continued on Seroquel 50mg in AM and 300mg in HS which was the last confirmed prescription. Transitional issues: - Patient with difficult to complete medication reconciliation, advised to bring all mediation bottles to next PCP appointment, unclear if medication reconciliation on admission is accurate "}
{"hadm_id": "16791831-DS-27", "note": "<SEX> M <SERVICE> MEDICINE <ALLERGIES> morphine <ATTENDING> ___. <CHIEF COMPLAINT> Nausea and vomiting <MAJOR SURGICAL OR INVASIVE PROCEDURE> none <HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS> The patient is a ___ yr old M with a PMH significant for chronic pancreatitis ___ pancreas divisum and CFTR mutations who is now 4 weeks s/p total pancreatectomy (complete pancreatectomy, total splenectomy, partial duodenectomy, partial gastrectomy, and auto-islet cell re-implantation) c/b PICC-line associated GNR bacteremia who since his most recent discharge from ___ has been experiencing increasing N/V and decreased PO intake. Per the patient and OSH records, the patient had been receiving care for his pancreatitis since ___. He has suffered from ___ episodes of pancreatitis over the past few years and has previously required TPN for nutrition. He received a total pancreatectomy with auto-islet cell transplantation in ___ at ___. His course was complicated by post-operative intraabdominal abscesses and is now s/p abscess drainage x2, and while on IV antibiotics developed GNR bactermia. The PICC line used to administer the antibiotics were removed and he was transitioned to oral antibiotics. He was discharged home and for the past 3 days ___ was the date of discharge) he has experienced increasing N/V and decreased PO intake. The patient denies bloody or projectile emesis, worsening abdominal pain, diarrhea, constipation, bloody or melanous stools. His last bowel movement was yesterday. He has not had one today. He denies fevers, chills, rigors, night sweats, or any sick contacts since being home from ___. The patient's nausea peaks in the evening and at its worse is a ___ out of 10. With 8mg of Zofran, he reports his nausea decreasing to a ___. He reports that the nausea isn't associated with eating nor has he noticed it worsen after taking his PO medications. In the ED, initial vital signs were: T 97.2, HR 82 BP 150/80, HR 15, 99% RA. Exam notable for ex-lap incision c/d/i, LLQ and RLQ pain but no rebound or guarding, JP drains from RUQ with serosanginous drainage and muddy brown drainage. Labs were notable for: WBC 10.3, Hg 10.8, platelets 1028, Na 138, K 4.7, Cl 95, Bicarb 32, BUN 10, Cr 1.1. No imaging obtained. Patient was given 1000cc NS and promethazine 6.25 mg. Vitals prior to transfer were: Temp 99.1, HR 77, BP 135/92, RR 18, 100% RA. Upon arrival to the floor, patient reported mild nausea. No abdominal pain. REVIEW OF SYSTEMS: [+] per HPI [-] Denies headache, cough, fevers, chills, sweats, weight loss, dyspnea, chest pain, abdominal pain, diarrhea, constipation, dysuria, or rash. <PAST MEDICAL HISTORY> hypertension anxiety pancreatitis ___ pancreas divisum and CFTR mutation Past Surgical History: appendectomy cholecystectomy sphincterotomy and ERCP. Pt has a partially torn bicepstendon for which he is awaiting surgery. <SOCIAL HISTORY> ___ <FAMILY HISTORY> Mother s/p ccy. <PHYSICAL EXAM> Admission Physical Exam -== VITALS: 98.1 140/86 72 18 99RA GENERAL: Alert, oriented, no acute distress HEENT: Sclerae anicteric, oropharynx clear NECK: Supple, JVP not elevated, no LAD RESP: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, ronchi CV: Regular rate and rhythm, normal S1 + S2, no murmurs, rubs, gallops ABD: well-healing vertical midline scar, +BS, soft, nondistended, tender to palpation over midabdomial scar. two drains in place, one with serosanguis output, the second with serous output GU: no foley EXT: warm, well perfused, 2+ pulses, no edema NEURO: CNs2-12 intact, motor function grossly normal Discharge Physical Exam -== Vitals: 99.4 97.5 122/78 74 18 98% RA I/O: ___ Weight: 78.3 kg (78.3 kg ___ Exam: GENERAL - Alert, interactive, well-appearing in NAD HEENT - EOMI, sclerae anicteric, MMM, OP clear HEART - RRR, nl S1-S2, no MRG LUNGS - CTAB ABD: well-healing vertical midline scar, +BS, soft, nondistended, tender to palpation over midabdomial scar. two drains in place, one with serosanguinous output, the second with serous output EXTREMITIES - WWP, no edema NEURO - awake, A&Ox3 <PERTINENT RESULTS> Admission Lab Results -= ___ 04: 27PM BLOOD WBC-10.3*# RBC-4.03* Hgb-10.8*# Hct-34.6* MCV-86 MCH-26.8 MCHC-31.2* RDW-16.9* RDWSD-52.5* Plt ___ ___ 04: 27PM BLOOD Neuts-66.8 Lymphs-13.2* Monos-11.7 Eos-6.0 Baso-1.7* Im ___ AbsNeut-6.87*# AbsLymp-1.36 AbsMono-1.21* AbsEos-0.62* AbsBaso-0.18* ___ 04: 27PM BLOOD Plt Smr-VERY HIGH Plt ___ ___ 04: 27PM BLOOD Glucose-271* UreaN-10 Creat-1.1 Na-138 K-4.7 Cl-95* HCO3-32 AnGap-16 ___ 08: 55AM BLOOD Albumin-3.2* Calcium-9.1 Phos-3.6 Mg-1.8 ___ 08: 55AM BLOOD ALT-11 AST-22 LD(LDH)-184 AlkPhos-192* TotBili-0.4 Discharge Lab Results - ___ 10: 39AM BLOOD WBC-10.4* RBC-4.00* Hgb-10.6* Hct-34.3* MCV-86 MCH-26.5 MCHC-30.9* RDW-16.7* RDWSD-52.4* Plt ___ ___ 04: 42AM BLOOD Glucose-136* UreaN-8 Creat-1.0 Na-140 K-4.6 Cl-98 HCO3-32 AnGap-15 ___ 04: 42AM BLOOD Calcium-9.1 Phos-4.4 Mg-2.0 MICRO: ========== Blood culture ___: NGTD at time of discharge <DISCHARGE INSTRUCTIONS> Dear Mr. ___, You were admitted to the ___ for evaluation of nausea and vomiting. You had a benign abdominal exam without fevers, chills or recent sick contacts making bowel obstruction or infection unlikely. The symptoms started after transitioning to oral antibiotics while in the hospital last week and this was felt to be the most likely cause of your symptoms. After speaking with your surgeon, Dr. ___, the decision was made to stop your flagyl (a common cause of stomach upset) and fluconazole. Your symptoms improved significantly with antiemetics (medications for nausea) and transition to ciprofloxacin only. At discharge, you were tolerating a normal, diabetic diet and able to take in adequate calories. Because of this, you were not felt to need nutritional support with tube feeds or TPN at this time. It is very important that you continue to consume enough calories to maintain your weight and ultimately start to gain back the weight you have lost over the last several months. While you were here, you noted that your pain post-surgery was significantly improved and your oxycontin was decreased from 40mg BID to 30mg BID. We also have provided you with stool softeners to prevent constipation. Please continue to take ciprofloxacin twice daily until you follow-up with Dr. ___ at ___ for drain removal on ___. It was a pleasure taking care of you! Your ___ Care Team ", "bhc": "Mr. ___ is a ___ gentleman who is 4 weeks s/p total pancreatectomy (complete pancreatectomy, total splenectomy, partial duodenectomy, partial gastrectomy, islet cell re-implantation) complicated by bacteremia (enterobacter) and two abdominal abscesses 1.5 weeks ago who presented with several days of nausea/vomiting on oral antibiotics. # Nausea/vomiting: The patient was evaluated for common etiologies of nausea/vomiting. Obstruction was not likely given his relatively benign exam and history recent bowel movements. Recurrence of his intrabdominal infection was less unlikely given no recent fevers or changes in output from his drains and improved WBC count. Gastroenteritis also unlikely, given no recent sick contacts and improvement with IV abx. His symptoms are most likely related to recent changes to PO medication list, notably a switch from IV to PO ciprofloxacin, flagyl, and fluconazole. A review of OSH records and discussion with the attending surgeon (Dr. ___ at ___ clarified antibiotic requirement. The patient had documented ciprofloxacin-sensitive E. aerogenes (GNR) bacteremia secondary to a PICC line, however patient has been on fluconazole and flagyl for 2 weeks without a clear target. The outpatient surgery team and inpatient medicine team agreed the fluconazole and flagyl could be discontinued given lack of culture data or clinical suspicion supporting their use beyond two weeks. The patient was switched to PO ciprofloxacin only, and allowed to progress his diet as tolerated. The patient did not feel nausea with the PO ciprofloxacin and progressed from a clear liquid, full liquid, to a regular diet by the time of discharge. He also gained weight during his recovery. PICC line placement was discussed with the patient, but given his tolerating PO ciprofloxacin, his progressing diet, and his gaining weight, this was not pursued, especially given his recent PICC-line associated bacteremia and splenectomy making TPN high risk. The patient was given a bowel regimen and was stooling comfortably before discharge. # Failure to thrive: The patient reports poor PO intake since before his pancreatectomy. Given improving PO intake and recent bacteremia, it was decided not to start TPN during this admission. The patient was seen by nutrition and caloric intake and his daily weights were monitored. By the time of discharge, his weight was stable on regular diabetic diet. # Total pancreatectomy: The patient's two JP drains were left in place. The patient was continued on his prescribed insulin replacement therapy. The patient was given his oxycontin dose for pain. The patient's pain was well-controlled and by the end of his hospitalization, his oxycontin dose was decreased to 30mg BID. The patient was also continued on his PPI. # Thrombocytosis: The patient was admitted with platelets over 1000. This is most likely reactive in the setting of recent surgery and due to splenectomy. His PLT count was downtrending through his stay and he was continued on his ASA 325mg. # Anxiety: The patient was treated with his home dosing of Escitalopram. Transitional Issues ================== [] Please ensure follow up with his surgeon on ___ (scheduled) for evaluation and possible drain removal [] the patient's fluconazole and flagyl were stopped [] Docusate, senna and miralax added to medication list to prevent constipation while on high dose narcotic pain regimen [] Discharged on zofran and ativan for nausea [] the patient was continued on PO ciprofloxacin with the intent of continuing this treatment until his drains are removed on ___ [] the patient's oxycontin dose was decreased to 30mg BID and he was encouraged to continue weaning off his pain meds as tolerated # Code Status: Full Code # CONTACT: ___ (husband, HCP) ___ ___ on Admission: The Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete. 1. Aspirin 325 mg PO DAILY 2. Fluconazole 400 mg PO Q24H 3. Glargine 8 Units Breakfast Insulin SC Sliding Scale using HUM Insulin 4. Magnesium Oxide 400 mg PO DAILY 5. MetRONIDAZOLE (FLagyl) 500 mg PO TID 6. OxyCODONE SR (OxyconTIN) 40 mg PO Q12H 7. OxycoDONE (Immediate Release) ___ mg PO Q4H:PRN pain 8. Ciprofloxacin HCl 500 mg PO Q12H 9. Vitamin D ___ UNIT PO 1X/WEEK (FR) 10. Escitalopram Oxalate 20 mg PO DAILY 11. Creon 12 2 CAP PO TID W/MEALS 12. Ondansetron 4 mg PO Q8H:PRN nausea 13. Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q24H 14. testosterone enanthate 100 mg injection 1X/WEEK Discharge Medications: 1. OxyCODONE SR (OxyconTIN) 30 mg PO Q12H RX *oxycodone [OxyContin] 10 mg 3 tablet by mouth twice a day Disp #*42 Tablet Refills:*0 2. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID RX *docusate sodium 100 mg 1 tablet by mouth twice a day Disp #*30 Tablet Refills:*0 3. Lorazepam 0.5 mg PO Q6H:PRN nausea 4. Polyethylene Glycol 17 g PO DAILY RX *polyethylene glycol 3350 [Miralax] 17 gram 1 packet by mouth Daily Disp #*24 Packet Refills:*0 5. Senna 8.6 mg PO BID RX *sennosides [senna] 8.6 mg 1 capsule by mouth twice a day Disp #*30 Capsule Refills:*0 6. testosterone enanthate 100 mg injection 1X/WEEK 7. Vitamin D ___ UNIT PO 1X/WEEK (FR) 8. Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q24H 9. OxycoDONE (Immediate Release) ___ mg PO Q4H:PRN pain 10. Ondansetron 4 mg PO Q8H:PRN nausea RX *ondansetron 4 mg 1 tablet by mouth every eight (8) hours Disp #*10 Tablet Refills:*0 11. Escitalopram Oxalate 20 mg PO DAILY 12. Creon 12 2 CAP PO TID W/MEALS 13. Ciprofloxacin HCl 500 mg PO Q12H 14. Aspirin 325 mg PO DAILY 15. Glargine 8 Units Breakfast Insulin SC Sliding Scale using HUM Insulin Discharge Disposition: Home With Service Facility: ___ ___: Nausea and vomiting Weight Loss Thrombocytosis Discharge Condition: Mental Status: Clear and coherent. Level of Consciousness: Alert and interactive. Activity Status: Ambulatory - Independent. Followup Instructions: ___"}
